/* Mini-Test Bank (window.MINITEST_BANK) - 10 tests x 40 questions (Bio/GChem/OChem/QR),
   difficulty tiers 1-10, authored + adversarially verified. Do not hand-edit; regenerate via assemble-minitests.mjs. */
window.MINITEST_BANK = {"version":"v1","tests":[{"n":1,"tier":1,"questions":[{"section":"bio","topic":"Biomolecules","stem":"Which of the following is the monomer (building block) from which proteins are constructed?","choices":["Nucleotide","Monosaccharide","Amino acid","Fatty acid","Glycerol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Proteins are polymers of amino acids joined by peptide bonds, so the amino acid is the correct monomer. Each distractor is deliberately the monomer of a DIFFERENT biomolecule class: nucleotides build nucleic acids (DNA/RNA), monosaccharides build carbohydrates, and fatty acids plus glycerol combine to form lipids (triglycerides). A student who confuses which subunit goes with which macromolecule would be tempted by these."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cell structure & organelles","stem":"Which organelle is the primary site of ATP production through aerobic (oxygen-using) respiration in eukaryotic cells?","choices":["Ribosome","Mitochondrion","Nucleus","Golgi apparatus","Lysosome"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The mitochondrion is the 'powerhouse' where the Krebs cycle and electron transport chain generate the bulk of a cell's ATP aerobically. The distractors each name a real organelle with a different job: ribosomes synthesize proteins, the nucleus stores DNA, the Golgi apparatus modifies and packages proteins, and the lysosome performs intracellular digestion. Only the mitochondrion is tied to aerobic ATP production."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Membrane transport","stem":"Osmosis specifically refers to the movement of which substance across a semipermeable membrane, from a region of lower solute concentration to a region of higher solute concentration?","choices":["Glucose","Sodium ions","Oxygen gas","Water","Protein"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Osmosis is defined as the diffusion of WATER across a selectively permeable membrane, moving toward the side with higher solute concentration (lower water concentration). Movement of solutes such as glucose, sodium ions, or oxygen down their gradients is simple or facilitated diffusion, not osmosis; large proteins cannot cross a typical membrane freely at all. The trap is treating osmosis as the movement of the solute rather than of water."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cellular respiration","stem":"In aerobic cellular respiration, which molecule serves as the final electron acceptor at the end of the electron transport chain?","choices":["Carbon dioxide (CO2)","Glucose","NAD+","Water","Oxygen (O2)"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Oxygen is the terminal electron acceptor: it accepts electrons at the end of the electron transport chain and is reduced to water. CO2 is a waste product released during the Krebs cycle, glucose is the starting fuel (electron donor), and NAD+ is an intermediate electron carrier rather than the final acceptor. Water is the PRODUCT formed when oxygen accepts electrons, so choosing it confuses the acceptor with the result."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Photosynthesis","stem":"In plant cells, photosynthesis takes place primarily within which organelle?","choices":["Mitochondrion","Nucleus","Chloroplast","Central vacuole","Ribosome"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The chloroplast contains chlorophyll and the thylakoid/stroma machinery that carry out the light reactions and the Calvin cycle, making it the site of photosynthesis. The mitochondrion is the tempting opposite (it performs respiration, not photosynthesis). The nucleus stores DNA, the central vacuole stores water and maintains turgor, and ribosomes make proteins; none capture light energy to build sugar."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Central dogma (DNA->RNA->protein)","stem":"In the central dogma of molecular biology, the synthesis of an RNA molecule using a DNA template is called:","choices":["Translation","Transcription","Replication","Reverse transcription","Splicing"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Transcription is DNA -> RNA, exactly the step described. Translation is the next step, RNA -> protein at the ribosome, and is the most common mix-up. Replication is DNA -> DNA (copying the genome). Reverse transcription runs backward, RNA -> DNA. Splicing is a processing step that removes introns from pre-mRNA, not the synthesis of RNA from a DNA template."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mitosis & meiosis","stem":"A single diploid cell undergoing one complete round of meiosis produces how many daughter cells?","choices":["Two","One","Eight","Four","Sixteen"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Meiosis consists of two consecutive divisions (meiosis I and meiosis II) and yields four haploid daughter cells from one diploid parent. The tempting distractor is two, which is the number of cells produced by a single mitotic division; a student who forgets that meiosis has two divisions picks it. Eight and sixteen would require additional rounds of division that do not occur."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mendelian genetics","stem":"An organism that carries two identical alleles for a given gene (for example, AA or aa) is best described as:","choices":["Homozygous","Heterozygous","Hemizygous","Dihybrid","Polyploid"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Homozygous means the two alleles at a locus are identical (AA or aa). Heterozygous is the direct opposite (two different alleles, Aa) and is the main trap. Hemizygous refers to having only one allele of a gene, as with X-linked genes in males. Dihybrid describes an organism heterozygous at two different loci (AaBb). Polyploid means having more than two complete chromosome sets, which is about chromosome number, not allele identity."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Evolution & population genetics","stem":"The process by which individuals with heritable traits that improve survival and reproduction leave more offspring, gradually increasing those traits' frequency in a population, is called:","choices":["Genetic drift","Gene flow","Mutation","Nondisjunction","Natural selection"],"correct":4,"explanation":"This describes natural selection: non-random, fitness-based differential reproduction that shifts allele frequencies. Genetic drift is RANDOM change in allele frequency (strongest in small populations), so it lacks the fitness component. Gene flow is the movement of alleles between populations by migration. Mutation is the random source of new alleles but is not the selective process itself. Nondisjunction is a chromosome-separation error during cell division, unrelated to this definition."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Physiology (a body system)","stem":"Which chamber of the human heart contracts to pump oxygen-rich blood out to the body through the aorta?","choices":["Right ventricle","Right atrium","Left ventricle","Left atrium","Aorta"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The left ventricle is the thick, most muscular chamber that pumps oxygenated blood into the aorta for systemic circulation. The right ventricle pumps oxygen-poor blood to the lungs via the pulmonary artery. The left atrium receives oxygenated blood returning from the lungs, and the right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the body; neither pumps to the aorta. The aorta is the artery that carries the blood away, not a heart chamber, so it cannot be the answer."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Moles & stoichiometry","stem":"What is the approximate molar mass of water (H2O)? (Use H = 1 g/mol, O = 16 g/mol.)","choices":["2 g/mol","16 g/mol","17 g/mol","18 g/mol","34 g/mol"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Add the atomic masses: 2(1) + 16 = 18 g/mol. 2 g/mol counts only the two hydrogens (H2). 16 g/mol counts only the oxygen. 17 g/mol is the hydroxide fragment (OH). 34 g/mol is hydrogen peroxide (H2O2), the result of doubling the oxygen count."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Atomic structure & quantum numbers","stem":"According to the Pauli exclusion principle, what is the maximum number of electrons a single atomic orbital can hold?","choices":["1","2","6","8","10"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A single orbital holds at most 2 electrons, and they must have opposite spins. 1 ignores that two opposite spins can share an orbital. 6 is the capacity of a p subshell (three orbitals), 10 is the capacity of a d subshell (five orbitals), and 8 is the octet (a full valence shell of s and p), not a single orbital."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Periodic trends","stem":"Which of the following elements has the highest electronegativity?","choices":["Fluorine","Oxygen","Chlorine","Cesium","Helium"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Electronegativity increases toward the top-right of the periodic table (excluding the noble gases), making fluorine the most electronegative element. Oxygen is second highest but still below fluorine. Chlorine is below fluorine in the same group. Cesium sits at the bottom-left and is among the least electronegative. Helium is a noble gas that does not readily bond and is not assigned a standard (Pauling) electronegativity value."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Bonding, Lewis & VSEPR","stem":"A central atom is surrounded by four bonding pairs and zero lone pairs, as in methane (CH4). What is its molecular geometry?","choices":["Linear","Bent","Trigonal pyramidal","Tetrahedral","Square planar"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Four bonding pairs and no lone pairs give a tetrahedral geometry with 109.5-degree bond angles. Bent describes two bonds plus two lone pairs (as in H2O). Trigonal pyramidal describes three bonds plus one lone pair (as in NH3). Square planar describes four bonds plus two lone pairs. Linear applies to only two electron domains."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Gas laws","stem":"According to Boyle's law, if the temperature and amount of a gas are held constant and its volume is decreased, what happens to its pressure?","choices":["It increases","It decreases","It stays constant","It drops to zero","It first doubles, then halves"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Boyle's law states pressure and volume are inversely related at constant temperature (P is proportional to 1/V), so decreasing the volume increases the pressure. Choosing 'decreases' mistakes the relationship for a direct one. 'Stays constant' would only be true if pressure and volume were independent, and the other options describe changes that violate the inverse proportionality."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Thermochemistry","stem":"A chemical reaction that releases heat to its surroundings and has a negative enthalpy change (delta H) is best described as:","choices":["Endothermic","Exothermic","Endergonic","Adiabatic","Isothermal"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An exothermic reaction releases heat and has a negative delta H. Endothermic is the opposite (absorbs heat, positive delta H). Endergonic refers to a positive delta G (free energy), not heat release, and is a tempting mix-up of energy terms. Adiabatic means no heat is exchanged with the surroundings, and isothermal means the temperature is held constant."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Kinetics","stem":"Which statement correctly describes the role of a catalyst in a chemical reaction?","choices":["It is consumed during the reaction and must be replenished","It raises the activation energy of the reaction","It lowers the activation energy and is not consumed","It shifts the equilibrium position toward the products","It makes the overall reaction more exothermic"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A catalyst speeds up a reaction by providing a pathway with lower activation energy and is regenerated (not consumed). It does not get used up, so it need not be replenished. Raising activation energy would slow the reaction. A catalyst speeds the forward and reverse rates equally, so it does not shift the equilibrium position, and it does not change delta H (the reaction's enthalpy)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Equilibrium","stem":"A reaction is at equilibrium. According to Le Chatelier's principle, if more reactant is added, the equilibrium will shift:","choices":["Toward the products","Toward the reactants","No shift occurs","The reaction stops completely","Toward the side with fewer gas molecules"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Adding reactant creates a stress that the system relieves by consuming some of it, shifting the equilibrium toward the products (forward direction). Shifting toward the reactants is the opposite of relieving the stress. The system does respond, so 'no shift' and 'stops completely' are wrong. The 'fewer gas molecules' rule applies to changes in pressure or volume, not to adding a substance."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Acids, bases & pH","stem":"At 25 degrees Celsius, what is the pH of a neutral aqueous solution?","choices":["0","1","7","10","14"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A neutral solution at 25 degrees Celsius has a pH of 7, where the hydrogen ion and hydroxide ion concentrations are equal (both 1 x 10^-7 M). A pH of 0 or 1 is strongly acidic, while a pH of 10 is basic and a pH of 14 is strongly basic. Only pH 7 represents the balance point between acidic and basic."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Electrochemistry","stem":"In an electrochemical cell, oxidation always occurs at which component?","choices":["The cathode","The anode","The salt bridge","The electrolyte solution","The external wire"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Oxidation occurs at the anode (remember 'An Ox': Anode = Oxidation). The cathode is where reduction occurs ('Red Cat'). The salt bridge maintains charge balance by allowing ion flow, the electrolyte solution provides the ions, and the external wire carries electrons between electrodes, but none of these is where oxidation takes place."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IUPAC nomenclature","stem":"What is the IUPAC name of the straight-chain alkane with the condensed structural formula CH3CH2CH2CH3?","choices":["Propane","Butane","Pentane","Isobutane","2-Methylpropane"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The molecule has four carbons in one continuous, unbranched chain, so it is butane (the '-ane' suffix marks an alkane, and 'but-' means four carbons). Propane is three carbons and pentane is five, so both have the wrong chain length. Isobutane and 2-methylpropane are two names for the same branched C4 isomer; the formula shown is a straight chain with no branch, so those branched-isomer names do not apply."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Stereochemistry (R/S, chirality)","stem":"Which of the following correctly defines a chiral center (stereocenter) in an organic molecule?","choices":["A carbon atom bonded to four different atoms or groups","A carbon atom bonded to four identical groups","A carbon atom that is part of a carbon-carbon double bond","A carbon atom that carries at least one lone pair of electrons","Any carbon atom that is bonded to a halogen"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A chiral center is an sp3 carbon attached to four different substituents, which makes the molecule non-superimposable on its mirror image. Four identical groups gives a symmetric, achiral carbon. A double-bonded carbon is sp2 and cannot be an R/S stereocenter (it can create cis/trans, a different concept). A lone pair on carbon does not by itself create a chiral center, and simply bearing a halogen does not guarantee four different groups."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"SN1/SN2/E1/E2","stem":"In an SN2 reaction, the nucleophile approaches the electrophilic carbon in a way best described as:","choices":["Backside attack, opposite the leaving group, giving inversion of configuration","Frontside attack, on the same side as the leaving group, giving retention of configuration","Attack only after the leaving group first departs to form a carbocation","A two-step process proceeding through a planar carbocation intermediate","Attack at the carbon bearing the greatest number of alkyl groups"],"correct":0,"explanation":"SN2 is concerted (one step): the nucleophile attacks from the side directly opposite the leaving group, inverting the stereochemistry (Walden inversion). Frontside attack with retention does not describe SN2. The options describing a carbocation and two steps are the SN1 mechanism, not SN2. SN2 is also fastest at the least hindered carbon, so attack at the most substituted carbon is wrong."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alkene addition reactions","stem":"Catalytic hydrogenation of an alkene using H2 gas with a metal catalyst such as Pd or Pt produces which type of product?","choices":["An alkane","An alkyne","An alcohol","An alkyl halide","A carboxylic acid"],"correct":0,"explanation":"H2 adds across the C=C double bond, saturating it and giving an alkane. An alkyne is more unsaturated than an alkene, so that is the wrong direction. An alcohol requires adding water (hydration), an alkyl halide requires adding HX, and a carboxylic acid requires oxidation, none of which occurs with H2 and a metal catalyst."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Aromaticity & EAS","stem":"How many pi electrons are contained in a single molecule of benzene?","choices":["2","3","6","8","12"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Benzene has three carbon-carbon double bonds, and each pi bond contributes two pi electrons, giving 6 total. This satisfies Huckel's rule (4n + 2, with n = 1), which is why benzene is aromatic. The choice '3' confuses the number of double bonds with the number of electrons, and 8 and 12 overcount the delocalized system."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alcohols & carbonyls","stem":"Which functional group is characterized by a carbon atom double-bonded to an oxygen atom (C=O)?","choices":["Hydroxyl","Carbonyl","Ether","Amine","Alkoxide"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A carbon double-bonded to oxygen (C=O) is the carbonyl group, found in aldehydes and ketones. A hydroxyl is an -OH single bond (alcohols). An ether is C-O-C with single bonds. An amine contains nitrogen, not a C=O. An alkoxide is a deprotonated alcohol (R-O minus), again a single bond to oxygen, not a double bond."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Acidity & pKa","stem":"Which statement correctly relates a compound's pKa to its acid strength?","choices":["A lower pKa indicates a stronger acid","A higher pKa indicates a stronger acid","pKa is always numerically equal to the solution pH","pKa measures only basicity and says nothing about acidity","pKa is completely independent of acid strength"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Because pKa = -log(Ka), a lower pKa corresponds to a larger Ka, meaning the acid dissociates more and is stronger. The reversed statement (higher pKa = stronger) is a classic trap. pKa is a fixed property of the acid, whereas pH depends on concentration, so they are not always equal. pKa directly quantifies acid strength, so the claims that it is independent of acidity are false."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Resonance & stability","stem":"Resonance structures of a single molecule differ from one another only in the position of the:","choices":["Electrons","Atomic nuclei","Both atoms and electrons together","Sigma-bond framework","Hydrogen atoms"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Resonance structures show the same molecule with only pi electrons and/or lone pairs moved; the positions of all atomic nuclei stay fixed. If atoms (including hydrogens) actually moved, you would have different constitutional isomers or tautomers, not resonance forms. The sigma-bond framework is unchanged across resonance structures, so only the electrons are repositioned."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IR & H-NMR spectroscopy","stem":"A strong infrared (IR) absorption near 1700 cm^-1 is most characteristic of which functional group?","choices":["O-H of an alcohol","C=O carbonyl","C-H of an alkane","C(triple bond)C of an alkyne","N-H of an amine"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The carbonyl (C=O) stretch appears as a strong, sharp band near 1700 cm^-1, a signature DAT students are expected to recognize. An alcohol O-H is a broad band around 3200-3550 cm^-1, alkane C-H is near 2850-3000 cm^-1, an alkyne C(triple)C is around 2100-2260 cm^-1, and an amine N-H is near 3300 cm^-1, so none of those fall at 1700 cm^-1."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Synthesis / roadmap","stem":"Which reagent oxidizes a primary alcohol all the way to a carboxylic acid?","choices":["KMnO4 (a strong oxidizing agent)","PCC","NaBH4","LiAlH4","H2 / Pd"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A strong oxidant such as KMnO4 (or CrO3/Jones reagent) drives a primary alcohol past the aldehyde stage all the way to a carboxylic acid. PCC is the tempting trap: it is a mild oxidant that stops at the aldehyde, so it does not reach the acid. NaBH4 and LiAlH4 are reducing agents (wrong direction entirely), and H2/Pd performs reduction/hydrogenation, not oxidation."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Fractions & decimals","stem":"What is the fraction 3/4 written as a decimal?","choices":["0.75","0.34","0.43","1.33","0.375"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Dividing 3 by 4 gives 0.75, so A is correct. 0.34 (B) comes from just writing the digits 3 and 4 with a decimal point. 0.43 (C) reverses those digits. 1.33 (D) is 4 divided by 3 (the fraction flipped). 0.375 (E) is 3/8, dividing by the wrong denominator."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Algebra & equations","stem":"Solve for x: x + 7 = 12.","choices":["5","19","-5","7","84"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Subtracting 7 from both sides gives x = 12 - 7 = 5, so A is correct. 19 (B) comes from adding 7 instead of subtracting. -5 (C) is a sign error (7 - 12). 7 (D) mistakes the constant for the answer. 84 (E) comes from multiplying 7 by 12 instead of isolating x."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Ratios & proportions","stem":"If 2/4 = x/8, what is the value of x?","choices":["4","16","2","8","6"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Cross-multiplying gives 2 times 8 = 4 times x, so 16 = 4x and x = 4 (A). 16 (B) is 2 times 8 without dividing by 4. 2 (C) just copies the top-left numerator. 8 (D) copies the given denominator. 6 (E) comes from adding 2 + 4 instead of solving the proportion."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Percentages","stem":"What is 10% of 250?","choices":["25","2.5","2500","10","50"],"correct":0,"explanation":"10% means 0.10, and 0.10 times 250 = 25, so A is correct. 2.5 (B) is 1% of 250 (moving the decimal one place too far). 2500 (C) multiplies 250 by 10 instead of by 0.10. 10 (D) just restates the percentage. 50 (E) is 20% of 250, using the wrong percent."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Exponents & roots","stem":"What is the value of 2^3 (2 raised to the third power)?","choices":["8","6","9","16","5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"2^3 means 2 times 2 times 2 = 8, so A is correct. 6 (B) multiplies the base by the exponent (2 times 3). 9 (C) is 3^2, swapping base and exponent. 16 (D) is 2^4, using one too many factors. 5 (E) adds the base and exponent (2 + 3)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Geometry (area/volume)","stem":"A rectangle has a length of 5 units and a width of 3 units. What is its area?","choices":["15 square units","16 square units","8 square units","30 square units","4 square units"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Area of a rectangle is length times width, so 5 times 3 = 15 square units (A). 16 (B) is the perimeter, 2 times (5 + 3). 8 (C) simply adds the two sides. 30 (D) doubles the area, confusing it with a volume-style calculation. 4 (E) is an implausible value that does not result from any correct area, perimeter, or side operation."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Trigonometry","stem":"What is the value of sin 30 degrees?","choices":["1/2","(square root of 3)/2","(square root of 2)/2","1","square root of 3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"sin 30 degrees equals 1/2, so A is correct. (root 3)/2 (B) is cos 30 degrees, its complement. (root 2)/2 (C) is sin 45 degrees. 1 (D) is sin 90 degrees. (root 3) (E) is tan 60 degrees. These are the standard special-angle values, and only 1/2 matches sin 30."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Probability","stem":"A fair six-sided die is rolled once. What is the probability of rolling a 4?","choices":["1/6","1/4","4/6","1/2","1"],"correct":0,"explanation":"There is one face showing 4 out of six equally likely faces, so the probability is 1/6 (A). 1/4 (B) mistakenly uses the number 4 as a denominator. 4/6 (C) puts the target number in the numerator. 1/2 (D) treats it like a two-outcome (yes/no) event without weighting. 1 (E) would mean the outcome is certain, which it is not."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Statistics (mean/median/SD)","stem":"What is the mean (average) of the data set 2, 4, and 9?","choices":["5","4","15","9","3.5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The mean is the sum divided by the count: (2 + 4 + 9)/3 = 15/3 = 5, so A is correct. 4 (B) is the median (the middle value), not the mean. 15 (C) is the sum without dividing. 9 (D) is the maximum value. 3.5 (E) is the average of only 2 and 4, ignoring the third value."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Word problems (rate/work/mixture)","stem":"A car travels 60 miles in 2 hours at a constant speed. What is its speed in miles per hour?","choices":["30","120","62","58","15"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Speed equals distance divided by time: 60 miles / 2 hours = 30 miles per hour, so A is correct. 120 (B) multiplies distance by time instead of dividing. 62 (C) adds 60 and 2. 58 (D) subtracts 2 from 60. 15 (E) divides by 4 instead of 2, misreading the time."}]},{"n":2,"tier":2,"questions":[{"section":"bio","topic":"Biomolecules","stem":"Which class of biomolecule is built from amino acid monomers linked by peptide bonds?","choices":["Carbohydrates","Proteins","Lipids","Nucleic acids","Steroids"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Proteins are polymers of amino acids joined by peptide bonds, so the key is Proteins. Carbohydrates are polymers of monosaccharides (sugar monomers), so that choice confuses the wrong monomer. Nucleic acids are polymers of nucleotides, another common monomer mix-up. Lipids and steroids are not built from repeating amino acid monomers; lipids are largely fatty acids and glycerol, and steroids are a lipid subclass with a fused-ring structure."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cell structure & organelles","stem":"Which organelle is the primary site of ATP production through aerobic respiration in eukaryotic cells?","choices":["Ribosome","Nucleus","Mitochondrion","Golgi apparatus","Lysosome"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The mitochondrion houses the citric acid cycle and the electron transport chain, generating most of the cell's ATP, so it is the key. Ribosomes synthesize proteins, not ATP. The nucleus stores DNA and directs transcription. The Golgi apparatus modifies and packages proteins for transport. The lysosome carries digestive enzymes for breaking down waste; none of these is the main ATP-producing organelle."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Membrane transport","stem":"What is the term for the passive movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane from a region of lower solute concentration to a region of higher solute concentration?","choices":["Osmosis","Simple diffusion of solute","Active transport","Facilitated diffusion","Endocytosis"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Osmosis is specifically the passive movement of water down its own concentration gradient (toward higher solute) across a selectively permeable membrane, so it is the key. Simple diffusion of solute describes movement of the dissolved particles rather than water. Active transport requires ATP and moves substances against a gradient. Facilitated diffusion moves solutes through protein channels or carriers, not the bulk movement of water described here. Endocytosis engulfs material in vesicles and is not passive water movement."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cellular respiration","stem":"In aerobic cellular respiration, which stage generates the greatest number of ATP molecules?","choices":["Glycolysis","Pyruvate oxidation","Citric acid (Krebs) cycle","Oxidative phosphorylation (electron transport chain)","Fermentation"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Oxidative phosphorylation uses the electron transport chain and chemiosmosis to produce the large majority of ATP (roughly 34 of the ~36-38 total), so it is the key. Glycolysis yields only a net of 2 ATP. Pyruvate oxidation produces no ATP directly (it makes NADH). The citric acid cycle makes only 2 ATP/GTP directly. Fermentation is anaerobic and produces just the 2 ATP from glycolysis while regenerating NAD+."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Photosynthesis","stem":"In a chloroplast, where do the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis take place?","choices":["Stroma","Thylakoid membrane","Outer chloroplast membrane","Cytoplasm","Mitochondrial matrix"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The light-dependent reactions occur in the thylakoid membranes, which house the photosystems, electron transport chain, and ATP synthase, so that is the key. The stroma is where the light-independent (Calvin cycle) reactions occur, a very common swap. The outer chloroplast membrane is just a boundary and is not the reaction site. The cytoplasm hosts glycolysis, not photosynthesis. The mitochondrial matrix belongs to cellular respiration, not the chloroplast."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Central dogma (DNA->RNA->protein)","stem":"What is the name of the process by which an RNA molecule is synthesized from a DNA template?","choices":["Replication","Transcription","Translation","Reverse transcription","Transformation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Transcription is the synthesis of RNA from a DNA template, so it is the key. Replication copies DNA into DNA and does not make RNA. Translation is the next step, building a protein from an mRNA sequence at the ribosome. Reverse transcription goes the opposite direction, making DNA from an RNA template. Transformation is the uptake of foreign DNA by a cell and is unrelated to RNA synthesis."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mitosis & meiosis","stem":"Starting from a single diploid cell, how many daughter cells are produced when meiosis is completed?","choices":["1","2","4","8","16"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Meiosis includes two sequential divisions (meiosis I and II) that yield four haploid daughter cells, so 4 is the key. The answer 2 corresponds to mitosis, which involves only one division, a frequent confusion. The answer 1 ignores that division occurs at all. The values 8 and 16 come from mistakenly adding an extra round of division beyond the two that meiosis actually has."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mendelian genetics","stem":"In pea plants, tall (T) is dominant to short (t). If two heterozygous tall plants (Tt x Tt) are crossed, what fraction of the offspring are expected to be short?","choices":["0","1/4","1/2","3/4","1/1"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A Tt x Tt cross gives a genotypic ratio of 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt, so 1/4 of the offspring are homozygous recessive (short); that is the key. The 3/4 choice is the fraction that are tall, the opposite trait. The 1/2 choice matches the heterozygous (Tt) offspring, not the short ones. The 0 choice wrongly assumes no recessive offspring, and 1/1 wrongly assumes all are short."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Evolution & population genetics","stem":"Which term describes a change in allele frequencies in a population that results from random chance events, an effect that is especially strong in small populations?","choices":["Natural selection","Genetic drift","Gene flow","Mutation","Adaptation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Genetic drift is the random, chance-driven change in allele frequencies that has the largest impact in small populations, so it is the key. Natural selection changes allele frequencies based on differential fitness, not random chance. Gene flow is the movement of alleles between populations via migration. Mutation introduces new alleles but is not the chance-based frequency shift described. Adaptation is an outcome (a beneficial trait), not the mechanism of random frequency change."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Physiology (a body system)","stem":"In the human heart, which chamber pumps oxygenated blood out to the body through the aorta?","choices":["Right atrium","Right ventricle","Left atrium","Left ventricle","Aorta"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The left ventricle receives oxygenated blood from the left atrium and pumps it into the aorta for delivery to the body, so it is the key. The right ventricle pumps deoxygenated blood to the lungs, a common mix-up. The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood returning from the body. The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs but only passes it to the left ventricle rather than pumping it to the body. The aorta is the artery that carries the blood, not a heart chamber."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Moles & stoichiometry","stem":"How many moles of water (H2O) are present in 36 g of water? (Molar masses: H = 1 g/mol, O = 16 g/mol.)","choices":["0.5 mol","1 mol","2 mol","18 mol","36 mol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Molar mass of H2O = 2(1) + 16 = 18 g/mol. Moles = mass / molar mass = 36 g / 18 g/mol = 2 mol, so C is correct. 0.5 mol comes from inverting the division (18/36). 1 mol comes from mistakenly using 36 g/mol as the molar mass. 18 is the molar mass itself, not a mole count. 36 is simply the given mass in grams, left unconverted."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Atomic structure & quantum numbers","stem":"What is the maximum number of electrons that can occupy the 2p subshell?","choices":["2","6","8","10","14"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A p subshell contains 3 orbitals, and each orbital holds 2 electrons, so the maximum is 3 x 2 = 6 electrons (B). 2 is the capacity of a single orbital or an s subshell. 8 confuses subshell capacity with the octet (a full valence shell for period 2). 10 is the capacity of a d subshell, and 14 is the capacity of an f subshell."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Periodic trends","stem":"Based on periodic trends, which of the following elements has the highest electronegativity?","choices":["F","O","Cl","N","Na"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Electronegativity increases up and to the right of the periodic table, making fluorine the most electronegative element (A). Oxygen is second-highest but sits below and left of F. Chlorine is electronegative but has a larger radius than F (electronegativity decreases down a group), so it is lower. Nitrogen is less electronegative than O and F. Sodium is a metal on the far left and has very low electronegativity."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Bonding, Lewis & VSEPR","stem":"What is the molecular geometry of methane, CH4?","choices":["Linear","Trigonal planar","Bent","Tetrahedral","Trigonal pyramidal"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Carbon in CH4 has 4 bonding pairs and no lone pairs, giving 4 electron domains that arrange tetrahedrally with bond angles of about 109.5 degrees (D). Trigonal pyramidal describes NH3 (3 bonds + 1 lone pair). Bent describes H2O (2 bonds + 2 lone pairs). Trigonal planar describes 3 domains (e.g., BF3), and linear describes 2 domains (e.g., CO2). CH4 has no lone pairs, so the geometry matches the electron arrangement: tetrahedral."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Gas laws","stem":"A sample of gas occupies 2 L at 1 atm and constant temperature. If the pressure is increased to 2 atm, what is the new volume?","choices":["0.5 L","1 L","2 L","3 L","4 L"],"correct":1,"explanation":"At constant temperature, Boyle's law gives P1V1 = P2V2, so V2 = (1 atm x 2 L) / 2 atm = 1 L (B). 4 L results from treating volume as directly proportional to pressure (doubling V when P doubles), which is backwards. 2 L results from forgetting that pressure changed. 3 L comes from adding the 1 atm change to the volume. 0.5 L comes from over-applying the ratio (dividing twice)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Thermochemistry","stem":"Which statement correctly describes an exothermic reaction?","choices":["It absorbs heat and has a positive delta H","It releases heat and has a negative delta H","It absorbs heat and has a negative delta H","It releases heat and has a positive delta H","Its delta H is always zero"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Exothermic reactions release heat to the surroundings, and by convention the enthalpy change of the system is negative (delta H < 0), so B is correct. Choice A describes an endothermic reaction (absorbs heat, delta H positive). Choices C and D pair the correct energy direction with the wrong sign of delta H. A delta H of zero would mean no net heat change, which is not what exothermic means."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Kinetics","stem":"What is the primary effect of adding a catalyst to a chemical reaction?","choices":["It raises the activation energy","It lowers the activation energy and increases the reaction rate","It shifts the equilibrium toward the products","It makes the reaction more exothermic by lowering delta H","It is permanently consumed during the reaction"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with a lower activation energy, which speeds up the reaction (B). It does not raise activation energy (A is the opposite). A catalyst speeds the forward and reverse reactions equally, so it does not shift the position of equilibrium (C is a common misconception). It does not change delta H, which depends only on reactants and products (D). And a catalyst is regenerated, not consumed (E)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Equilibrium","stem":"For the reaction N2(g) + 3H2(g) reversible-arrow 2NH3(g), what happens to the equilibrium when the pressure is increased by decreasing the container volume?","choices":["It shifts left, toward the reactants","It shifts right, toward the products","No shift occurs because gases are on both sides","The reaction stops","The value of the equilibrium constant K increases"],"correct":1,"explanation":"By Le Chatelier's principle, increasing pressure shifts equilibrium toward the side with fewer moles of gas. The reactants have 4 moles of gas (1 + 3) and the products have 2 moles, so the system shifts right toward the products (B). It does not shift left (A is the wrong direction). The shift does occur because the two sides have unequal gas moles (C is wrong). Equilibrium is dynamic, not stopped (D). And K changes only with temperature, not pressure (E)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Acids, bases & pH","stem":"What is the pH of a 0.0010 M solution of HCl, a strong acid?","choices":["1","3","7","11","14"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HCl is a strong acid that dissociates completely, so [H+] = 0.0010 M = 1 x 10^-3 M. pH = -log(1 x 10^-3) = 3 (B). 11 is the pOH (14 - 3) mistakenly reported as pH. 7 assumes the solution is neutral, ignoring that HCl is an acid. 1 and 14 come from misreading the concentration exponent or confusing pH with the scale endpoints."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Electrochemistry","stem":"In a galvanic (voltaic) cell, at which electrode does oxidation occur?","choices":["The cathode","The anode","The salt bridge","Both electrodes equally","Neither electrode"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Oxidation always occurs at the anode, remembered by the mnemonic 'An Ox' (Anode = Oxidation), so B is correct. Reduction occurs at the cathode ('Red Cat'), so A reverses the two. The salt bridge maintains charge balance by allowing ion flow but is not a site of oxidation or reduction (C). Oxidation and reduction are separated onto different electrodes in a galvanic cell, so D and E are incorrect."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IUPAC nomenclature","stem":"What is the correct IUPAC name of the compound (CH3)2CHCH2CH3?","choices":["Pentane","3-methylbutane","2-methylbutane","Isopentane","2-methylpropane"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The longest continuous carbon chain is four carbons (butane) with a methyl branch. Numbering from the end nearest the branch places the methyl on C2, giving 2-methylbutane. 'Pentane' counts all five carbons as if they were in one straight chain, but the molecule is branched. '3-methylbutane' numbers from the wrong end (you must give the substituent the lowest locant). 'Isopentane' is the correct common name but not the IUPAC name. '2-methylpropane' undercounts the main chain by one carbon."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Stereochemistry (R/S, chirality)","stem":"Which of the following molecules contains a chiral (stereogenic) carbon?","choices":["CH2Cl2","CHFClBr","CCl4","CHCl3","CH2ClBr"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A chiral carbon must bear four different substituents. In CHFClBr the central carbon is bonded to H, F, Cl, and Br, all different, so it is a stereocenter and the molecule is chiral. CH2Cl2, CCl4, and CHCl3 each have at least two identical groups on carbon, so they are achiral (they have internal mirror symmetry). CH2ClBr is tempting because it carries two different halogens, but the carbon also has two hydrogens, so only three kinds of groups are present and it is not chiral."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"SN1/SN2/E1/E2","stem":"Which substrate undergoes an SN2 reaction the fastest?","choices":["(CH3)3CBr","(CH3)2CHBr","(CH3)3CCH2Br","CH3Br","CH3CH2Br"],"correct":3,"explanation":"SN2 proceeds through a backside attack, so the least sterically hindered carbon reacts fastest. CH3Br (a methyl halide) is the least hindered and reacts fastest. (CH3)3CBr is tertiary and essentially cannot do SN2 (it favors SN1/E1). (CH3)2CHBr is secondary, slower than methyl. (CH3)3CCH2Br is neopentyl: although primary, the bulky adjacent tert-butyl group blocks backside attack. CH3CH2Br (primary) is the closest competitor but is still slower than methyl."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alkene addition reactions","stem":"When HBr adds to propene (CH3CH=CH2), what is the major product?","choices":["1-bromopropane","2-bromopropane","1,2-dibromopropane","propan-1-ol","propan-2-ol"],"correct":1,"explanation":"HBr addition follows Markovnikov's rule: the H adds to the double-bond carbon that already has more hydrogens, and Br ends up on the more substituted carbon, giving 2-bromopropane (via the more stable secondary carbocation). 1-bromopropane is the anti-Markovnikov product, which forms only with peroxides (radical HBr). 1,2-dibromopropane would come from adding Br2, not HBr. Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol are alcohols from acid-catalyzed hydration (H2O), not from HBr; propan-2-ol is especially tempting because it has the correct Markovnikov regiochemistry but the wrong atom/reagent."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Aromaticity & EAS","stem":"Which of the following compounds is aromatic?","choices":["Cyclohexane","Cyclohexene","1,3,5-hexatriene","Cyclooctatetraene","Benzene"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Aromaticity requires a cyclic, planar, fully conjugated ring with (4n+2) pi electrons (Huckel's rule). Benzene has 6 pi electrons (4n+2 with n=1) in a planar ring, so it is aromatic. Cyclohexane has no pi bonds, and cyclohexene has only one isolated double bond. 1,3,5-hexatriene is fully conjugated but is an open chain, not a ring. Cyclooctatetraene is the strongest distractor: it is cyclic with double bonds, but it has 8 pi electrons (4n, not 4n+2) and adopts a non-planar tub shape, so it is non-aromatic."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alcohols & carbonyls","stem":"What is the product when 2-propanol (isopropyl alcohol) is oxidized?","choices":["Propanoic acid","Propanal","Propanone (acetone)","Propane","Propan-1-ol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"2-propanol is a secondary alcohol, and oxidation of a secondary alcohol gives a ketone, here propanone (acetone). Propanal (an aldehyde) and propanoic acid (a carboxylic acid) come from oxidizing a primary alcohol such as 1-propanol, not a secondary alcohol, and a secondary carbon cannot be oxidized to a carboxylic acid because it lacks the extra C-H needed. Propane would be a reduction (loss of oxygen), the opposite of oxidation. Propan-1-ol is just an isomer, not an oxidation product."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Acidity & pKa","stem":"Which of the following is the strongest acid?","choices":["CH3CH2OH","H2O","NH3","CH3COOH","CH3CH3"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The strongest acid has the lowest pKa, which corresponds to the most stabilized conjugate base. Acetic acid (CH3COOH, pKa about 5) is strongest because its conjugate base, acetate, is resonance-stabilized over two equivalent oxygens. Ethanol (pKa about 16) and water (pKa about 15.7) form alkoxide/hydroxide bases with no such resonance delocalization. Ammonia (pKa about 38) and ethane (pKa about 50) are far weaker acids because their conjugate bases (amide, carbanion) are very unstable."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Resonance & stability","stem":"Which of the following carbocations is most stabilized by resonance delocalization?","choices":["Methyl cation (CH3+)","Ethyl cation (CH3CH2+)","Isopropyl cation ((CH3)2CH+)","tert-Butyl cation ((CH3)3C+)","Benzyl cation (C6H5CH2+)"],"correct":4,"explanation":"The benzyl cation is stabilized by resonance: the positive charge is delocalized from the benzylic carbon into the aromatic ring across several resonance structures. The tert-butyl cation is the main distractor because it is the most stable of the simple alkyl cations, but its stability comes from hyperconjugation and inductive donation, not resonance. The methyl, ethyl, and isopropyl cations have progressively more alkyl stabilization but none has any resonance delocalization, so none is stabilized by resonance."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IR & H-NMR spectroscopy","stem":"In an IR spectrum, a strong, broad absorption spanning roughly 3200 to 3550 cm-1 most likely indicates the presence of which functional group?","choices":["C=O carbonyl","C(triple bond)C alkyne","O-H (alcohol)","C=C alkene","sp3 C-H"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A broad band in the ~3200 to 3550 cm-1 region is characteristic of an O-H stretch (alcohol), broadened by hydrogen bonding. A carbonyl (C=O) absorbs much lower, near 1700 to 1750 cm-1, as a strong sharp peak. An alkyne C-C triple bond stretch is a weaker band near 2100 to 2260 cm-1. A C=C alkene stretch appears around 1600 to 1680 cm-1. Ordinary sp3 C-H stretches occur just below 3000 cm-1 as sharp bands, not as a broad envelope above 3200 cm-1."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Synthesis / roadmap","stem":"Which reagent set converts propene (CH3CH=CH2) into propane (CH3CH2CH3)?","choices":["Br2 in CCl4","H2, Pd/C","HBr","H2O, H2SO4","Cold dilute KMnO4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Converting an alkene to an alkane requires adding H2 across the double bond, which is catalytic hydrogenation using H2 with a metal catalyst such as Pd/C. Br2 in CCl4 adds two bromines to give 1,2-dibromopropane. HBr adds H and Br to give 2-bromopropane (Markovnikov). H2O with H2SO4 is acid-catalyzed hydration, giving propan-2-ol. Cold dilute KMnO4 performs syn dihydroxylation, giving propane-1,2-diol. Only H2/Pd/C reduces the alkene all the way to the saturated alkane."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Fractions & decimals","stem":"Which decimal is equivalent to the fraction 3/8?","choices":["2.667","0.375","0.24","0.38","0.35"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Divide the numerator by the denominator: 3 divided by 8 = 0.375. The 2.667 comes from inverting the fraction and computing 8 divided by 3. The 0.24 comes from multiplying 3 by 8 instead of dividing. The 0.38 and 0.35 are careless rounded guesses rather than an exact division."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Algebra & equations","stem":"Solve for x: 3x + 5 = 20.","choices":["6.67","8.33","5","15","3"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Subtract 5 from both sides to get 3x = 15, then divide by 3 to get x = 5. The 15 results from subtracting but forgetting to divide by 3. The 6.67 (20/3) comes from ignoring the +5. The 8.33 comes from adding 5 instead of subtracting it (3x = 25)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Ratios & proportions","stem":"If 2/5 = x/20, what is the value of x?","choices":["4","8","10","40","50"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Cross-multiply: 2 times 20 = 5 times x, so 40 = 5x and x = 8. The 40 (2 times 20) skips the final division by 5. The 50 comes from cross-multiplying the wrong pair (5 times 20 divided by 2). The 10 is just half of 20, ignoring the ratio."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Percentages","stem":"What is 20% of 150?","choices":["3","15","30","45","300"],"correct":2,"explanation":"20% = 0.20, and 0.20 times 150 = 30. The 3 comes from misplacing the decimal (using 0.02). The 15 is 10% of 150, not 20%. The 45 is 30% of 150. The 300 comes from multiplying 20 times 150 and dividing by 10 incorrectly."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Exponents & roots","stem":"What is the value of 2^5?","choices":["10","16","25","32","64"],"correct":3,"explanation":"2^5 means 2 multiplied by itself five times: 2·2·2·2·2 = 32. The 10 comes from multiplying the base by the exponent (2 times 5). The 25 comes from computing 5^2 instead. The 16 is 2^4 and the 64 is 2^6, each off by one factor of 2."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Geometry (area/volume)","stem":"A rectangle has a length of 8 and a width of 5. What is its area?","choices":["13","20","26","40","80"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Area of a rectangle is length times width: 8 times 5 = 40. The 26 is the perimeter, 2 times (8 + 5). The 13 is just the sum of the sides. The 80 doubles the correct area, and 20 is roughly half of it."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Trigonometry","stem":"In the right triangle shown, the side opposite angle θ has length 3 and the hypotenuse has length 5. What is sin θ?","choices":["0.5","0.6","0.75","0.8","1.67"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Sine equals opposite over hypotenuse: 3/5 = 0.6. The 0.8 is 4/5, which is the cosine (adjacent over hypotenuse). The 0.75 is 3/4 (opposite over adjacent), which is the tangent. The 1.67 inverts the ratio to 5/3.","figureSvg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 220 160' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><polygon points='20,120 180,120 180,40' fill='none' stroke='#333' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='166' y='106' width='14' height='14' fill='none' stroke='#333' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='192' y='85' font-family='Georgia' font-size='14'>3</text><text x='92' y='72' font-family='Georgia' font-size='14'>5</text><text x='34' y='114' font-family='Georgia' font-size='14'>&#952;</text></svg>"},{"section":"qr","topic":"Probability","stem":"A bag contains 3 red marbles and 5 blue marbles. If one marble is drawn at random, what is the probability that it is red?","choices":["1/3","3/8","3/5","5/8","3/11"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Probability is favorable outcomes over total outcomes. There are 3 red marbles out of 3 + 5 = 8 total, so P(red) = 3/8. The 3/5 mistakenly uses red over blue instead of red over total. The 5/8 is the probability of drawing blue. The 3/11 wrongly adds 3 to 8 for the denominator."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Statistics (mean/median/SD)","stem":"What is the mean of the data set 3, 5, 8, 12, 2?","choices":["5","6","8","10","30"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The mean is the sum divided by the count: (3 + 5 + 8 + 12 + 2) = 30, and 30 divided by 5 = 6. The 30 is the sum without dividing. The 5 is the median (the middle value once sorted: 2, 3, 5, 8, 12). The 10 is the range (12 minus 2)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Word problems (rate/work/mixture)","stem":"A car travels 180 miles in 3 hours. What is its average speed?","choices":["20","60","90","183","540"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Average speed is distance divided by time: 180 miles / 3 hours = 60 miles per hour. The 540 comes from multiplying distance by time instead of dividing. The 183 comes from adding 180 and 3. The 90 comes from dividing by 2 instead of 3."}]},{"n":3,"tier":3,"questions":[{"section":"bio","topic":"Biomolecules","stem":"A ribosome joins 5 amino acids into a single linear polypeptide through dehydration (condensation) reactions. How many water molecules are released in forming this polypeptide?","choices":["3","4","5","8","10"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Each peptide bond forms by removing one water molecule, and linking 5 monomers into one chain requires (5 - 1) = 4 bonds, so 4 water molecules are released. The trap answer 5 comes from counting one water per amino acid instead of per bond. 10 comes from wrongly assuming 2 waters per bond, and 3 or 8 come from miscounting the number of linkages."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cell structure & organelles","stem":"A cell in the pancreas is specialized to synthesize and secrete large quantities of protein hormones for export. Which organelle would you expect to be especially abundant in this cell?","choices":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Lysosome","Central vacuole","Peroxisome"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Rough ER is studded with ribosomes and is the site where proteins destined for secretion are synthesized and folded, so a heavily secretory cell is rich in it. Smooth ER is the trap: it handles lipid synthesis and detoxification, not protein export. Lysosomes digest material, peroxisomes break down fatty acids and peroxides, and a large central vacuole is a plant feature, none of which drive protein secretion."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Membrane transport","stem":"A plant cell with an internal solute concentration of 0.4 M is placed in a beaker of 0.2 M solution. The cell membrane is permeable to water but not to the solute. What will happen?","choices":["Water leaves the cell and it plasmolyzes","Water enters the cell and it becomes turgid","Water enters the cell and it bursts (lyses)","There is no net movement of water","Solute diffuses out until both sides are 0.2 M"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The external solution (0.2 M) has a lower solute concentration than the cell (0.4 M), so it is hypotonic to the cell and water moves into the cell by osmosis, making it turgid. The rigid cell wall prevents bursting, so choice C is wrong even though water enters. Choice A reverses the direction of osmosis, choice D ignores the concentration gradient, and choice E is impossible because the membrane is not permeable to the solute."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cellular respiration","stem":"During intense exercise, a human muscle cell runs out of oxygen and produces ATP through glycolysis followed by lactic acid fermentation. What is the net ATP yield per glucose molecule under these anaerobic conditions?","choices":["0","2","4","32","36"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Glycolysis produces 4 ATP but consumes 2 ATP in its early steps, giving a net of 2 ATP per glucose, and fermentation itself adds no ATP (it only regenerates NAD+). The trap answer 4 counts the gross ATP produced without subtracting the 2 invested. 32 and 36 are aerobic totals that require oxygen and the electron transport chain, which are unavailable here."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Photosynthesis","stem":"A plant is supplied with water containing the heavy oxygen isotope (H2 18O) while its carbon dioxide contains only normal oxygen. After photosynthesis, where will the labeled 18O first appear?","choices":["In the O2 gas released","In glucose","In the CO2 taken in","In G3P","In ATP"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In the light reactions, water is split (photolysis), and the oxygen atoms from water are released as O2 gas, so the 18O label shows up first in the O2. The tempting choice is glucose, but the oxygen atoms incorporated into glucose come from CO2, not from water, so glucose stays unlabeled in this experiment. This tracing was demonstrated by classic isotope-labeling studies."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Central dogma (DNA->RNA->protein)","stem":"During transcription, RNA polymerase uses a DNA template strand with the sequence 3'-TACGGT-5'. What is the sequence of the mRNA produced?","choices":["5'-AUGCCA-3'","5'-ATGCCA-3'","5'-UACGGU-3'","5'-ACCGUA-3'","5'-TACGGT-3'"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The mRNA is complementary and antiparallel to the template, using uracil in place of thymine: template 3'-TACGGT-5' pairs base-by-base to give 5'-AUGCCA-3'. Choice B makes the correct complement but keeps thymine instead of using uracil. Choice C simply swaps T for U without complementing, choice D writes the correct sequence backwards, and choice E just copies the template strand unchanged."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mitosis & meiosis","stem":"An organism has a diploid chromosome number of 2n = 8. One of its cells completes meiosis. How many chromosomes are present in each resulting daughter cell?","choices":["2","4","8","12","16"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Meiosis is a reductional division that halves the chromosome number, so a diploid cell with 2n = 8 produces haploid daughter cells with n = 4 chromosomes each. The trap answer 8 treats meiosis like mitosis, which keeps the chromosome number the same. 16 comes from doubling instead of halving, and 2 or 12 come from miscounting the reduction."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mendelian genetics","stem":"In pea plants, purple flower color (P) is completely dominant to white (p). A heterozygous purple plant is crossed with a white-flowered plant. What fraction of the offspring are expected to have white flowers?","choices":["0","1/4","1/2","3/4","1"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The cross is Pp x pp. The Punnett square gives 1/2 Pp (purple) and 1/2 pp (white), so half the offspring are white. The trap answers 1/4 and 3/4 come from mistakenly setting up a Pp x Pp cross, which would give a 3:1 ratio. Because one parent is homozygous recessive, only two genotypes are possible in equal proportions."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Evolution & population genetics","stem":"A large, randomly mating population is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium for a gene with two alleles. The recessive allele frequency q is 0.3. What percentage of the population is expected to be heterozygous?","choices":["9%","21%","30%","42%","49%"],"correct":3,"explanation":"With q = 0.3, the dominant allele frequency p = 0.7, and the heterozygote frequency is 2pq = 2(0.7)(0.3) = 0.42, or 42%. The trap answer 21% comes from computing pq but forgetting the factor of 2. 9% is q squared (homozygous recessive), 49% is p squared (homozygous dominant), and 30% simply restates q."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Physiology (a body system)","stem":"After blood is oxygenated in the lungs, which blood vessel carries this oxygen-rich blood back to the heart?","choices":["Pulmonary vein","Pulmonary artery","Aorta","Vena cava","Coronary artery"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The pulmonary veins carry freshly oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium of the heart. This is an exception to the general rule that veins carry deoxygenated blood. The trap is the pulmonary artery, which carries deoxygenated blood the opposite direction, from the heart to the lungs. The aorta carries oxygenated blood away from the heart, the vena cava returns deoxygenated blood from the body, and the coronary artery supplies the heart muscle itself."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Moles & stoichiometry","stem":"Methane burns completely according to the equation: CH4 + 2 O2 -> CO2 + 2 H2O. If 0.50 mol of CH4 is fully combusted, what mass of CO2 is produced? (Molar mass of CO2 = 44 g/mol.)","choices":["11 g","16 g","22 g","44 g","88 g"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The mole ratio of CH4 to CO2 is 1:1, so 0.50 mol CH4 yields 0.50 mol CO2. Mass = 0.50 mol x 44 g/mol = 22 g. 44 g comes from forgetting to scale by 0.50 mol (using a full 1.0 mol). 88 g comes from wrongly applying the coefficient 2 (from O2/H2O) to CO2. 11 g results from using 0.25 mol (halving the given amount). 16 g mistakenly uses the molar mass of the reactant CH4."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Atomic structure & quantum numbers","stem":"What is the maximum number of electrons that can occupy the 3d subshell of an atom?","choices":["2","5","6","10","14"],"correct":3,"explanation":"For a d subshell the azimuthal quantum number l = 2, giving 2l + 1 = 5 orbitals. Each orbital holds 2 electrons, so 5 x 2 = 10 electrons. 5 counts the orbitals but forgets each holds two electrons. 6 is the capacity of a p subshell, and 14 is the capacity of an f subshell. 2 is the capacity of a single orbital (or an s subshell)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Periodic trends","stem":"Which of the following atoms has the largest atomic radius?","choices":["Na","Mg","Al","K","Ca"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Atomic radius increases going down a group and decreases going left to right across a period. K sits in period 4, group 1, so it is both farther down and farthest to the left, giving it the largest radius. Ca is in the same period as K but one column to the right, so its greater nuclear charge makes it smaller. Na is the largest of the period-3 atoms (Na, Mg, Al) but lies one period above K, so it is smaller. Mg and Al are smaller still."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Bonding, Lewis & VSEPR","stem":"Sulfur dioxide (SO2) has a central sulfur atom with two bonding regions and one lone pair. What is its molecular geometry?","choices":["Linear","Bent","Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal","Tetrahedral"],"correct":1,"explanation":"SO2 has three electron domains around sulfur (two bonding regions plus one lone pair), giving trigonal planar electron geometry. Because one domain is a lone pair, the molecular shape is bent (about 119 degrees). 'Linear' ignores the lone pair (treating it like CO2). 'Trigonal planar' reports the electron-domain geometry rather than the molecular shape. 'Trigonal pyramidal' assumes four domains with one lone pair (like NH3), and 'tetrahedral' assumes four domains with no lone pairs."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Gas laws","stem":"A 2.0 L sample of an ideal gas at 300 K is heated to 600 K while the pressure is held constant. What is the new volume?","choices":["1.0 L","2.0 L","4.0 L","6.0 L","8.0 L"],"correct":2,"explanation":"At constant pressure, Charles's law applies: V1/T1 = V2/T2. So (2.0 L)/(300 K) = V2/(600 K), giving V2 = 4.0 L. Doubling the absolute temperature doubles the volume. 1.0 L results from inverting the ratio (treating temperature like pressure in Boyle's law). 2.0 L assumes no change. 8.0 L and 6.0 L come from mis-scaling the temperature ratio (for example squaring it or adding 300)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Thermochemistry","stem":"How much heat is required to raise the temperature of 50.0 g of liquid water from 20 degrees C to 30 degrees C? (Specific heat of water = 4.18 J/(g x degrees C).)","choices":["209 J","1045 J","2090 J","4180 J","20,900 J"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Use q = m x c x deltaT with deltaT = 30 - 20 = 10 degrees C: q = 50.0 g x 4.18 J/(g x degrees C) x 10 degrees C = 2090 J. 209 J uses deltaT = 1 degree C. 4180 J mistakenly uses deltaT = 20 (treating the initial temperature as the change). 1045 J uses half the mass (25 g). 20,900 J includes an extra factor of ten."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Kinetics","stem":"A reaction has the experimentally determined rate law: rate = k[A][B]^2. If the concentration of B is tripled while [A] is held constant, by what factor does the reaction rate change?","choices":["1x","3x","6x","9x","27x"],"correct":3,"explanation":"B is second order, so the rate depends on [B]^2. Tripling [B] multiplies the rate by 3^2 = 9. 3x treats B as first order (ignoring the exponent). 6x incorrectly multiplies the order (2) by the concentration factor (3). 27x uses 3^3, treating B as third order. 1x assumes B does not appear in the rate law (zero order)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Equilibrium","stem":"Consider the exothermic equilibrium: N2(g) + 3 H2(g) <-> 2 NH3(g) + heat. Which change will shift the equilibrium toward producing more NH3?","choices":["Increasing the temperature","Decreasing the total pressure","Removing some N2","Increasing the total pressure","Adding a catalyst"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The reactant side has 4 moles of gas and the product side has 2 moles, so increasing the total pressure (by compression) shifts the equilibrium toward the side with fewer gas moles, favoring NH3. Increasing temperature adds heat to an exothermic reaction, shifting toward reactants. Decreasing pressure favors the side with more moles (reactants). Removing N2 pulls the equilibrium toward reactants. A catalyst speeds both directions equally and does not shift the position of equilibrium."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Acids, bases & pH","stem":"What is the pH of a 0.010 M solution of NaOH, a strong base, at 25 degrees C?","choices":["2.0","7.0","10.0","12.0","13.0"],"correct":3,"explanation":"NaOH dissociates completely, so [OH-] = 0.010 M = 1.0 x 10^-2. pOH = -log(1.0 x 10^-2) = 2. At 25 degrees C, pH = 14 - pOH = 14 - 2 = 12. 2.0 stops at pOH without converting to pH. 7.0 wrongly assumes a neutral solution. 13.0 uses [OH-] = 0.10 M (a misplaced decimal). 10.0 subtracts pOH from 12 instead of 14."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Electrochemistry","stem":"A galvanic cell is built from these standard reduction potentials: Zn2+ + 2 e- -> Zn, E = -0.76 V; Cu2+ + 2 e- -> Cu, E = +0.34 V. What is the standard cell potential for the spontaneous cell?","choices":["-1.10 V","-0.42 V","0.42 V","1.10 V","2.20 V"],"correct":3,"explanation":"For a spontaneous galvanic cell, copper (higher reduction potential) is the cathode and zinc is the anode. E_cell = E_cathode - E_anode = (+0.34) - (-0.76) = +1.10 V. -1.10 V reverses cathode and anode. -0.42 V and 0.42 V come from adding the potentials (0.34 + (-0.76)) instead of subtracting. 2.20 V wrongly multiplies E by the number of electrons (n = 2); standard potentials are intensive and are not scaled by n."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IUPAC nomenclature","stem":"What is the correct IUPAC name for the compound shown below (each vertex is a carbon; terminal carbons carry implied hydrogens)?","choices":["2-methylpent-4-ene","4-methylpent-1-ene","4-methylpent-2-ene","2-methylpent-1-ene","hex-1-ene"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The longest chain containing the C=C is five carbons (pent-ene) with a methyl branch. The double bond is the principal characteristic group expressed as the suffix, so numbering must give it the lowest locant: start at the double-bond end so the C=C is at C1 and the methyl lands at C4, giving 4-methylpent-1-ene. '2-methylpent-4-ene' comes from numbering from the wrong end (double bond would get the high locant 4). '4-methylpent-2-ene' misplaces the double bond. '2-methylpent-1-ene' puts the methyl on the wrong carbon. 'hex-1-ene' ignores the branch and wrongly counts a straight six-carbon chain.","figureSvg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 185 85\" width=\"200\" height=\"92\" role=\"img\"><g stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"45\"/><line x1=\"24\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"52\"/><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"65\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"45\"/><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"65\"/><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"15\"/></g></svg>"},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Stereochemistry (R/S, chirality)","stem":"The Fischer projection below shows a single stereocenter (horizontal bonds point toward you, vertical bonds point away). What is its absolute configuration?","choices":["R","S","The molecule is achiral","Configuration cannot be assigned without the optical rotation","Z"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Priorities: OH (1) > CHO (its carbon is bonded to O,O,H) (2) > CH2OH (carbon bonded to O,H,H) (3) > H (4). Reading 1 to 2 to 3 (OH-right, CHO-top, CH2OH-bottom) traces counterclockwise, which would read S. But the lowest priority (H) sits on a horizontal bond, so it points TOWARD the viewer; you must reverse the apparent direction, giving R. This is D-glyceraldehyde, whose absolute configuration is R. Choice B is the classic trap of forgetting to reverse when the #4 group is toward you. C is wrong: four different groups make one true stereocenter. D confuses R/S (a structural CIP assignment) with the sign of optical rotation, which is measured, not deduced. E confuses R/S with alkene E/Z.","figureSvg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" width=\"200\" height=\"120\" role=\"img\"><g stroke=\"#222\" stroke-width=\"2\"><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"92\"/><line x1=\"62\" y1=\"61\" x2=\"138\" y2=\"61\"/></g><g font-family=\"Georgia, serif\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#222\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"100\" y=\"22\">CHO</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"65\">H</text><text x=\"153\" y=\"65\">OH</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"110\">CH2OH</text></g></svg>"},{"section":"ochem","topic":"SN1/SN2/E1/E2","stem":"2-Bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide) is treated with a high concentration of sodium ethoxide in ethanol. Which mechanism predominates?","choices":["SN2","SN1","E1","E2","No reaction occurs"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A tertiary substrate plus a strong, high-concentration base (ethoxide) favors E2 elimination to give 2-methylpropene. SN2 (A) is blocked because the tertiary carbon is too hindered for backside attack. SN1 (B) and E1 (C) require a weak base/nucleophile (solvolysis conditions); a strong base drives the concerted E2 rather than a stepwise carbocation pathway. E is wrong because the reaction clearly proceeds."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alkene addition reactions","stem":"2-Methylbut-2-ene is treated with dilute aqueous sulfuric acid (acid-catalyzed hydration). What is the major product?","choices":["2-methylbutan-2-ol","3-methylbutan-2-ol","2-methylbutan-1-ol","2,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol","No reaction; H2O/H2SO4 cannot add to the alkene"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Acid-catalyzed hydration follows Markovnikov's rule via the more stable carbocation. Protonation places the positive charge on the more substituted alkene carbon (tertiary), so -OH ends up there, giving 2-methylbutan-2-ol. The initial carbocation is already tertiary, so no rearrangement occurs. B (3-methylbutan-2-ol) is the anti-Markovnikov product, placing OH on the less substituted carbon. C is a wrong regiochemistry/skeleton. D adds an extra carbon that is not present. E is wrong: acid readily catalyzes electrophilic addition of water to an alkene."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Aromaticity & EAS","stem":"Nitrobenzene undergoes a second nitration (HNO3/H2SO4, forcing conditions). What is the major product?","choices":["1,2-dinitrobenzene","1,3-dinitrobenzene","1,4-dinitrobenzene","No reaction; the ring is completely unreactive","1,3,5-trinitrobenzene"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The -NO2 group is a strong electron-withdrawing, meta-directing deactivator. It destabilizes the arenium (sigma-complex) intermediate most at the ortho and para positions, so the electrophile adds meta, giving 1,3-dinitrobenzene. A and C are the ortho/para products expected from activating (ortho/para-directing) groups, not a deactivator. D is a trap: nitrobenzene reacts more slowly but still reacts under forcing conditions. E over-nitrates; a single second substitution gives the di-substituted product."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alcohols & carbonyls","stem":"1-Propanol is treated with PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in dichloromethane. What is the major product?","choices":["propanal","propanoic acid","propan-2-one","propene","No reaction"],"correct":0,"explanation":"PCC is a mild, anhydrous oxidant that converts a primary alcohol to an aldehyde and stops there. So 1-propanol gives propanal. B (propanoic acid) is the over-oxidation product you would get with a strong aqueous oxidant like Jones reagent (H2CrO4), not PCC. C would require a secondary alcohol (2-propanol). D is dehydration, which needs acid/heat, not PCC. E is wrong: a primary alcohol is readily oxidized by PCC."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Acidity & pKa","stem":"Which of the following carboxylic acids is the strongest acid (lowest pKa)?","choices":["acetic acid","fluoroacetic acid","chloroacetic acid","trichloroacetic acid","propanoic acid"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Electron-withdrawing halogens stabilize the carboxylate conjugate base by the inductive effect, lowering pKa. Trichloroacetic acid has three chlorines pulling electron density away, giving the most stabilized anion and the strongest acid (pKa about 0.7). Fluoroacetic acid (B) is tempting because fluorine is the most electronegative atom, but a single fluorine cannot outweigh three chlorines. Chloroacetic acid (C) has only one Cl. Acetic (A) and propanoic (E) acids have only electron-donating alkyl groups and are the weakest here."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Resonance & stability","stem":"In which cation is the positive charge delocalized over more than one carbon by resonance?","choices":["tert-butyl cation, (CH3)3C+","isopropyl cation, (CH3)2CH+","allyl cation, CH2=CH-CH2+","ethyl cation, CH3CH2+","methyl cation, CH3+"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The allyl cation has an empty p orbital adjacent to a C=C pi bond, so it has two equivalent resonance structures and the positive charge is shared over C1 and C3 (true resonance delocalization). The tert-butyl cation (A) is the most stable overall by hyperconjugation and induction, which is the trap for anyone equating 'most stable' with 'resonance,' but its charge stays localized on one carbon with no pi system to delocalize into. The isopropyl, ethyl, and methyl cations likewise have no adjacent pi system and are stabilized only inductively/hyperconjugatively, not by resonance."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IR & H-NMR spectroscopy","stem":"A compound with molecular formula C3H6O shows a strong IR absorption near 1715 cm-1 and a 1H NMR spectrum consisting of a single peak (one singlet). Which compound is it?","choices":["propanal","propan-2-one (acetone)","prop-2-en-1-ol (allyl alcohol)","methyloxirane (propylene oxide)","methyl vinyl ether"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The 1715 cm-1 band indicates a C=O, and a single 1H NMR signal means all hydrogens are equivalent. Acetone fits both: its C=O absorbs near 1715 cm-1 and its six methyl hydrogens are equivalent, giving one singlet. Propanal (A) also has a C=O but has three sets of nonequivalent hydrogens, including an aldehyde H near 9.7 ppm, so it is not a singlet. Allyl alcohol (C), propylene oxide (D), and methyl vinyl ether (E) are all C3H6O isomers but lack a carbonyl, so none shows the strong 1715 cm-1 band, and each has several inequivalent hydrogens rather than one singlet."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Synthesis / roadmap","stem":"Which reagents convert but-1-ene into butan-1-ol (the anti-Markovnikov, primary alcohol)?","choices":["H2O, H2SO4 (catalytic)","BH3-THF; then H2O2, NaOH","Hg(OAc)2, H2O; then NaBH4","HBr; then aqueous NaOH","cold, dilute KMnO4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Hydroboration-oxidation (BH3-THF, then H2O2/NaOH) adds -H and -OH with anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry, placing OH on the less substituted terminal carbon to give the primary alcohol butan-1-ol. Acid-catalyzed hydration (A) and oxymercuration-demercuration (C) are both Markovnikov, putting OH on the more substituted carbon to give butan-2-ol. HBr then NaOH (D) proceeds through the Markovnikov 2-bromide and yields butan-2-ol (or elimination). Cold, dilute KMnO4 (E) is syn dihydroxylation, giving butane-1,2-diol, not a mono-alcohol."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Fractions & decimals","stem":"Evaluate: 3/4 − 0.6 + 1/5","choices":["0.15","1.55","0.35","−0.05","0.55"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Convert everything to decimals: 3/4 = 0.75 and 1/5 = 0.20. Then 0.75 − 0.60 + 0.20 = 0.35 (choice C). 0.15 drops the +1/5 term (0.75 − 0.60). 1.55 adds all three (0.75 + 0.60 + 0.20). −0.05 wrongly subtracts the last term (0.75 − 0.60 − 0.20). 0.55 comes from misconverting 0.6 to 0.4 (0.75 − 0.40 + 0.20)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Algebra & equations","stem":"Solve for x: 3(x − 2) = 2x + 5","choices":["7","11","−1","−7","1"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Distribute the 3: 3x − 6 = 2x + 5. Subtract 2x and add 6 to both sides: x = 11 (choice B). 7 comes from failing to distribute (writing 3x − 2 = 2x + 5). −1 is a sign error moving the 6 (3x − 2x = 5 − 6). −7 comes from dividing only the left side by 3 (x − 2 = 2x + 5). 1 results from mishandling the constant when collecting terms (x − 1 = 0)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Ratios & proportions","stem":"In a class the ratio of boys to girls is 5:3. If there are 32 students total, how many are girls?","choices":["20","4","19.2","12","10.67"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The ratio has 5 + 3 = 8 equal parts, so each part is 32 ÷ 8 = 4 students. Girls get 3 parts: 3 × 4 = 12 (choice D). 20 is the number of boys (5 parts). 4 is the size of one part, not the total girls. 19.2 wrongly takes 3/5 of 32. 10.67 wrongly divides 32 by 3."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Percentages","stem":"After a 25% discount, a jacket sells for $90. What was its original price?","choices":["$112.50","$360","$120","$67.50","$115"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A 25% discount means the sale price is 75% of the original: 0.75 × original = 90, so original = 90 ÷ 0.75 = $120 (choice C). $112.50 wrongly adds 25% of the sale price (90 × 1.25). $360 divides by the discount rate 0.25 instead of 0.75. $67.50 applies the discount again (90 × 0.75). $115 treats 25% as a flat $25 added to $90."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Exponents & roots","stem":"Simplify: (2^3 × 2^−1)^2","choices":["4","256","8","16","64"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Inside the parentheses add exponents: 2^3 × 2^−1 = 2^(3−1) = 2^2. Then square: (2^2)^2 = 2^4 = 16 (choice D). 4 stops at 2^2, forgetting the outer square. 256 comes from adding |exponents| as 3 + 1 = 4 then squaring to 2^8. 8 keeps only 2^3. 64 = 2^6 from mismultiplying the exponents (3 × 2) and dropping the −1."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Geometry (area/volume)","stem":"A cylinder has radius 3 and height 10. What is its volume?","choices":["30π","90π","360π","900π","60π"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Volume of a cylinder is V = πr²h = π(3²)(10) = π(9)(10) = 90π (choice B). 30π forgets to square the radius (π·3·10). 360π mistakenly uses the diameter 6 as the radius (π·6²·10). 900π squares both the radius and the height (π·9·100). 60π is the lateral surface area 2πrh, not the volume."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Trigonometry","stem":"In the right triangle shown, the side opposite angle θ is 6 and the hypotenuse is 10. What is cos θ?","choices":["0.6","0.75","0.8","0.4","1.33"],"correct":2,"explanation":"First find the adjacent side with the Pythagorean theorem: adjacent = √(10² − 6²) = √64 = 8. Then cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse = 8/10 = 0.8 (choice C). 0.6 is sin θ (opposite/hypotenuse = 6/10). 0.75 is tan θ (opposite/adjacent = 6/8). 0.4 wrongly takes the adjacent side as 10 − 6 = 4 instead of using the Pythagorean theorem. 1.33 inverts the tangent (8/6).","figureSvg":"<svg viewBox='0 0 240 180' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'><polygon points='30,150 210,150 210,50' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='196' y='136' width='14' height='14' fill='none' stroke='currentColor' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='120' y='168' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='currentColor'>adjacent = ?</text><text x='222' y='104' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='currentColor'>6</text><text x='108' y='92' font-size='14' text-anchor='middle' fill='currentColor'>10</text><text x='52' y='144' font-size='14' fill='currentColor'>&#952;</text></svg>"},{"section":"qr","topic":"Probability","stem":"A bag holds 4 red and 6 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","choices":["4/25","8/45","11/15","2/15","2/5"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Without replacement, P(both red) = (4/10) × (3/9) = 12/90 = 2/15 (choice D). 4/25 treats it as with replacement, (4/10)(4/10). 8/45 forgets to reduce the red count on the second draw, (4/10)(4/9). 11/15 adds the two probabilities instead of multiplying (2/5 + 1/3). 2/5 accounts for only the first draw."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Statistics (mean/median/SD)","stem":"Find the median of the data set: 8, 15, 4, 8, 10","choices":["4","8","9","10","11.5"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Sort the values first: 4, 8, 8, 10, 15. With five values the median is the third one, which is 8 (choice B). 4 is the middle of the unsorted list (no ordering done). 9 is the mean (45 ÷ 5), not the median. 10 comes from miscounting the middle position after sorting. 11.5 averages two middle values as if the count were even."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Word problems (rate/work/mixture)","stem":"One pipe fills a tank in 4 hours and another fills it in 6 hours. Working together, how long does it take to fill the tank?","choices":["5 h","10 h","0.42 h","2 h","2.4 h"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Add the rates: 1/4 + 1/6 = 3/12 + 2/12 = 5/12 tank per hour. Time = 1 ÷ (5/12) = 12/5 = 2.4 hours (choice E). 5 h averages the two times, (4+6)/2. 10 h adds the times. 0.42 h reports the combined rate 5/12 without inverting it to a time. 2 h comes from adding the fractions incorrectly as 1/4 + 1/6 = 1/2, then inverting."}]},{"n":4,"tier":4,"questions":[{"section":"bio","topic":"Biomolecules","stem":"At room temperature, most animal fats (which are saturated) are solid, whereas most plant oils (which are unsaturated) are liquid. Which statement best explains this difference?","choices":["Saturated fatty acids contain cis double bonds that kink their chains, letting them pack tightly.","Unsaturated fatty acids have cis double bonds that introduce kinks, preventing the chains from packing tightly.","Unsaturated fatty acids contain more hydrogen atoms, forming stronger hydrogen bonds between chains.","Saturated fatty acids are polar and therefore dissolve readily in water.","Unsaturated fatty acids are always much shorter, which is what lowers their melting point."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Unsaturated fatty acids contain cis carbon-carbon double bonds that put a bend (kink) in the hydrocarbon tail. Kinked chains cannot stack closely, so van der Waals attractions between them are weaker and the fat stays liquid; straight saturated chains pack tightly and are solid. Choice A reverses the fact (double bonds are found in UNSATURATED, not saturated, fatty acids). C is the classic beginner trap: unsaturated fatty acids actually have FEWER hydrogens (a double bond replaces two C-H bonds), and physical state is governed by packing/van der Waals forces, not hydrogen bonding. D is wrong because fats are nonpolar and insoluble in water. E misapplies chain length; the saturation (kinks), not length, is the deciding factor here."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cell structure & organelles","stem":"A cell in the adrenal cortex specializes in synthesizing large amounts of steroid (lipid-based) hormones. Which organelle would you expect to be especially abundant in this cell?","choices":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Nucleolus","Lysosome","Peroxisome"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The smooth ER is the primary site of lipid and steroid synthesis, so a steroid-secreting cell is packed with it. The tempting trap is A, rough ER: students reach for it because it is the 'export factory,' but the rough ER (studded with ribosomes) makes and processes PROTEINS, not steroids. Steroids are lipids, which are handled by the SMOOTH ER. The nucleolus assembles ribosomes, the lysosome performs intracellular digestion, and the peroxisome breaks down fatty acids and detoxifies; none is the main steroid-synthesis site."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Membrane transport","stem":"A small polar solute crosses the plasma membrane by passing through a specific channel protein, moving from an area of higher concentration to an area of lower concentration, and the cell expends no ATP. This process is best described as:","choices":["Simple diffusion","Facilitated diffusion","Active transport","Primary active transport","Endocytosis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Transport that uses a membrane protein, follows the concentration gradient (high to low), and requires no ATP is facilitated diffusion, a form of passive transport. The key trap is C (active transport): beginners assume that any protein-mediated transport must consume energy, but 'active' specifically means pumping AGAINST the gradient using ATP, which is not happening here. Simple diffusion (A) would not require a protein at all, since the solute is polar it needs the channel. Primary active transport (D) also requires ATP. Endocytosis (E) engulfs material in vesicles, not through a single channel protein."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cellular respiration","stem":"When oxygen is unavailable, human muscle cells convert pyruvate into lactate. What is the primary benefit of this fermentation step for the cell?","choices":["It generates a large amount of ATP directly from the conversion of pyruvate to lactate.","It regenerates NAD+ so that glycolysis can continue producing ATP.","It produces oxygen that restarts the electron transport chain.","It converts lactate directly back into glucose for storage.","It directly powers the Krebs cycle inside the mitochondria."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Fermentation's job is to reoxidize NADH back to NAD+. Glycolysis needs a supply of NAD+ to keep running; without oxygen the electron transport chain is shut down and NADH cannot be reoxidized there, so lactate fermentation regenerates NAD+ and glycolysis keeps making its small net yield of 2 ATP. The trap is A: the pyruvate-to-lactate step itself produces NO ATP; all the ATP comes from glycolysis, which fermentation merely enables. C is false because fermentation does not create oxygen. D describes gluconeogenesis (a separate, energy-requiring process), and E is wrong because the Krebs cycle is not running under anaerobic conditions."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Photosynthesis","stem":"During photosynthesis, plants release O2 gas into the atmosphere. This oxygen is derived directly from which molecule?","choices":["Carbon dioxide (CO2)","Water (H2O)","Glucose (C6H12O6)","ATP","Ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The O2 released in photosynthesis comes from the splitting of water (photolysis) during the light reactions; the oxygen atoms of H2O are released as O2 gas. The dominant beginner trap is A (CO2): students reason that because CO2 is taken in and contains oxygen, it must be the source of the released O2. In fact, the carbon and oxygen atoms of CO2 are incorporated into sugar during the Calvin cycle, not exhaled as O2. Glucose (C) is a product, and ATP (D) and RuBP (E) are internal intermediates, none of which is the source of the released oxygen."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Central dogma (DNA->RNA->protein)","stem":"A segment of the DNA template (transcribed) strand reads 3'-A T G C-5'. What is the sequence of the mRNA transcribed from this template?","choices":["5'-T A C G-3'","5'-U A C G-3'","5'-A T G C-3'","5'-G C A U-3'","3'-U A C G-5'"],"correct":1,"explanation":"mRNA is built antiparallel and complementary to the template, with uracil replacing thymine. Pairing each template base (A pairs with U, T with A, G with C, C with G) and reading the new strand 5' to 3' gives 5'-U A C G-3'. The key trap is A (5'-T A C G-3'), which is the correct complement but keeps thymine instead of switching to uracil, the single most common transcription mistake. C simply copies the template rather than complementing it, D reverses the reading order, and E lists the right bases but labels the polarity backward."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mitosis & meiosis","stem":"An organism has a diploid chromosome number of 8 (2n = 8). After one of its germ cells completes meiosis, how many chromosomes will each resulting gamete contain?","choices":["2","4","8","16","32"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Meiosis is a reduction division: it halves the chromosome number from diploid (2n = 8) to haploid (n = 4), so each gamete carries 4 chromosomes. The main trap is C (8): forgetting that meiosis reduces the number and instead reporting the diploid count. Choice D (16) reflects confusion with DNA replication in S phase (which doubles the DNA but not the chromosome number), and choice A (2) over-halves the count."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mendelian genetics","stem":"In pea plants, purple flower color (P) is completely dominant to white (p). A purple-flowered plant is crossed with a white-flowered plant, and ALL of the resulting offspring have purple flowers. What is the most likely genotype of the purple parent?","choices":["pp","Pp","PP","PPpp","Cannot be determined from the information given"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The white parent must be pp (recessive phenotype requires two recessive alleles). If the purple parent were heterozygous (Pp), the cross Pp x pp would yield about half white offspring. Because EVERY offspring is purple, the purple parent is most likely homozygous PP (PP x pp gives all Pp, all purple). The trap is B (Pp): beginners assume a purple plant is heterozygous by default, but that genotype would have produced white offspring. Choice A (pp) would itself be white, and D is not a valid genotype for a single gene."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Evolution & population genetics","stem":"In a large population that is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, 16% of individuals express a recessive phenotype. What is the frequency of the recessive allele in this population?","choices":["0.04","0.16","0.40","0.60","0.84"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The recessive phenotype frequency equals q^2, the frequency of the homozygous recessive genotype. So q^2 = 0.16, and q = sqrt(0.16) = 0.40. The classic trap is B (0.16): using the phenotype frequency directly as the allele frequency, forgetting to take the square root of q^2. Choice D (0.60) is p, the dominant allele frequency (1 - q), and E (0.84) is the dominant PHENOTYPE frequency (p^2 + 2pq = 1 - 0.16)."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Physiology (a body system)","stem":"In the human circulatory system, which blood vessel carries oxygen-rich blood back TOWARD the heart, returning it to a heart chamber?","choices":["Pulmonary artery","Pulmonary vein","Aorta","Superior vena cava","Coronary artery"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The pulmonary vein returns freshly oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium, so it carries oxygen-rich blood back toward a heart chamber. The strongest trap is A (pulmonary artery): students assume 'artery = oxygenated,' but the pulmonary artery is the exception that carries DEOXYGENATED blood AWAY from the heart to the lungs. The aorta (C) does carry oxygen-rich blood but AWAY from the heart, failing the 'toward the heart' condition. The superior vena cava (D) carries deoxygenated blood toward the heart, and the coronary artery (E) delivers oxygen-rich blood outward into the heart muscle rather than returning it to a chamber."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Moles & stoichiometry","stem":"Methane burns completely in excess oxygen: CH4 + 2 O2 -> CO2 + 2 H2O. How many grams of CO2 are produced from 8.0 g of CH4? (Molar masses: CH4 = 16 g/mol, CO2 = 44 g/mol)","choices":["11 g","22 g","44 g","8.0 g","16 g"],"correct":1,"explanation":"8.0 g CH4 / 16 g/mol = 0.50 mol CH4. The CH4:CO2 ratio is 1:1, so 0.50 mol CO2 x 44 g/mol = 22 g. 11 g halves the answer by wrongly applying the O2 coefficient (2) to CO2. 44 g skips the mass-to-mole step and assumes a full 1 mol of CH4. 8.0 g and 16 g are non-calculations (assuming mass is conserved as the reactant, or just quoting the molar mass of CH4)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Atomic structure & quantum numbers","stem":"Which of the following sets of quantum numbers (n, l, ml, ms) is NOT allowed for an electron?","choices":["(2, 1, -1, +1/2)","(3, 2, 0, -1/2)","(2, 2, 1, +1/2)","(4, 0, 0, +1/2)","(3, 1, -1, -1/2)"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The azimuthal quantum number l can only range from 0 to n - 1. In (2, 2, 1, +1/2), l = 2 requires n of at least 3, so it is forbidden. It is tempting because ml = 1 is within the allowed -l to +l range and ms = +1/2 is valid, so students who only check those two miss the l < n rule. Every other set obeys both l <= n - 1 and -l <= ml <= +l."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Periodic trends","stem":"The species O2-, F-, Na+, Mg2+, and Al3+ are isoelectronic (all have 10 electrons). Which one has the SMALLEST ionic radius?","choices":["O2-","F-","Na+","Mg2+","Al3+"],"correct":4,"explanation":"For an isoelectronic series, the same 10 electrons are held by different numbers of protons; more protons means a stronger pull and a smaller radius. Al3+ has 13 protons, the most in the set, so it is smallest. O2- (only 8 protons) is actually the largest, so choosing it confuses 'more negative charge / more electrons' with 'smaller size.' The middle choices are ranked correctly by nuclear charge but none pulls as hard as Al3+."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Bonding, Lewis & VSEPR","stem":"What is the molecular geometry of SO2?","choices":["Linear","Bent","Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal","Tetrahedral"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Sulfur in SO2 has three electron domains: two bonding regions and one lone pair (AX2E), giving a bent molecular shape near 119 degrees. Linear is the classic trap of ignoring the lone pair and treating SO2 like CO2. Trigonal planar names the electron-domain geometry, not the molecular shape once the lone pair position is removed. Trigonal pyramidal and tetrahedral wrongly assume four electron domains."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Gas laws","stem":"A sample of gas occupies 3.0 L at 27 degrees C. At constant pressure, what volume does it occupy at 127 degrees C?","choices":["2.25 L","4.0 L","14.1 L","3.0 L","0.71 L"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Charles's law (V1/T1 = V2/T2) requires absolute temperature: 27 C = 300 K and 127 C = 400 K, so V2 = 3.0 L x (400/300) = 4.0 L. 14.1 L is the signature trap of using Celsius directly (3.0 x 127/27). 2.25 L inverts the temperature ratio (using 300/400). 0.71 L is a non-physical shrink from compounding the inverted ratio with a Celsius error; heating a gas at constant pressure must increase its volume, so any answer below 3.0 L is impossible. 3.0 L assumes no change at all."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Thermochemistry","stem":"For the reaction 2 H2(g) + O2(g) -> 2 H2O(l), delta H = -572 kJ. What is the enthalpy change when 3.0 mol of H2 reacts completely?","choices":["-572 kJ","-858 kJ","-1716 kJ","+858 kJ","-286 kJ"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Enthalpy scales with the amount reacting. The -572 kJ is released per 2 mol of H2, so for 3.0 mol: -572 kJ x (3.0/2) = -858 kJ. -1716 kJ wrongly treats -572 kJ as the value per mole of H2 and multiplies by 3. -572 kJ uses the tabulated value without scaling. +858 kJ has the right magnitude but flips the sign of an exothermic reaction. -286 kJ is the value for only 1 mol."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Kinetics","stem":"A reaction has the rate law rate = k[A][B]^2. If the concentration of B is tripled while [A] is held constant, how does the reaction rate change?","choices":["Increases 3 times","Increases 6 times","Increases 9 times","Increases 27 times","No change"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The reaction is second order in B, so tripling [B] multiplies the rate by 3^2 = 9. 'Increases 3 times' ignores the exponent and treats B as first order. 'Increases 27 times' cubes the factor (3^3), confusing the exponent value with 3. 'Increases 6 times' multiplies the factor by the exponent (3 x 2). 'No change' wrongly assumes only [A] affects rate."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Equilibrium","stem":"For the reaction C(s) + H2O(g) <-> CO(g) + H2(g), which is the correct equilibrium expression Kc?","choices":["[CO][H2] / [H2O]","[CO][H2] / ([C][H2O])","[H2O] / ([CO][H2])","[C][H2O] / ([CO][H2])","[CO][H2] / [C]"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Pure solids (and pure liquids) are excluded from the equilibrium expression because their concentration is constant. Carbon is a solid, so it is omitted: Kc = [CO][H2]/[H2O]. Including [C] in the denominator is the classic trap. The remaining choices invert the expression (reactants over products) and/or also incorrectly include the solid."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Acids, bases & pH","stem":"What is the pH of a 0.010 M solution of NaOH at 25 degrees C?","choices":["2.0","7.0","12.0","13.0","10.0"],"correct":2,"explanation":"NaOH is a strong base, so [OH-] = 0.010 M, giving pOH = -log(0.010) = 2, and pH = 14 - 2 = 12.0. The big trap is 2.0: computing -log(0.010) and reporting it as pH as if the concentration were [H+]. 7.0 wrongly assumes a dilute solution is neutral. 13.0 misreads the exponent (using pOH = 1). 10.0 subtracts from 12 instead of from 14."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Electrochemistry","stem":"Given the standard reduction potentials Cu2+ + 2 e- -> Cu (E = +0.34 V) and Zn2+ + 2 e- -> Zn (E = -0.76 V), what is the standard cell potential for the spontaneous galvanic cell built from these two half-cells?","choices":["0.42 V","1.10 V","-0.42 V","2.20 V","-1.10 V"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Zinc is oxidized (anode) and copper is reduced (cathode). E(cell) = E(cathode) - E(anode) = 0.34 - (-0.76) = +1.10 V, which is positive as expected for a spontaneous cell. 2.20 V is the trap of multiplying by the 2 electrons transferred, but cell potential is intensive and is never scaled by coefficients. 0.42 V drops a negative sign; -0.42 V adds the two values; -1.10 V reverses the anode and cathode."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IUPAC nomenclature","stem":"What is the correct IUPAC name for the compound CH2=CHCH2CH(CH3)CH3?","choices":["4-methyl-1-pentene","2-methyl-4-pentene","4-methyl-2-pentene","2-methyl-1-pentene","4-methylpentane"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The longest chain containing the double bond is five carbons (pentene). The chain must be numbered to give the double bond the lowest possible locant, so numbering starts at the CH2= end: the double bond is between C1 and C2, and the methyl branch lands on C4, giving 4-methyl-1-pentene. The tempting trap is 2-methyl-4-pentene: that comes from numbering from the wrong end (the saturated side), which puts the double bond at C4 and violates the lowest-locant rule for the principal unsaturation. 4-methyl-2-pentene misplaces the double bond; 2-methyl-1-pentene misplaces the methyl; and 4-methylpentane ignores the alkene entirely."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Stereochemistry (R/S, chirality)","stem":"A molecule has a single stereocenter bearing four different groups: -Cl (CIP priority 1), -CH2CH3 (priority 2), -CH3 (priority 3), and -H (priority 4, lowest). In the drawn structure the H atom points directly toward the viewer, and tracing Cl to CH2CH3 to CH3 sweeps clockwise. What is the configuration at this stereocenter?","choices":["R","S","Cannot be determined without a physical model","Meso","The molecule is achiral"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Reading priorities 1 to 2 to 3 appears clockwise, which by itself suggests R. But R/S is defined with the lowest priority group (H) pointing AWAY from the viewer. Here H points toward the viewer, so the apparent rotation is reversed: the true sense is counterclockwise, giving S. The trap answer R comes from taking the clockwise sweep at face value and forgetting to flip when the lowest-priority group faces you. 'Cannot be determined' is wrong because the description fully specifies the geometry; 'meso' requires at least two stereocenters; and the molecule is clearly chiral (one stereocenter, four different groups)."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"SN1/SN2/E1/E2","stem":"2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide) is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOCH2CH3) in ethanol. What is the predominant reaction pathway and major product?","choices":["SN2, giving tert-butyl ethyl ether","SN1, giving tert-butyl ethyl ether","E1, giving 2-methylpropene","E2, giving 2-methylpropene","No reaction occurs"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The substrate is tertiary, so backside SN2 attack is blocked by steric hindrance. Ethoxide is a strong base, and a strong base drives bimolecular elimination (E2), removing a beta hydrogen to give 2-methylpropene (isobutylene). The tempting trap is SN2 giving the ether: students see a good nucleophile and reach for substitution, but a tertiary carbon cannot undergo SN2. SN1 and E1 are minor here because the strong base favors the concerted E2 over slow ionization pathways, and 'no reaction' is wrong since E2 proceeds readily."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alkene addition reactions","stem":"2-methylpropene (isobutylene) is treated with (1) BH3 in THF, then (2) H2O2 with NaOH. What is the major product?","choices":["2-methyl-2-propanol (tert-butyl alcohol)","2-methyl-1-propanol (isobutyl alcohol)","2-methylpropane","1-bromo-2-methylpropane","2-bromo-2-methylpropane"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Hydroboration-oxidation adds H and OH across the double bond with anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry: boron (and ultimately OH) attaches to the LESS substituted carbon. For 2-methylpropene, OH goes to the terminal CH2, giving 2-methyl-1-propanol. The trap answer, 2-methyl-2-propanol, is the Markovnikov product you would get from acid-catalyzed hydration; students who default to 'OH on the more substituted carbon' fall for it. Hydroboration also involves no carbocation, so no rearrangement occurs, and the bromide choices are irrelevant to this OH-installing sequence."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Aromaticity & EAS","stem":"Chlorobenzene is subjected to nitration (HNO3/H2SO4). Which best describes the outcome?","choices":["Predominantly the meta product, because chlorine is deactivating","Predominantly the ortho and para products","Predominantly the meta and para products","A roughly statistical ortho:meta:para mixture with no positional preference","No reaction; chlorobenzene is too deactivated to undergo EAS"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Halogens are a special case: they are deactivating (electron-withdrawing by induction, so the reaction is slower than benzene) yet ortho/para DIRECTING, because chlorine's lone pairs donate by resonance to stabilize the arenium ion specifically at the ortho and para positions. So the major products are ortho- and para-nitrochlorobenzene. The classic trap is 'meta,' which conflates 'deactivating' with 'meta-directing' (true for groups like nitro, but not for halogens). The reaction does proceed (just more slowly), so 'no reaction' and a random statistical mixture are both wrong."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alcohols & carbonyls","stem":"1-propanol (CH3CH2CH2OH) is treated with PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in dichloromethane. What is the major product?","choices":["Propanoic acid","Propanal","Propanone (acetone)","1-propanol (no reaction)","Propene"],"correct":1,"explanation":"PCC is a mild, anhydrous oxidant that stops the oxidation of a primary alcohol at the aldehyde stage, giving propanal. The trap answer, propanoic acid, is the over-oxidation product you would obtain with a strong aqueous oxidant such as chromic acid (Jones) or KMnO4; students often assume any oxidation of a primary alcohol goes all the way to the carboxylic acid. Acetone would require a secondary alcohol (2-propanol), 'no reaction' is wrong because PCC does oxidize the alcohol, and propene would require dehydration, not oxidation."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Acidity & pKa","stem":"Which of the following is the strongest acid?","choices":["Iodoacetic acid (ICH2COOH)","Bromoacetic acid (BrCH2COOH)","Chloroacetic acid (ClCH2COOH)","Fluoroacetic acid (FCH2COOH)","Acetic acid (CH3COOH)"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Acid strength tracks the stability of the conjugate base (carboxylate). An electron-withdrawing halogen on the alpha carbon pulls electron density away by induction, spreading out and stabilizing the negative charge. The MORE electronegative the halogen, the stronger this inductive stabilization, so fluoroacetic acid is the strongest of the set (pKa about 2.6, versus about 4.76 for unsubstituted acetic acid, which is the weakest). The trap is iodoacetic acid: students reason that the largest/heaviest halogen must be the most electron-withdrawing. But inductive withdrawal is governed by electronegativity, not atomic size, and fluorine is the most electronegative halogen."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Resonance & stability","stem":"Which of the following carbocations is the most stable?","choices":["CH3+ (methyl cation)","CH3CH2+ (ethyl cation)","C6H5CH2+ (benzyl cation)","(CH3)2CH+ (isopropyl cation)","CH3CH2CH2+ (propyl cation)"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The benzyl cation is stabilized by resonance: its positive charge is delocalized into the aromatic ring through several resonance structures, spreading the charge onto multiple ring carbons and substantially lowering the energy. This extensive resonance stabilization makes it clearly more stable than a simple secondary carbocation. The tempting trap is the isopropyl (secondary) cation: students rank cations only by degree of substitution (tertiary > secondary > primary) and pick the secondary carbon, overlooking that delocalizing the charge over an aromatic ring outweighs the modest hyperconjugative stabilization of a single secondary center. (Note that a plain allyl cation, delocalized over only two carbons, is roughly comparable to a secondary cation, which is why benzyl rather than allyl is used here to make the winner unambiguous.) Methyl, ethyl, and propyl are localized methyl/primary cations and are the least stable."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IR & H-NMR spectroscopy","stem":"A compound with molecular formula C3H6O shows a strong IR absorption at 1715 cm^-1 and exhibits only a single peak (one signal) in its 1H NMR spectrum. Which compound is it?","choices":["Propanal (CH3CH2CHO)","Propanone (acetone)","1-propanol","2-propen-1-ol (allyl alcohol)","Methyloxirane (propylene oxide)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A strong band at 1715 cm^-1 is a carbonyl (C=O) stretch, and a single 1H NMR signal means every hydrogen is equivalent. Acetone fits both: it has a C=O and two identical methyl groups, so all six protons appear as one singlet. The trap is propanal, which also has a carbonyl (about 1725 cm^-1) but would give three distinct signals (CH3, CH2, and a downfield aldehyde H near 9.7 ppm), so it fails the 'one signal' clue. The alcohols (1-propanol, allyl alcohol) would show a broad O-H stretch around 3200-3550 cm^-1 and lack a clean 1715 band, and the epoxide has no strong carbonyl absorption."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Synthesis / roadmap","stem":"You need to convert 2-butyne into trans-2-butene (the E isomer). Which reagent set accomplishes this?","choices":["H2 with Lindlar's catalyst","Na in liquid NH3","Excess H2 with Pt","H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","One equivalent of HBr"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Dissolving-metal reduction (Na or Li in liquid NH3) reduces an internal alkyne to the trans (E) alkene, because it proceeds through a trans vinyl radical/anion intermediate that places the two new hydrogens on opposite faces (anti addition). The trap is Lindlar's catalyst: H2/Lindlar also stops at the alkene, but it delivers both hydrogens to the same face (syn addition), giving the CIS (Z) alkene instead. Excess H2/Pt over-reduces all the way to butane; oxymercuration-type hydration (H2O/H2SO4/HgSO4) gives a ketone; and one equivalent of HBr gives a bromoalkene, not the desired trans alkene."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Fractions & decimals","stem":"Compute 2/3 - 1/4 + 1/6.","choices":["7/12","1/4","13/12","2/5","5/12"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Use the LCD of 12: 2/3 = 8/12, 1/4 = 3/12, 1/6 = 2/12, so 8/12 - 3/12 + 2/12 = 7/12. Choice 1/4 (=3/12) comes from subtracting the last term instead of adding (8-3-2). Choice 13/12 adds all three (8+3+2). Choice 2/5 is the beginner trap of combining across (2-1+1)/(3-4+6). Choice 5/12 drops the +1/6 entirely (8/12 - 3/12)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Algebra & equations","stem":"Solve for x: 2(3x - 4) = 4x + 6.","choices":["5","7","-1","14","1"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Distribute: 6x - 8 = 4x + 6, so 2x = 14 and x = 7. Choice 5 is the classic distribution trap: multiplying the 2 only onto 3x and not onto -4 (6x - 4 = 4x + 6 gives 2x = 10). Choice -1 is a sign error (2x = 6 - 8). Choice 14 forgets to divide by 2. Choice 1 combines the distribution and sign errors."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Ratios & proportions","stem":"At a shelter the ratio of cats to dogs is 3:5. If there are 24 animals in all, how many are dogs?","choices":["15","9","40","12","8"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The parts sum to 3 + 5 = 8, so each part is 24/8 = 3; dogs = 5 x 3 = 15. Choice 9 is the number of cats (3 x 3), the other group. Choice 40 inverts the ratio fraction (24 x 5/3). Choice 12 splits the total in half, ignoring the 3:5 ratio. Choice 8 is just the sum of the ratio parts, not a count of dogs."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Percentages","stem":"A shirt priced at $80 is marked 25% off, then an additional 10% is taken off the discounted price at checkout. What is the final price?","choices":["$54","$52","$60","$72","$45"],"correct":0,"explanation":"First discount: 80 x 0.75 = 60. Second discount on the new price: 60 x 0.90 = 54. Choice $52 is the trap of adding the discounts to 35% off (80 x 0.65) - successive percents do not add. Choice $60 applies only the first discount. Choice $72 applies only the 10%. Choice $45 misreads the percents as dollars (80 - 25 - 10)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Exponents & roots","stem":"Simplify (2x^3)^2 * x^4.","choices":["4x^10","2x^10","4x^9","4x^24","2x^24"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A power of a product: (2x^3)^2 = 2^2 * x^(3*2) = 4x^6; then times x^4 adds exponents: 4x^(6+4) = 4x^10. Choice 2x^10 forgets to square the coefficient 2. Choice 4x^9 adds the inner exponents instead of multiplying (3+2 = 5, then 5+4). Choice 4x^24 multiplies the final exponents (6 * 4) instead of adding them. Choice 2x^24 makes both the coefficient and the final-exponent errors."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Geometry (area/volume)","stem":"A circle has a diameter of 10 cm. What is its area? (Use pi = 3.14.)","choices":["78.5 cm^2","314 cm^2","31.4 cm^2","62.8 cm^2","15.7 cm^2"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The radius is d/2 = 5 cm, so area = pi*r^2 = 3.14 x 25 = 78.5 cm^2. Choice 314 is the trap of using the diameter (10) as the radius: 3.14 x 100. Choice 31.4 is the circumference pi*d, not the area. Choice 62.8 is 2*pi*d, mixing formulas. Choice 15.7 is pi*r (half the circumference)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Trigonometry","stem":"In right triangle ABC the right angle is at C. The side opposite angle A has length 3 and the hypotenuse AB has length 5. What is cos A?","choices":["4/5","3/5","3/4","5/4","4/3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The side adjacent to A is the other leg: sqrt(5^2 - 3^2) = 4. Cosine is adjacent/hypotenuse = 4/5. Choice 3/5 is sin A (opposite/hypotenuse) - the classic sin/cos mix-up. Choice 3/4 is tan A (opposite/adjacent). Choice 5/4 inverts cosine (hypotenuse/adjacent). Choice 4/3 is adjacent/opposite (cotangent).","figureSvg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 200 180\" width=\"200\" height=\"180\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Right triangle ABC with the right angle at C, side BC labeled 3 and hypotenuse AB labeled 5\"><polygon points=\"40,40 40,140 115,140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"40\" y=\"128\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"36\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">A</text><text x=\"26\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">C</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">B</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">3</text><text x=\"83\" y=\"88\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">5</text></svg>"},{"section":"qr","topic":"Probability","stem":"A bag holds 4 red marbles and 6 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","choices":["2/15","4/25","3/25","2/5","12/19"],"correct":0,"explanation":"P(both red) = (4/10)(3/9) = 12/90 = 2/15, since the second draw has one fewer red and one fewer total. Choice 4/25 is the with-replacement trap (4/10 x 4/10). Choice 3/25 decrements the numerator (4 to 3) but forgets to decrement the denominator (4/10 x 3/10). Choice 2/5 is only the first draw. Choice 12/19 multiplies numerators but adds denominators (4*3 over 10+9)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Statistics (mean/median/SD)","stem":"Find the median of the data set: 16, 4, 42, 15, 8, 23.","choices":["15.5","28.5","18","15","16"],"correct":0,"explanation":"First sort: 4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42. With an even count of 6, the median is the average of the two middle values, (15 + 16)/2 = 15.5. Choice 28.5 skips sorting and averages the two middle numbers of the original list, (42 + 15)/2. Choice 18 is the mean (108/6). Choices 15 and 16 take a single middle value instead of averaging the middle pair."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Word problems (rate/work/mixture)","stem":"Pipe A can fill a tank in 4 hours and pipe B can fill the same tank in 6 hours. If both pipes run together, how long will it take to fill the tank?","choices":["2.4 hours","5 hours","10 hours","2 hours","1.2 hours"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Add the rates: 1/4 + 1/6 = 5/12 tank per hour, so the time is 12/5 = 2.4 hours. Choice 5 is the beginner trap of averaging the two times, (4 + 6)/2. Choice 10 adds the times. Choice 2 subtracts them (6 - 4). Choice 1.2 correctly reaches 2.4 hours but then carelessly halves it (2.4/2). A quick sanity check: working together must be faster than the faster pipe alone (4 hours), so any answer of 4 hours or more is impossible."}]},{"n":5,"tier":5,"questions":[{"section":"bio","topic":"Biomolecules","stem":"A ribosome assembles a single polypeptide chain that is exactly 8 amino acids long. During this assembly, how many water molecules are released, and what type of covalent bond joins the residues?","choices":["8 water molecules released; peptide (amide) bonds","7 water molecules released; peptide (amide) bonds","7 water molecules released; phosphodiester bonds","7 water molecules released; glycosidic bonds","16 water molecules released; peptide (amide) bonds"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Linking n monomers by dehydration synthesis forms n-1 bonds and releases n-1 water molecules, so 8 amino acids give 7 peptide bonds and 7 waters. The bond between adjacent amino acids is a peptide (amide) bond formed between a carboxyl and an amino group. Choice A is the classic off-by-one error (counting one water per residue instead of per bond). Phosphodiester bonds join nucleotides and glycosidic bonds join sugars, so C and D pair the right water count with the wrong biomolecule class. E double-counts the waters."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cell structure & organelles","stem":"A gland cell manufactures a glycoprotein hormone that will be secreted from the cell. Which sequence correctly traces the protein from its site of synthesis to its release?","choices":["Rough ER -> Golgi (cis to trans face) -> secretory vesicle -> plasma membrane","Smooth ER -> Golgi (cis to trans face) -> secretory vesicle -> plasma membrane","Rough ER -> Golgi (trans to cis face) -> secretory vesicle -> plasma membrane","Free cytosolic ribosomes -> Golgi -> lysosome -> plasma membrane","Rough ER -> lysosome -> Golgi -> plasma membrane"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Secreted and membrane proteins are made on ribosomes of the rough ER, then transported to the Golgi, which they enter at the cis face and exit modified at the trans face, packaged into secretory vesicles that fuse with the plasma membrane. Choice B is the tempting half-right answer: the order is correct but the smooth ER lacks ribosomes and handles lipids/detoxification, not protein synthesis. C reverses Golgi polarity (proteins enter cis, leave trans). D uses free ribosomes (which make cytosolic proteins) and routes to a lysosome. E inserts a lysosome incorrectly before Golgi processing."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Membrane transport","stem":"In an intestinal epithelial cell, glucose is moved from the gut lumen into the cell against its concentration gradient. The transporter does not hydrolyze ATP itself; instead it moves glucose by coupling it to Na+ flowing down the gradient that the Na+/K+ ATPase maintains. This mechanism is best classified as:","choices":["Facilitated diffusion, because a carrier protein is used","Primary active transport, because it depends on the Na+/K+ ATPase","Secondary active transport, because it is powered by the Na+ gradient rather than direct ATP hydrolysis","Simple diffusion, because glucose is a small molecule","Receptor-mediated endocytosis, because the membrane engulfs glucose"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The glucose transporter moves glucose uphill without directly splitting ATP; it taps the potential energy stored in the Na+ gradient. Energy from a previously established ion gradient defines secondary (indirect) active transport. Choice B is the seductive half-right answer: the process does ultimately rely on the ATP-consuming Na+/K+ pump, but primary active transport requires the transporter itself to hydrolyze ATP, which this one does not. Facilitated diffusion and simple diffusion (A, D) only move solutes down their gradient, and endocytosis (E) engulfs bulk material in vesicles, not individual glucose molecules through a carrier."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cellular respiration","stem":"A yeast cell is suddenly deprived of oxygen. Within moments its glycolysis will grind to a halt unless it begins fermentation. What is the direct reason fermentation must occur for glycolysis to keep running?","choices":["To produce the majority of the cell's ATP directly","To regenerate NAD+ from NADH so glycolysis can continue","To regenerate FAD from FADH2 for the electron transport chain","To convert lactate back into pyruvate","To pump protons across the inner mitochondrial membrane"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Glycolysis requires a supply of oxidized NAD+ at its glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate step. Without O2 the electron transport chain cannot reoxidize NADH, so NAD+ runs out and glycolysis stalls. Fermentation restores NAD+ by transferring electrons from NADH onto pyruvate. Choice A is the classic trap: fermentation does allow continued (small) net ATP from glycolysis, but that is a consequence, not the reason glycolysis halts, which is NAD+ depletion. FAD (C) is not used in glycolysis, fermentation consumes pyruvate rather than making it from lactate (D), and proton pumping (E) is a function of the ETC, which is shut down."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Photosynthesis","stem":"A researcher adds an inhibitor that completely blocks electron flow in the thylakoid light reactions of a chloroplast. Once the small existing pools of ATP and NADPH are used up, the Calvin cycle stops. It stops primarily because it no longer receives:","choices":["CO2 to fix into organic molecules","O2 required to run carbon fixation","ATP and NADPH needed to fix and reduce carbon","NADPH only, since the Calvin cycle makes its own ATP","Water, which supplies electrons directly to the Calvin cycle"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The light reactions supply the Calvin cycle with both ATP (to drive carbon fixation and regeneration of RuBP) and NADPH (to reduce 3-PGA to G3P). Blocking the light reactions cuts off both, so the Calvin cycle halts. Choice D is the tempting half-right answer: NADPH is indeed lost, but the Calvin cycle does not generate its own ATP, so ATP is also cut off. CO2 (A) enters from the atmosphere independent of the light reactions, O2 (B) is a product not a requirement of carbon fixation, and water (E) is split in the light reactions, not consumed directly by the Calvin cycle."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Central dogma (DNA->RNA->protein)","stem":"The template (antisense) strand of a gene reads 3'-TACGGT-5'. Assuming standard transcription, what is the sequence of the mRNA produced?","choices":["5'-ATGCCA-3'","5'-UACGGU-3'","5'-AUGCCA-3'","5'-ACCGUA-3'","5'-TACGGT-3'"],"correct":2,"explanation":"mRNA is synthesized antiparallel and complementary to the template, with uracil replacing thymine. Pairing each template base (3'-T A C G G T-5') gives 5'-A U G C C A-3'. Choice A is the tempting half-right answer: the base pairing is correct, but it keeps thymine (writing a DNA strand) instead of substituting uracil, so it is not RNA. B simply swaps T for U on the template without complementing it. D is the correct bases written in the wrong (non-antiparallel) direction. E just copies the template strand unchanged."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mitosis & meiosis","stem":"An organism has a diploid chromosome number of 8 (2n = 8). One of its cells is undergoing meiosis and has reached metaphase II. In that single cell at this moment, how many chromosomes and how many total chromatids are present?","choices":["8 chromosomes; 16 chromatids","4 chromosomes; 8 chromatids","4 chromosomes; 4 chromatids","8 chromosomes; 8 chromatids","2 chromosomes; 4 chromatids"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Meiosis I is reductional: homologs separate, so each cell entering meiosis II is haploid with n = 4 chromosomes. Sister chromatids have not yet separated at metaphase II, so each of the 4 chromosomes still has 2 chromatids, giving 8 chromatids total. Choice A is the classic trap: 8 chromosomes and 16 chromatids describe metaphase I (before the reductional division), not metaphase II. Choice C (4 and 4) applies only after sister chromatids separate in anaphase II, when each chromatid becomes its own chromosome."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mendelian genetics","stem":"Two organisms heterozygous at two independently assorting genes are crossed (AaBb x AaBb). A is completely dominant to a, and B is completely dominant to b. What is the probability that an offspring shows the dominant phenotype for trait A but the recessive phenotype for trait B?","choices":["1/16","3/16","9/16","3/4","1/4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Handle each gene separately, then multiply. Aa x Aa gives 3/4 dominant (A_) for trait A; Bb x Bb gives 1/4 recessive (bb) for trait B. Because the genes assort independently, P(A_ and bb) = 3/4 x 1/4 = 3/16. Choice A (1/16) is the trap for students who treat trait A as also recessive (1/4 x 1/4). Choice C (9/16) is the probability of dominant for both traits, and 3/4 and 1/4 each address only one gene instead of combining them."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Evolution & population genetics","stem":"In a large, randomly mating population at Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, a trait shows simple recessive inheritance, and 16% of individuals display the recessive phenotype. What percentage of the population is expected to be heterozygous (carriers)?","choices":["16%","24%","36%","48%","60%"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The recessive phenotype frequency equals q^2 = 0.16, so q = 0.4 and p = 1 - 0.4 = 0.6. Heterozygotes are 2pq = 2(0.6)(0.4) = 0.48, or 48%. Choice B (24%) is the tempting half-right answer: it computes pq but forgets the factor of 2 that accounts for both heterozygous arrangements. Choice C (36%) is p^2, the homozygous dominant class, and A (16%) is the given q^2 recessive class, not the carriers."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Physiology (a body system)","stem":"In the human cardiovascular system, which sequence correctly traces a drop of blood from the right ventricle until it reaches the left atrium?","choices":["Right ventricle -> pulmonary artery -> pulmonary capillaries -> pulmonary vein -> left atrium","Right ventricle -> pulmonary vein -> pulmonary capillaries -> pulmonary artery -> left atrium","Right ventricle -> aorta -> systemic capillaries -> vena cava -> left atrium","Right atrium -> pulmonary artery -> lung capillaries -> pulmonary vein -> left ventricle","Right ventricle -> pulmonary artery -> pulmonary vein -> left atrium"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The right ventricle pumps deoxygenated blood into the pulmonary artery to the lung capillaries, where gas exchange occurs, and oxygenated blood returns via the pulmonary vein to the left atrium. Choice B is the tempting half-right answer: the organ sequence and gas-exchange step are correct, but the vessel names are swapped. Arteries carry blood away from the heart (here the pulmonary artery, despite carrying deoxygenated blood) and veins return it, so the vessel leaving the right ventricle must be the pulmonary artery. C follows the systemic circuit instead of the pulmonary one, D starts at the wrong chamber and ends at the wrong chamber, and E omits the pulmonary capillary bed where gas exchange must occur."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Moles & stoichiometry","stem":"For the reaction N2(g) + 3 H2(g) -> 2 NH3(g), a flask is charged with 14 g of N2 and 6.0 g of H2. Assuming the reaction goes to completion, what maximum mass of NH3 can form? (Molar masses: N2 = 28 g/mol, H2 = 2.0 g/mol, NH3 = 17 g/mol.)","choices":["8.5 g","17 g","34 g","51 g","68 g"],"correct":1,"explanation":"14 g N2 = 0.50 mol and 6.0 g H2 = 3.0 mol. Reacting 0.50 mol N2 requires only 1.5 mol H2, so H2 is in excess and N2 is limiting. By the 1:2 ratio, 0.50 mol N2 yields 1.0 mol NH3 = 17 g (B). Choice C (34 g) is the trap of picking H2 as limiting (3.0 mol H2 x 2/3 = 2.0 mol NH3). Choice A (8.5 g) forgets the coefficient of 2 and uses a 1:1 N2:NH3 ratio (0.50 mol NH3). D and E overcount moles of product."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Atomic structure & quantum numbers","stem":"Which of the following sets of quantum numbers (n, l, ml, ms) is NOT permitted for an electron?","choices":["(3, 2, -2, +1/2)","(2, 1, -1, -1/2)","(1, 0, 0, +1/2)","(2, 2, 0, +1/2)","(4, 3, -3, -1/2)"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The rule is l can range only from 0 to n-1. In choice D, n = 2 allows l = 0 or 1, so l = 2 is impossible (D is forbidden). Choice E (4, 3, -3, -1/2) is the tempting trap: it looks exotic (a 4f electron with ml = -3), but for n = 4, l = 3 is allowed and ml can run from -3 to +3, so it is valid. A (3d), B (2p), and C (1s) all satisfy l <= n-1 and -l <= ml <= +l."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Periodic trends","stem":"The species O2-, F-, Na+, and Mg2+ are all isoelectronic (each has 10 electrons). Which one has the SMALLEST ionic radius?","choices":["O2-","F-","Na+","Mg2+","They are all the same size because they are isoelectronic"],"correct":3,"explanation":"For an isoelectronic series, the ion with the greatest nuclear charge pulls the same 10 electrons in most tightly, giving the smallest radius. Proton counts are O = 8, F = 9, Na = 11, Mg = 12, so Mg2+ is smallest (D). Choice E is the half-right trap: students correctly recognize the ions are isoelectronic but wrongly conclude equal size, ignoring that Z differs. O2- (A) has the fewest protons and is actually the largest."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Bonding, Lewis & VSEPR","stem":"Chlorine trifluoride, ClF3, has a central Cl atom. Based on its Lewis structure and VSEPR theory, what is its molecular (not electron-domain) geometry? (Cl has 7 valence electrons.)","choices":["Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal","T-shaped","Bent","Trigonal bipyramidal"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Cl uses 3 of its 7 valence electrons in bonds to F, leaving 4 electrons = 2 lone pairs. That is 5 electron domains (3 bonding + 2 lone pairs), giving a trigonal-bipyramidal electron geometry. The two lone pairs occupy equatorial positions, leaving a T-shaped molecular geometry (C). Choice A (trigonal planar) is the trap of counting only the 3 bonds and ignoring the lone pairs. Choice E gives the electron-domain geometry, not the molecular shape."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Gas laws","stem":"A sample of ideal gas occupies 2.0 L at 300 K and 1.0 atm. The gas is then heated to 600 K while the pressure is raised to 2.0 atm. What is the new volume?","choices":["1.0 L","2.0 L","4.0 L","0.50 L","8.0 L"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Use the combined gas law: V2 = V1 x (T2/T1) x (P1/P2) = 2.0 L x (600/300) x (1.0/2.0) = 2.0 x 2 x 0.5 = 2.0 L (B). Doubling T alone would double the volume, but doubling P alone halves it; the two effects cancel, so the volume is unchanged. Choice C (4.0 L) applies only the temperature increase and forgets the pressure change. Choice A (1.0 L) applies only the pressure change."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Thermochemistry","stem":"Given: (1) C(s) + O2(g) -> CO2(g), delta H = -394 kJ and (2) CO(g) + 1/2 O2(g) -> CO2(g), delta H = -283 kJ. Using Hess's law, what is delta H for C(s) + 1/2 O2(g) -> CO(g)?","choices":["-677 kJ","-111 kJ","+111 kJ","-283 kJ","+677 kJ"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Take reaction (1) as written and reverse reaction (2), which flips its sign to +283 kJ. Adding: C + O2 -> CO2 (-394) plus CO2 -> CO + 1/2 O2 (+283) gives C + 1/2 O2 -> CO with delta H = -394 + 283 = -111 kJ (B). Choice A (-677) is the trap of adding the two given values without reversing reaction (2). Choice C (+111) gets the magnitude but keeps the wrong sign (fails to reverse)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Kinetics","stem":"A reaction has the experimentally determined rate law rate = k[A]^2[B]. If the concentration of A is doubled and, at the same time, the concentration of B is halved, by what factor does the initial rate change?","choices":["Increases by 2x","Increases by 4x","Increases by 8x","No change","Decreases by 2x"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Rate depends on (2)^2 for A and (1/2)^1 for B: factor = 2^2 x (1/2) = 4 x 0.5 = 2, so the rate increases by 2x (A). Choice B (4x) is the tempting error of accounting for the second-order effect of doubling A but forgetting that halving B cuts the rate in half. Choice D (no change) wrongly assumes the two changes cancel one-for-one, ignoring that A is squared."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Equilibrium","stem":"For N2(g) + 3 H2(g) <-> 2 NH3(g), delta H = -92 kJ (exothermic), which single change will shift the equilibrium toward more NH3?","choices":["Increase the temperature","Decrease the container volume (increase the pressure)","Add a catalyst","Increase the container volume","Remove some N2"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The reactant side has 4 mol of gas and the product side has 2 mol. Decreasing the volume (raising pressure) shifts equilibrium toward the side with fewer gas moles, i.e., toward NH3 (B). Choice A is the classic trap: raising temperature does speed up the reaction, but for an exothermic reaction it shifts equilibrium backward (left), lowering NH3. Choice D (larger volume) shifts toward more moles (reactants). A catalyst (C) changes rate but not position, and removing N2 (E) shifts left."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Acids, bases & pH","stem":"What is the approximate pH of a 0.10 M solution of a weak monoprotic acid HA with Ka = 1.0 x 10^-5? (Assume x is small compared to 0.10.)","choices":["1.0","2.0","3.0","5.0","6.0"],"correct":2,"explanation":"For a weak acid, [H+] is approximately sqrt(Ka x C) = sqrt((1.0 x 10^-5)(0.10)) = sqrt(1.0 x 10^-6) = 1.0 x 10^-3 M, so pH = 3.0 (C). Choice A (pH 1) is the major trap of treating HA as a strong acid, where [H+] would equal 0.10 M. Choice D (pH 5) wrongly sets pH = -log(Ka) = pKa, and choice E confuses the calculation with pKa-related values. The small Ka confirms only partial dissociation, so pH must be well above 1."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Electrochemistry","stem":"Given the standard reduction potentials Cu2+ + 2e- -> Cu, E = +0.34 V and Zn2+ + 2e- -> Zn, E = -0.76 V, what is the standard cell potential (E cell) for the spontaneous galvanic cell built from these two half-reactions?","choices":["+1.10 V","+0.42 V","-0.42 V","-1.10 V","0 V"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In a spontaneous galvanic cell, the half-reaction with the higher (more positive) reduction potential is the cathode. Here Cu2+/Cu (+0.34 V) is the cathode and Zn2+/Zn (-0.76 V) is the anode. E cell = E cathode - E anode = 0.34 - (-0.76) = +1.10 V (A), which is positive, confirming spontaneity. Choice D (-1.10 V) reverses the cathode/anode assignment. Choices B and C (+/-0.42 V) come from simply subtracting the two reduction potentials without correctly handling the double negative sign."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IUPAC nomenclature","stem":"Give the correct IUPAC name for the following compound: CH2=CH-CH2-CH2-CH(OH)-CH3","choices":["hex-1-en-5-ol","2-hydroxyhex-5-ene","hex-5-en-2-ol","hex-1-en-2-ol","hex-4-en-1-ol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Two rules must be combined. First, the -OH is the principal characteristic group, so it (not the C=C) must receive the lowest locant. Numbering from the alcohol end gives OH at C2 and the double bond at C5: hex-5-en-2-ol. 'hex-1-en-5-ol' is the classic trap of numbering to give the double bond the low locant, but the alcohol outranks the alkene for numbering priority. '2-hydroxyhex-5-ene' wrongly demotes the senior -OH to a 'hydroxy' substituent instead of the '-ol' suffix. The other two place the unsaturation or OH on the wrong carbons."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Stereochemistry (R/S, chirality)","stem":"The Fischer projection below shows a molecule with one stereocenter (the central carbon). Assign its configuration. Recall that in a Fischer projection the horizontal bonds point toward the viewer and the vertical bonds point away.","choices":["R, because H points toward the viewer, so the apparent counterclockwise 1->2->3 order must be reversed","S, because the order OH -> CHO -> CH2OH is read directly as counterclockwise","S, because CH2OH outranks CHO, giving a clockwise order that is then reversed","R, because H points away from the viewer, so no reversal is needed","The central carbon is not a stereocenter"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Priorities are OH(1) > CHO(2, the C=O oxygen is duplicated giving O,O,H) > CH2OH(3, only O,H,H) > H(4). Tracing 1->2->3 (right -> top -> bottom) is counterclockwise, which by itself reads S. But the lowest-priority H sits on a horizontal bond, which in a Fischer projection points TOWARD the viewer, so you must reverse the reading: the true configuration is R (this is D-glyceraldehyde). 'S' fails to reverse. The other 'S' misranks CH2OH above CHO. The second 'R' reaches the right letter with wrong logic: in a Fischer projection horizontal bonds point toward you, not away, so a reversal IS required.","figureSvg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 220 200\" width=\"220\" height=\"200\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Fischer projection\"><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"55\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"165\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"32\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"currentColor\">CHO</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"175\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"currentColor\">CH2OH</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"currentColor\">H</text><text x=\"175\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"currentColor\">OH</text></svg>"},{"section":"ochem","topic":"SN1/SN2/E1/E2","stem":"2-chloro-2-methylbutane is treated with a high concentration of sodium ethoxide (a strong, non-bulky base) in ethanol with heating. What is the dominant mechanism and the major organic product?","choices":["E2; 2-methylbut-1-ene","E2; 2-methylbut-2-ene","SN2; 2-ethoxy-2-methylbutane","SN1; 2-methylbut-2-ene","E1; 2-methylbut-2-ene"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Two decisions are linked. Mechanism: a tertiary substrate with a strong, high-concentration base and heat drives bimolecular elimination (E2). A 3-degree carbon cannot undergo SN2, and a strong base at high concentration favors E2 over the unimolecular SN1/E1 pathways. Regiochemistry: because the base is small (not bulky), the Zaitsev, more-substituted alkene 2-methylbut-2-ene predominates. 'E2; 2-methylbut-1-ene' is the tempting half-right answer: correct mechanism but the Hofmann product, which would only dominate with a bulky base such as tert-butoxide."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alkene addition reactions","stem":"3,3-dimethylbut-1-ene (CH2=CH-C(CH3)2-CH3) is treated with HBr. What is the major product?","choices":["2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane","3-bromo-2,2-dimethylbutane","1-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane","2,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol","1,2-dibromo-3,3-dimethylbutane"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Markovnikov protonation puts H on C1 and a secondary carbocation on C2. That cation sits next to a quaternary carbon, so a methyl group shifts to form a more stable tertiary carbocation; bromide then traps it, giving 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane. '3-bromo-2,2-dimethylbutane' is the tempting half-right answer: correct Markovnikov orientation but it ignores the carbocation rearrangement. '1-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane' is anti-Markovnikov (would require radical/peroxide conditions). The alcohol is a hydration product (wrong reagent), and the dibromide comes from Br2 (wrong reagent)."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Aromaticity & EAS","stem":"4-methylphenol (a benzene ring with -OH at C1 and -CH3 at C4) is nitrated. Where does the nitro group add, and why?","choices":["At C3 (ortho to -CH3), because the alkyl group is the stronger ortho/para-director","At C3 (meta to -OH), because -OH is deactivating and meta-directing","At C2 (ortho to -OH), because -OH is the stronger activating director","No reaction; two electron-donating groups make the ring unreactive","Equal amounts at every open position, since both groups are ortho/para-directors"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Both substituents are ortho/para-directors, so the tie is broken by strength. The -OH is a much stronger activator/director than an alkyl group, so it controls orientation, and because the para position to -OH is blocked by the -CH3, the open positions ortho to -OH (C2/C6) are favored. Choosing 'ortho to -CH3' is the trap of treating the weaker alkyl director as dominant. Calling -OH 'deactivating/meta-directing' is simply wrong (it is a strong activator). Two donors make the ring MORE reactive, not unreactive, and the large difference in director strength rules out an even mixture."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alcohols & carbonyls","stem":"Which reagent converts 1-butanol into butanal without over-oxidizing it to butanoic acid?","choices":["K2Cr2O7, H2SO4, H2O, heat","KMnO4 (warm), then H3O+","NaBH4 in methanol","PCC in CH2Cl2","H2, Pd/C"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Two ideas are linked: the oxidation ladder (primary alcohol -> aldehyde -> carboxylic acid) and reagent selectivity. Stopping at the aldehyde requires a mild, anhydrous oxidant, which is PCC. K2Cr2O7/H2SO4/H2O and KMnO4 are strong aqueous oxidants that carry a primary alcohol all the way to the carboxylic acid, so they are tempting (they DO oxidize) but overshoot the aldehyde. NaBH4 and H2/Pd are reducing conditions and do not oxidize an alcohol at all."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Acidity & pKa","stem":"Which of the following is the most acidic compound?","choices":["p-nitrophenol","phenol","p-methoxyphenol","cyclohexanol","ethanol"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Acidity tracks conjugate-base stability through two effects. First, resonance: a phenoxide delocalizes negative charge into the ring, so all three phenols are far more acidic than cyclohexanol and ethanol (which have no resonance stabilization). Second, substituent electronics: a para electron-withdrawing -NO2 further stabilizes the phenoxide by resonance, making p-nitrophenol the most acidic. 'phenol' is tempting if you stop after the resonance idea and ignore the substituent. 'p-methoxyphenol' is a trap in the opposite direction: -OCH3 donates electrons and destabilizes the anion, so it is actually less acidic than phenol."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Resonance & stability","stem":"Which of the following carbocations is the most stable?","choices":["ethyl cation, CH3CH2+","isopropyl cation, (CH3)2CH+","allyl cation, CH2=CH-CH2+","tert-butyl cation, (CH3)3C+","benzyl cation, C6H5-CH2+"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Resonance delocalization stabilizes a cation more strongly than hyperconjugation. The benzyl cation spreads its positive charge over the aromatic ring through several resonance contributors, making it the most stable. The tert-butyl cation is the tempting half-right answer: it is a 3-degree cation stabilized by hyperconjugation, which beats the simple alkyl cations but not resonance delocalization. The allyl cation is resonance-stabilized too, but only over two carbons (fewer contributors than benzyl). Isopropyl (2-degree) and ethyl (1-degree) are the least stable."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IR & H-NMR spectroscopy","stem":"A compound with molecular formula C3H6O shows a strong IR absorption near 1715 cm-1 and a single 1H NMR signal: a 6H singlet near 2.1 ppm. What is the compound?","choices":["propanal","propanone (acetone)","prop-2-en-1-ol (allyl alcohol)","propylene oxide (methyloxirane)","methoxyethene (methyl vinyl ether)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Combine the two data points. The strong ~1715 cm-1 band means a C=O is present, and a single 6H singlet near 2.1 ppm means all six hydrogens are equivalent methyl protons with no neighboring H's: two identical CH3 groups flanking a carbonyl, which is acetone. Propanal is the tempting trap because it has the same formula and also a C=O, but it would show an aldehyde proton near 9.7 ppm plus a quartet and a triplet (three signals), not one singlet. Allyl alcohol, propylene oxide, and methyl vinyl ether all lack the ~1715 cm-1 carbonyl band."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Synthesis / roadmap","stem":"Which reagent sequence best converts but-1-ene into butan-1-ol?","choices":["dilute H2SO4, H2O","1) Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2) NaBH4","1) BH3-THF; 2) H2O2, NaOH","1) HBr (no peroxide); 2) then aqueous base","1) Br2, H2O"],"correct":2,"explanation":"This requires recognizing the target as the anti-Markovnikov alcohol and then choosing the reagents that deliver it. Butan-1-ol has the OH on the terminal (less-substituted) carbon, which is the signature of hydroboration-oxidation: boron adds to the less-hindered carbon, so OH ends up on C1. Acid-catalyzed hydration and oxymercuration-demercuration are the tempting traps because they also 'add water,' but both follow Markovnikov and give butan-2-ol. The HBr route follows Markovnikov, so even after aqueous workup it leads to a C2 product (2-bromobutane, or butan-2-ol), not butan-1-ol; and Br2/H2O gives a bromohydrin, not the simple alcohol."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Fractions & decimals","stem":"Evaluate the expression (5/8 + 0.25) divided by (3/4).","choices":["7/6","21/32","1","6/7","7/8"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Convert 0.25 to 2/8, so 5/8 + 2/8 = 7/8. Dividing by 3/4 means multiplying by its reciprocal: 7/8 x 4/3 = 28/24 = 7/6. Key = 7/6. The 21/32 trap comes from multiplying by 3/4 instead of dividing (7/8 x 3/4). The 1 trap comes from misreading 0.25 as 1/8, giving 6/8 = 3/4, then 3/4 divided by 3/4 = 1. The 6/7 trap inverts the division (3/4 divided by 7/8). The 7/8 trap stops after adding and never divides at all."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Algebra & equations","stem":"The numbers x and y satisfy 3x + 2y = 18 and x - y = 1. What is the value of the product xy?","choices":["7","9","4","12","3"],"correct":3,"explanation":"From x - y = 1 we get x = y + 1. Substitute: 3(y + 1) + 2y = 18, so 5y + 3 = 18, giving 5y = 15, y = 3 and x = 4. The product xy = 4 x 3 = 12. Key = 12. The 7 trap adds x + y instead of multiplying. The 9 trap assumes x = y = 3 (ignoring that x - y = 1) and squares 3. The 4 trap reports x only; the 3 trap reports y only."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Ratios & proportions","stem":"A dough recipe uses flour to sugar in the ratio 5:3. A baker mixes a batch containing 20 cups of flour, then stirs in 4 extra cups of sugar. What is the new ratio of flour to sugar in simplest form?","choices":["5:3","5:4","5:7","4:5","3:2"],"correct":1,"explanation":"At 5:3 with 20 cups of flour, the scale factor is 20/5 = 4, so sugar starts at 3 x 4 = 12 cups. Adding 4 cups gives 16 cups of sugar. The new ratio is 20:16, which simplifies (divide by 4) to 5:4. Key = 5:4. The 5:3 trap forgets to add the extra sugar. The 5:7 trap adds 4 directly to the ratio number 3 without scaling. The 4:5 trap gives the right numbers but inverts the order. The 3:2 trap mistakenly adds 4 cups to both flour and sugar (24:16)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Percentages","stem":"A jacket is priced at $80. A store takes 25% off the price, and then adds a 10% sales tax to the discounted amount. What is the final price?","choices":["$52","$68","$60","$54","$66"],"correct":4,"explanation":"First apply the discount: 80 x 0.75 = $60. Then add 10% tax: 60 x 1.10 = $66. Key = $66. The $68 trap nets the percentages (25% - 10% = 15% off, 80 x 0.85) or equivalently applies both to the original price (80 - 20 + 8). The $52 trap combines them as a single 35% discount (80 x 0.65). The $60 trap forgets the tax entirely. The $54 trap takes 10% OFF the $60 instead of adding 10% tax (60 x 0.90)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Exponents & roots","stem":"Evaluate the square root of 50, divided by the square root of 2, then add 2 cubed.","choices":["13","11","33","21","18"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The square root of 50 divided by the square root of 2 equals the square root of (50/2) = square root of 25 = 5. Then 2 cubed = 8, so 5 + 8 = 13. Key = 13. The 11 trap computes 2 cubed as 2 x 3 = 6. The 33 trap drops the radicals and does 50/2 = 25, then adds 8. The 21 trap reads 2 cubed as 2 to the fourth power (16), giving 5 + 16. The 18 trap multiplies the radicals instead of dividing (square root of 100 = 10), then adds 8."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Geometry (area/volume)","stem":"A cube has a total surface area of 96 square centimeters. What is its volume?","choices":["16 cubic cm","4 cubic cm","64 cubic cm","96 cubic cm","24 cubic cm"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A cube has 6 identical square faces, so 6 s^2 = 96, giving s^2 = 16 and side s = 4 cm. Volume = s^3 = 4^3 = 64 cubic cm. Key = 64. The 16 trap stops at s^2 = 16 and mistakes that face area for the volume. The 4 trap reports the side length instead of the volume. The 96 trap mistakes the given surface area for the volume. The 24 trap multiplies the 6 faces by the side length (6 x 4), confusing the surface-area setup."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Trigonometry","stem":"A right triangle has legs of length 6 and 8. For the acute angle theta that lies opposite the leg of length 6, what is sin(theta) + cos(theta)?","choices":["0.75","1.2","0.2","1.4","0.48"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The hypotenuse is the square root of (36 + 64) = 10. For theta opposite the leg 6: sin(theta) = 6/10 = 0.6 and cos(theta) = 8/10 = 0.8. Their sum is 1.4. Key = 1.4. The 0.75 trap computes tan(theta) = 6/8 instead of sin + cos. The 1.2 trap uses 6/10 for both ratios (0.6 + 0.6), forgetting the adjacent side is 8. The 0.2 trap subtracts (0.8 - 0.6). The 0.48 trap multiplies sin x cos."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Probability","stem":"A bag contains 4 red, 3 blue, and 5 green marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both are green?","choices":["25/144","5/33","5/36","5/12","10/33"],"correct":1,"explanation":"There are 12 marbles. P(first green) = 5/12. After removing one green there are 4 green of 11 left, so P(second green) = 4/11. Multiply: (5/12)(4/11) = 20/132 = 5/33. Key = 5/33. The 25/144 trap treats the draws as with replacement (5/12 x 5/12). The 5/36 trap reduces the green count but keeps the denominator at 12 (5/12 x 4/12). The 5/12 trap gives only the first draw. The 10/33 trap doubles the answer by wrongly counting the two draw orders for an unordered outcome."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Statistics (mean/median/SD)","stem":"For the data set 4, 8, 8, 12, 18, what is the mean minus the median?","choices":["0","10","8","4","2"],"correct":4,"explanation":"The sum is 4 + 8 + 8 + 12 + 18 = 50, so the mean is 50/5 = 10. With five sorted values, the median is the middle (third) value, which is 8. Mean minus median = 10 - 8 = 2. Key = 2. The 0 trap wrongly averages the values on either side of center (8 and 12) to get a median of 10, using even-count logic on an odd set. The 10 trap reports the mean alone; the 8 trap reports the median (or the mode) alone. The 4 trap comes from an addition slip that inflates the sum to 60 (mean 12), giving 12 - 8."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Word problems (rate/work/mixture)","stem":"How many liters of a 20% acid solution must be added to 10 liters of a 50% acid solution to produce a mixture that is 30% acid?","choices":["30 liters","10 liters","20 liters","5 liters","15 liters"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Let x be the liters of 20% solution added. Acid balance: 0.20x + 0.50(10) = 0.30(x + 10). So 0.20x + 5 = 0.30x + 3, giving 2 = 0.10x, x = 20 liters. Key = 20. The 30 trap solves correctly but reports the final total volume (x + 10) instead of the amount added. The 10 trap assumes an equal 1:1 mix. The 5 trap inverts the alligation ratio (using 1:2 instead of 2:1). The 15 trap comes from loosely averaging the concentrations rather than balancing the acid."}]},{"n":6,"tier":6,"questions":[{"section":"bio","topic":"Biomolecules","stem":"A single linear polypeptide is assembled from 15 amino acids by dehydration synthesis. How many water molecules are released, and how many peptide bonds are formed?","choices":["15 water molecules; 15 peptide bonds","14 water molecules; 14 peptide bonds","14 water molecules; 15 peptide bonds","30 water molecules; 15 peptide bonds","7 water molecules; 14 peptide bonds"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Joining n monomers in a single chain forms n-1 bonds, and each dehydration synthesis releases exactly one water per bond. So 15 amino acids give 15-1 = 14 peptide bonds and 14 waters. Choice A (15/15) is the classic off-by-one trap of forgetting that the ends are not joined. C mismatches waters and bonds, which cannot happen since they are one-to-one. D double-counts water (two per bond), and E halves the count."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cell structure & organelles","stem":"A protein tagged for secretion out of the cell is followed from synthesis to release. Which sequence of structures does it pass through in the correct order?","choices":["Rough ER, then Golgi (cis to trans face), then secretory vesicle, then plasma membrane","Smooth ER, then Golgi (cis to trans face), then secretory vesicle, then plasma membrane","Rough ER, then Golgi (trans to cis face), then secretory vesicle, then plasma membrane","Free cytoplasmic ribosome, then Golgi, then lysosome, then plasma membrane","Rough ER, then secretory vesicle, then Golgi, then plasma membrane"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Secretory proteins are made on ribosomes of the rough ER, shuttled to the Golgi entering at the cis face and exiting at the trans face, packaged into a secretory vesicle, and released at the plasma membrane by exocytosis. B substitutes smooth ER, which handles lipids and detoxification, not protein synthesis. C reverses Golgi polarity (material enters cis, leaves trans). D routes to a lysosome (degradation), not secretion. E swaps the Golgi and vesicle order, so the protein would never be processed before packaging."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Membrane transport","stem":"A cell whose cytoplasm is 300 mOsm is placed in a solution that is 500 mOsm. Which statement correctly describes the solution, the direction of net water movement, and what happens to the cell?","choices":["The external solution is hypotonic; water enters the cell; the cell swells","The external solution is isotonic; there is no net water movement","The external solution is hypertonic; water leaves the cell; the cell shrinks","The external solution is hypertonic; water enters the cell; the cell swells","The external solution is hypotonic; water leaves the cell; the cell shrinks"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The outside (500 mOsm) has more solute than the inside (300 mOsm), so it is hypertonic. Water moves toward the higher solute concentration, meaning out of the cell, and the cell shrinks (crenation). D is the most tempting trap: it correctly labels the solution hypertonic but then sends water the wrong way; water always moves toward higher solute, not away from it. A and E reverse the tonicity label, and B ignores the 200 mOsm gradient."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cellular respiration","stem":"Starting from one molecule of glucose that is completely oxidized in aerobic respiration, how many molecules of CO2 are released in total across glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation, and the citric acid cycle?","choices":["6","2","4","3","12"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Glycolysis releases no CO2. Pyruvate oxidation releases 2 CO2 (one per pyruvate, and glucose yields 2 pyruvate). The citric acid cycle releases 2 CO2 per turn and runs twice, giving 4. Total = 0 + 2 + 4 = 6, which matches the 6 carbons in glucose. Choice B counts only pyruvate oxidation, C counts only the citric acid cycle, and D forgets that glucose produces two pyruvate (so both later stages run twice). E doubles the correct answer."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Photosynthesis","stem":"In the Calvin cycle, how many ATP and how many NADPH are consumed to produce the equivalent of one glucose molecule (a net of two G3P, requiring six CO2 to be fixed)?","choices":["9 ATP and 6 NADPH","6 ATP and 6 NADPH","12 ATP and 18 NADPH","18 ATP and 12 NADPH","36 ATP and 24 NADPH"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Fixing 3 CO2 makes one G3P using 9 ATP and 6 NADPH. A glucose equivalent needs two G3P (six CO2 fixed), so double those values: 18 ATP and 12 NADPH. Choice A (9 ATP, 6 NADPH) is the built-in trap of stopping at a single G3P instead of a full glucose. B uses one each per CO2, C swaps the two numbers, and E doubles the glucose values a second time."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Central dogma (DNA->RNA->protein)","stem":"A mature mRNA has an open reading frame that is 300 nucleotides long, measured from the start codon through the stop codon inclusive. How many amino acids are in the resulting polypeptide?","choices":["100","99","98","300","33"],"correct":1,"explanation":"300 nucleotides divided by 3 gives 100 codons. The start codon (AUG) codes for methionine and is counted, but the stop codon codes for no amino acid, so subtract one: 100 - 1 = 99 amino acids. Choice A (100) is the trap of forgetting the stop codon adds no residue. C wrongly removes both start and stop. D never divides by 3, and E divides 100 by 3 rather than 300."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mitosis & meiosis","stem":"An organism has a diploid number of 2n = 8. A cell from this organism is arrested at metaphase of meiosis II. How many chromosomes and how many chromatids does this cell contain?","choices":["4 chromosomes and 8 chromatids","8 chromosomes and 16 chromatids","8 chromosomes and 8 chromatids","4 chromosomes and 4 chromatids","16 chromosomes and 16 chromatids"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Meiosis I already reduced the chromosome number to haploid, so a meiosis II cell has n = 4 chromosomes. Sister chromatids do not separate until anaphase II, so at metaphase II each of the 4 chromosomes still has 2 chromatids, giving 8 chromatids. Choice B describes a mitotic metaphase (still diploid), C forgets the reduction of meiosis I, and D incorrectly assumes the sister chromatids have already split."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mendelian genetics","stem":"Two genes assort independently. In the cross AaBb x Aabb, what fraction of the offspring is expected to show the dominant trait for gene A and the recessive trait for gene B (genotype A_bb)?","choices":["3/16","9/16","3/8","1/4","1/2"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Split the dihybrid cross into two monohybrid crosses. Aa x Aa gives 3/4 dominant (A_). Bb x bb gives 1/2 Bb and 1/2 bb, so bb = 1/2. Multiply: 3/4 x 1/2 = 3/8, which is choice C. Choice A (3/16) is the trap of treating gene B as a Bb x Bb cross, which gives only 1/4 bb (3/4 x 1/4 = 3/16). Choice B (9/16) is the classic dihybrid ratio for two dominant traits, which does not apply here. Choices D (1/4) and E (1/2) each ignore one of the two genes."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Evolution & population genetics","stem":"A population is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium for a single gene with two alleles. Homozygous recessive individuals make up 0.16 of the population. What is the expected frequency of heterozygotes?","choices":["0.24","0.40","0.36","0.32","0.48"],"correct":4,"explanation":"The recessive homozygote frequency is q^2 = 0.16, so q = 0.4 and p = 1 - 0.4 = 0.6. Heterozygotes are 2pq = 2(0.6)(0.4) = 0.48. Choice A (0.24) forgets the factor of 2 in 2pq. B (0.40) mistakes the answer for the allele frequency q. C (0.36) reports p^2 (homozygous dominant). D (0.32) simply doubles q^2 instead of computing 2pq."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Physiology (cardiovascular system)","stem":"A patient has an end-diastolic volume of 120 mL, an end-systolic volume of 50 mL, and a heart rate of 70 beats per minute. What is the cardiac output in liters per minute?","choices":["8.4 L/min","4.9 L/min","4900 L/min","4.9 mL/min","0.049 L/min"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Stroke volume = end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume = 120 - 50 = 70 mL. Cardiac output = stroke volume x heart rate = 70 mL x 70 beats/min = 4900 mL/min = 4.9 L/min. Choice A (8.4) is the trap of using the end-diastolic volume (120 mL) directly instead of subtracting the end-systolic volume to get stroke volume. C is the unit-slip error of never converting mL to L. D and E are additional unit or decimal errors."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Moles & stoichiometry","stem":"Ammonia forms by the reaction N2(g) + 3 H2(g) -> 2 NH3(g). A flask is charged with 14 g of N2 and 6.0 g of H2. Assuming the reaction goes to completion, what maximum mass of NH3 can be produced? (Molar masses: N2 = 28 g/mol, H2 = 2.0 g/mol, NH3 = 17 g/mol.)","choices":["8.5 g","16 g","17 g","20 g","34 g"],"correct":2,"explanation":"14 g N2 = 0.50 mol; 6.0 g H2 = 3.0 mol. The 0.50 mol N2 needs only 1.5 mol H2 (3:1 ratio), so H2 is in excess and N2 is limiting. NH3 formed = 0.50 mol N2 x (2 NH3/1 N2) = 1.0 mol = 17 g. 34 g wrongly treats H2 as limiting (3 mol H2 x 2/3 = 2 mol NH3). 8.5 g drops the x2 mole ratio (0.50 mol NH3). 20 g is the total reactant mass. 16 g uses an incorrect molar mass of 16 for NH3."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Atomic structure & quantum numbers","stem":"In a ground-state atom, what is the maximum number of electrons that can have principal quantum number n = 4 and magnetic quantum number ml = +1?","choices":["2","6","8","10","32"],"correct":1,"explanation":"For n = 4, the allowed l values are 0, 1, 2, 3 (s, p, d, f). An orbital with ml = +1 exists only when l is at least 1, so l = 1, 2, and 3 each contribute exactly one orbital having ml = +1. That is 3 orbitals, and each holds 2 electrons, giving 6. Choosing 2 counts only a single orbital. 10 assumes only the d subshell. 32 is the total capacity of n = 4 (2n^2), ignoring the ml restriction. 8 miscounts the number of qualifying subshells."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Periodic trends","stem":"The species O2-, F-, Na+, Mg2+, and Al3+ are all isoelectronic (each has 10 electrons). Which one has the SMALLEST radius?","choices":["O2-","F-","Na+","Mg2+","Al3+"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Within an isoelectronic series the electron count is fixed, so radius is set by nuclear charge: the more protons pulling on the same 10 electrons, the smaller the ion. Al3+ has the highest nuclear charge (Z = 13), so it is smallest. F- is tempting because fluorine forms a small atom, but as an anion with Z = 9 it is actually the second-largest here. O2- (Z = 8) is the largest, not smallest. Na+ and Mg2+ have intermediate pull. Ranking: O2- > F- > Na+ > Mg2+ > Al3+."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Bonding, Lewis & VSEPR","stem":"What is the molecular geometry (shape) of ClF3? (Chlorine and fluorine are each in group 17 with 7 valence electrons.)","choices":["Trigonal planar","Trigonal pyramidal","T-shaped","Seesaw","Bent"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Total valence electrons = 7 (Cl) + 3 x 7 (F) = 28. Three Cl-F bonds use 6 electrons; the three F atoms take 18 as lone pairs; the remaining 4 electrons are two lone pairs on Cl. Chlorine therefore has 5 electron domains (3 bonding + 2 lone) = trigonal bipyramidal electron geometry. The two lone pairs occupy equatorial positions to minimize repulsion, leaving the three bonds in a T-shape. Trigonal planar ignores the lone pairs. Trigonal pyramidal assumes only one lone pair (like NH3). Seesaw is 5 domains with only one lone pair. Bent has too few bonds."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Gas laws","stem":"A sample of ideal gas occupies 2.0 L at 27 degrees C and 1.0 atm. What volume will it occupy at 127 degrees C and 2.0 atm?","choices":["0.75 L","1.33 L","2.67 L","4.7 L","5.33 L"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Use the combined gas law with Kelvin temperatures: T1 = 300 K, T2 = 400 K. V2 = V1 x (P1/P2) x (T2/T1) = 2.0 x (1.0/2.0) x (400/300) = 2.0 x 0.5 x 1.33 = 1.33 L. The classic trap, 4.7 L, uses Celsius (127/27) instead of Kelvin. 2.67 L forgets the pressure change. 0.75 L inverts the temperature ratio (300/400). 5.33 L inverts the pressure factor (multiplies by P2/P1 = 2 instead of 0.5)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Thermochemistry","stem":"Estimate the enthalpy change for H2(g) + Cl2(g) -> 2 HCl(g) using bond energies: H-H = 436 kJ/mol, Cl-Cl = 243 kJ/mol, H-Cl = 431 kJ/mol.","choices":["+248 kJ","+183 kJ","-183 kJ","-862 kJ","-1541 kJ"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Delta H = (bonds broken) - (bonds formed). Bonds broken = 436 + 243 = 679 kJ; bonds formed = 2 x 431 = 862 kJ. Delta H = 679 - 862 = -183 kJ (exothermic). +183 kJ reverses the sign by computing formed minus broken. +248 kJ forgets the coefficient 2 on HCl (679 - 431). -862 kJ counts only the bonds formed. -1541 kJ incorrectly sums all bond energies as released."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Kinetics","stem":"For A + B -> products, initial-rate data (rate in M/s) are: Exp 1: [A]=0.10, [B]=0.10, rate=2.0x10^-3; Exp 2: [A]=0.20, [B]=0.10, rate=8.0x10^-3; Exp 3: [A]=0.10, [B]=0.20, rate=2.0x10^-3. What is the rate constant k, with correct units?","choices":["0.02 s^-1","0.2 M^-1 s^-1","0.2 M^-2 s^-1","2 x 10^-3 M s^-1","2 M^-2 s^-1"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Comparing Exp 1 and 2, doubling [A] quadruples the rate, so the reaction is second order in A. Comparing Exp 1 and 3, doubling [B] leaves the rate unchanged, so it is zero order in B. Overall order = 2, so units of k are M^-1 s^-1. k = rate/[A]^2 = 2.0x10^-3 / (0.10)^2 = 0.2 M^-1 s^-1. 0.02 s^-1 wrongly treats A as first order. 2x10^-3 M s^-1 treats it as zero order (k = rate). 0.2 M^-2 s^-1 has the right number but third-order units. 2 M^-2 s^-1 mistakenly includes B as first order."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Equilibrium","stem":"For N2(g) + 3 H2(g) <-> 2 NH3(g), Delta H < 0. Which single change shifts the equilibrium to produce MORE NH3?","choices":["Increasing the temperature","Increasing the container volume","Adding a catalyst","Removing NH3 as it forms","Adding argon gas at constant volume"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Removing NH3 (a product) drives the system to replace it, shifting toward products. Increasing temperature adds heat to an exothermic reaction, which behaves like adding a product and shifts back toward reactants. Increasing the volume lowers pressure and favors the side with more moles of gas (4 mol reactants vs 2 mol product), so it shifts toward reactants. A catalyst speeds both directions equally and does not shift the position. Adding an inert gas at constant volume leaves all partial pressures unchanged, so there is no shift."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Acids, bases & pH","stem":"What is the pH of a 0.10 M solution of a weak monoprotic acid HA with Ka = 1.0 x 10^-5? (Assume x is small compared with 0.10.)","choices":["1.0","3.0","5.0","6.0","11.0"],"correct":1,"explanation":"For a weak acid, [H+] = sqrt(Ka x C) = sqrt(1.0x10^-5 x 0.10) = sqrt(1.0x10^-6) = 1.0x10^-3 M, so pH = 3.0. pH = 1.0 wrongly treats it as a strong acid ([H+] = 0.10). pH = 6.0 forgets to take the square root, using [H+] = Ka x C = 1.0x10^-6. pH = 5.0 mistakes Ka itself for [H+]. pH = 11.0 confuses this with pOH or a base."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Electrochemistry","stem":"A galvanic cell is built from these half-reactions: Cu2+ + 2 e- -> Cu, E° = +0.34 V; Ag+ + e- -> Ag, E° = +0.80 V. What is the standard cell potential E°cell?","choices":["-0.46 V","+0.46 V","+1.14 V","+1.26 V","+1.60 V"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The half-reaction with the higher reduction potential (Ag+/Ag, +0.80 V) is the cathode; Cu is the anode. E°cell = E°cathode - E°anode = 0.80 - 0.34 = +0.46 V (positive, so spontaneous). The overall reaction Cu + 2 Ag+ -> Cu2+ + 2 Ag requires doubling the silver half-reaction, but E° is an intensive property and is NOT multiplied by coefficients; doing so gives the +1.26 V trap (2 x 0.80 - 0.34). -0.46 V reverses cathode and anode. +1.14 V wrongly adds the two potentials. +1.60 V is simply 2 x 0.80."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IUPAC nomenclature","stem":"Give the correct IUPAC name for the compound CH3CH2-CH(CH2CH2CH3)-CH2CH3 (a central CH bonded to two ethyl groups and one propyl group).","choices":["3-propylpentane","4-ethylhexane","3-ethylhexane","3-ethylheptane","2-ethylhexane"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The longest continuous chain runs from one ethyl group, through the central CH, and out the propyl group: six carbons, so the parent is hexane and the remaining ethyl is the substituent. Numbering to give that ethyl the lowest locant puts it at C3, giving 3-ethylhexane. 3-propylpentane is the classic longest-chain trap: it picks a 5-carbon parent (ethyl-CH-ethyl) even though a 6-carbon chain exists. 4-ethylhexane numbers from the wrong end (locant 4 instead of 3). 3-ethylheptane miscounts the parent as 7 carbons, and 2-ethylhexane assigns the wrong locant."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Stereochemistry (R/S, chirality)","stem":"The stereocenter shown is the central carbon of butan-2-ol. OH and CH3 lie in the plane of the page, the H is on a bold wedge (pointing toward the viewer), and the ethyl group (CH2CH3) is on a hashed bond (pointing away from the viewer). What is the configuration of this carbon?","choices":["R, because the priority sequence 1 -> 2 -> 3 runs clockwise and is read directly.","R, because the priority sequence runs counterclockwise while H points away, so it is read directly.","The carbon is not a stereocenter; the molecule has a plane of symmetry.","S, because the sequence runs clockwise as drawn, but the lowest-priority H faces the viewer, so the assignment is reversed.","R, because CH3 and CH2CH3 are equal in priority, so only OH and H decide the sense."],"correct":3,"explanation":"Priorities are OH (1) > CH2CH3 (2) > CH3 (3) > H (4). Tracing 1 -> 2 -> 3 (OH on the right, ethyl down, methyl on the left) follows a clockwise path, which by itself reads as R. But the lowest-priority group, H, sits on the wedge toward the viewer, so the apparent sense must be reversed: the true configuration is S. Choice A is the trap of reading the rotation directly without reversing. It is chiral (four different groups), so the symmetry option is wrong, and ethyl outranks methyl at the first point of difference, so treating them as equal is also wrong.","figureSvg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 300 210\" width=\"300\" height=\"210\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Tetrahedral stereocenter with OH on a plain bond to the right, CH3 on a plain bond to the left, H on a bold wedge pointing up toward the viewer, and CH2CH3 on a hashed bond pointing down away from the viewer\"><g fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"105\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"105\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><polygon points=\"150,105 146,55 154,55\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"158\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,4\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"109\" font-family=\"Georgia, serif\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"end\" stroke=\"none\">CH3</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"109\" font-family=\"Georgia, serif\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"start\" stroke=\"none\">OH</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"48\" font-family=\"Georgia, serif\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" stroke=\"none\">H</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"176\" font-family=\"Georgia, serif\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" stroke=\"none\">CH2CH3</text></g></svg>"},{"section":"ochem","topic":"SN1/SN2/E1/E2","stem":"2-Bromobutane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOCH2CH3) in ethanol. What is the major product and mechanism?","choices":["1-Butene, via E2 (Hofmann product)","2-Butene (mainly the trans isomer), via E2 (Zaitsev product)","2-Ethoxybutane, via SN2","2-Ethoxybutane, via SN1","2-Butanol, via SN1 with the solvent"],"correct":1,"explanation":"2-Bromobutane is a secondary halide and ethoxide is a strong base; with heat the dominant pathway is E2 elimination. By Zaitsev's rule the more-substituted alkene forms preferentially, so 2-butene (mostly the more stable trans isomer) is the major product, not the terminal 1-butene, which is the Hofmann product favored only by bulky bases. SN2 to the ether does compete on a secondary carbon but stays minor under strong-base, heated conditions. SN1 routes are disfavored because a strong base drives E2/SN2 rather than a carbocation pathway."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alkene addition reactions","stem":"3-Methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr (no peroxides present). What is the major product?","choices":["2-Bromo-3-methylbutane (Markovnikov addition with no rearrangement)","1-Bromo-3-methylbutane (anti-Markovnikov addition)","1-Bromo-2-methylbutane","No reaction occurs without peroxides","2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (Markovnikov addition with a 1,2-hydride shift)"],"correct":4,"explanation":"HBr with no peroxides adds by Markovnikov (ionic) addition. Protonating the terminal carbon gives a secondary carbocation at C2 that sits next to a carbon bearing an H; a 1,2-hydride shift converts it to the more stable tertiary cation, and bromide then traps that carbon to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. 2-Bromo-3-methylbutane is the Markovnikov product you get only if you ignore the rearrangement. 1-Bromo-3-methylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov product, which requires peroxide/radical conditions. Concluding 'no reaction' confuses ionic HBr addition with radical HBr chemistry."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Aromaticity & EAS","stem":"Which of the following species is aromatic?","choices":["Cyclopentadienyl cation","Cyclooctatetraene","Cyclopentadienyl anion","Cycloheptatrienyl (tropylium) anion","Cyclobutadiene"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Aromaticity requires a planar, fully conjugated ring with 4n+2 pi electrons (Huckel's rule). The cyclopentadienyl anion has 6 pi electrons (n=1) and is aromatic. The cyclopentadienyl cation has only 4 pi electrons and is antiaromatic - the charge-sign trap. Cyclooctatetraene has 8 pi electrons and is tub-shaped (nonplanar), so it is nonaromatic. The tropylium system is aromatic as the cation (6 pi electrons); the anion shown here has 8 pi electrons and is antiaromatic. Cyclobutadiene has 4 pi electrons and is antiaromatic."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alcohols & carbonyls","stem":"What is the major product when 1-propanol is treated with PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in dichloromethane?","choices":["Propanoic acid","Propene","Propan-2-ol","Propanal","No reaction; PCC oxidizes only secondary alcohols"],"correct":3,"explanation":"PCC is a mild, anhydrous oxidant that stops a primary alcohol at the aldehyde, so 1-propanol gives propanal. Propanoic acid is the over-oxidation trap - that requires a strong aqueous oxidant such as Jones reagent (CrO3/H2SO4) or KMnO4. PCC does not dehydrate (so not propene) or isomerize (so not propan-2-ol), and it readily oxidizes primary alcohols, so 'no reaction' is wrong."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Acidity & pKa","stem":"Which of the following is the strongest acid?","choices":["Chloroacetic acid (ClCH2COOH)","Acetic acid (CH3COOH)","Phenol (C6H5OH)","2-Chloroethanol (ClCH2CH2OH)","Ethanol (CH3CH2OH)"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Lower pKa means the stronger acid. Chloroacetic acid (pKa about 2.9) is stronger than acetic acid (about 4.8) because the electron-withdrawing chlorine inductively stabilizes the carboxylate. Acetic acid is the trap for anyone who stops at 'it is the carboxylic acid' without weighing the inductive effect. Phenol (about 10) is far weaker than any carboxylic acid. 2-Chloroethanol (about 14) has a chlorine but is still an alcohol, and ethanol (about 16) is weakest. So chloroacetic acid is the strongest."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Resonance & stability","stem":"Which of the following carbocations is the most stable?","choices":["Cyclohexyl cation (secondary)","sec-Butyl cation (secondary)","Propyl cation (primary)","Isobutyl cation (primary)","Benzyl cation (C6H5CH2+)"],"correct":4,"explanation":"The benzyl cation is stabilized by resonance that delocalizes the positive charge into the aromatic ring (three additional resonance contributors), and this outweighs simple substitution effects. Even though its cationic carbon is primary, it is more stable than the secondary cyclohexyl and sec-butyl cations, which rely only on hyperconjugation and induction. The trap is to pick a secondary cation because 'more substituted equals more stable,' forgetting that resonance delocalization is the stronger stabilizing effect. The propyl and isobutyl cations are primary and least stable."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IR & H-NMR spectroscopy","stem":"A compound with molecular formula C3H6O shows a strong IR absorption near 1715 cm-1, and its 1H NMR spectrum is a single peak (one signal). Which compound is it?","choices":["Propanal","Acetone (propan-2-one)","Methyloxirane (propylene oxide)","Prop-2-en-1-ol (allyl alcohol)","Methyl vinyl ether"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The 1715 cm-1 band signals a C=O, so the compound is a carbonyl. A single 1H NMR signal means every hydrogen is equivalent, which fits acetone (six equivalent methyl protons). Propanal is the trap: it has the same formula and the same C=O band, but it gives three different signals plus a characteristic aldehyde proton near 9.7 ppm, so it is not a singlet. Methyloxirane, allyl alcohol, and methyl vinyl ether have no carbonyl at all, so none would show a strong 1715 cm-1 absorption."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Synthesis / roadmap","stem":"Which reagent sequence best converts 1-butene into 1-butanol?","choices":["H2O, H2SO4 (acid-catalyzed hydration)","Hg(OAc)2, H2O; then NaBH4 (oxymercuration-demercuration)","BH3 in THF; then H2O2, NaOH (hydroboration-oxidation)","HBr; then aqueous NaOH","Cold, dilute KMnO4"],"correct":2,"explanation":"1-Butanol is the anti-Markovnikov (terminal) alcohol, so hydroboration-oxidation (BH3 in THF, then H2O2/NaOH) is the route: boron adds to the less-hindered terminal carbon and OH ends up there. Acid-catalyzed hydration and oxymercuration-demercuration both follow Markovnikov and give 2-butanol - the main trap. HBr then hydroxide also proceeds through the Markovnikov intermediate 2-bromobutane, giving 2-butanol. Cold dilute KMnO4 makes the vicinal diol (butane-1,2-diol), not a single alcohol."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Fractions & decimals","stem":"Evaluate: 0.4 + (3/5)(2/3)","choices":["0.8","0.67","1.0","0.40","0.27"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Order of operations: do the multiplication first. (3/5)(2/3) = 6/15 = 2/5 = 0.4, then 0.4 + 0.4 = 0.8. Choice 0.67 comes from adding left-to-right first ((0.4 + 3/5) x 2/3 = 1.0 x 2/3). Choice 1.0 adds 0.4 + 3/5 and ignores the x 2/3. Choice 0.40 computes the product 2/5 but forgets to add the leading 0.4. Choice 0.27 multiplies 0.4 x 2/3 with wrong grouping."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Algebra & equations","stem":"Solve for x: 3(2x - 4) = 2(x + 6) - 4","choices":["x = 7","x = 5","x = 3","x = 6","x = 2.5"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Left side: 6x - 12. Right side: 2x + 12 - 4 = 2x + 8. So 6x - 12 = 2x + 8, giving 4x = 20, x = 5. Choice 7 treats the -4 on the right as +4 (2x + 16). Choice 3 mis-distributes the left as 6x - 4 (only the first term times 3). Choice 6 drops the -4 on the right entirely (2x + 12). Choice 2.5 collects the x-terms with a sign error, getting 8x = 20."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Ratios & proportions","stem":"A map is drawn at a scale of 1 cm : 6 km. Two towns are 6 cm apart on the map. A car drives between them at 72 km/h. How many minutes does the drive take?","choices":["0.5 minutes","5 minutes","30 minutes","36 minutes","12 minutes"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Real distance = 6 cm x 6 km/cm = 36 km. Time = 36 km / 72 km/h = 0.5 h = 30 minutes. Choice 0.5 is the classic unit slip: it stops at 0.5 hour and reports it as minutes. Choice 5 forgets the scale (6 / 72 h = 5 min). Choice 36 reports the distance in km as if it were the time in minutes. Choice 12 divides the speed by the map length (72 / 6)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Percentages","stem":"A stock priced at $50 rises 20% one day, then falls 20% the next day. What is its final price?","choices":["$50","$60","$40","$48","$52"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Apply the factors in sequence: 50 x 1.20 = 60, then 60 x 0.80 = 48. The percentages act on different bases, so they do not cancel. Choice 50 is the classic trap of assuming +20% then -20% returns to the start. Choice 60 applies only the increase; choice 40 applies only the decrease. Choice 52 treats the net as a +4% gain (50 x 1.04) instead of the actual 4% loss - a sign error."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Exponents & roots","stem":"Simplify: sqrt(50) + sqrt(18) - sqrt(8)","choices":["2*sqrt(15)","10*sqrt(2)","4*sqrt(2)","8*sqrt(2)","6*sqrt(2)"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Reduce each radical: sqrt(50) = 5*sqrt(2), sqrt(18) = 3*sqrt(2), sqrt(8) = 2*sqrt(2). Then 5 + 3 - 2 = 6, so the result is 6*sqrt(2). Choice 10*sqrt(2) treats the minus as a plus (5 + 3 + 2). Choice 2*sqrt(15) illegally combines under one radical: sqrt(50 + 18 - 8) = sqrt(60). Choice 4*sqrt(2) mis-simplifies sqrt(8) as 4*sqrt(2) (giving 5 + 3 - 4). Choice 8*sqrt(2) drops the -sqrt(8) term entirely."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Geometry (area/volume)","stem":"A right circular cylinder has a diameter of 6 cm and a height of 10 cm. What is its volume?","choices":["90*pi cm^3","360*pi cm^3","78*pi cm^3","60*pi cm^3","30*pi cm^3"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Volume = pi*r^2*h. The diameter is 6, so the radius is 3: pi(3^2)(10) = 90*pi cm^3. Choice 360*pi is the classic trap of using the diameter (6) as the radius. Choice 78*pi computes total surface area (2*pi*r^2 + 2*pi*r*h = 18*pi + 60*pi). Choice 60*pi computes lateral surface area (2*pi*r*h). Choice 30*pi forgets to square the radius (pi*r*h)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Trigonometry","stem":"A 10 m ladder leans against a wall, making a 60 degree angle with the level ground. How high up the wall does the top of the ladder reach?","choices":["≈ 11.5 m","≈ 8.7 m","≈ 17.3 m","5.0 m","≈ 7.1 m"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The height is opposite the 60 degree angle, so height = 10*sin(60) = 10*(sqrt(3)/2) = 5*sqrt(3) ≈ 8.7 m. Choice 5.0 uses cos(60) = 0.5, which gives the horizontal distance from the wall, not the height. Choice 17.3 uses tan(60) = sqrt(3) (10*sqrt(3)). Choice 7.1 mistakenly uses the 45 degree sine value (5*sqrt(2)). Choice 11.5 divides instead of multiplies (10 / sin(60))."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Probability","stem":"A bag holds 4 red and 6 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?","choices":["2/15","4/25","11/15","4/27","2/5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Draws are dependent: P(both red) = (4/10)(3/9) = 12/90 = 2/15. Choice 4/25 wrongly assumes replacement (4/10 x 4/10 = 16/100). Choice 11/15 adds the probabilities instead of multiplying (4/10 + 3/9). Choice 4/27 uses 9 as the total for both draws (4/9 x 3/9). Choice 2/5 only finds P(first red) = 4/10 and ignores the second draw."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Statistics (mean/median/SD)","stem":"Find the median of the data set: 4, 8, 8, 10, 15, 21","choices":["11","8","10","17","9"],"correct":4,"explanation":"With 6 values already in order, the median is the average of the 3rd and 4th values: (8 + 10)/2 = 9. Choice 11 computes the mean instead (66/6 = 11) - the most common trap. Choice 8 reports the mode (or just the lower middle value). Choice 10 takes only the upper middle value and forgets to average the two middles. Choice 17 reports the range (21 - 4)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Word problems (rate/work/mixture)","stem":"How many liters of a 20% acid solution must be added to 10 L of a 50% acid solution to produce a 30% acid solution?","choices":["5 L","50 L","10 L","30 L","20 L"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Let x = liters of 20% solution. Acid balance: 0.20x + 0.50(10) = 0.30(x + 10). So 0.2x + 5 = 0.3x + 3, giving 0.1x = 2, x = 20 L. Choice 5 L flips the alligation ratio (using (30-20):(50-30) instead of (50-30):(30-20)). Choice 50 L forgets to add the new volume on the right side (0.2x + 5 = 0.3x). Choice 10 L assumes equal volumes are needed. Choice 30 L reports the final total volume instead of the amount added."}]},{"n":7,"tier":7,"questions":[{"section":"bio","topic":"Biomolecules","stem":"Which statement correctly pairs a class of biological macromolecule with the covalent bond that links its monomers during dehydration synthesis?","choices":["Proteins are polymers of amino acids joined by phosphodiester bonds.","Polysaccharides are formed when monosaccharides link through glycosidic bonds.","Nucleic acids are chains of nucleotides connected by peptide bonds.","Triglycerides are assembled by joining fatty acids to glycerol through glycosidic bonds.","Proteins and polysaccharides both use ester linkages between their monomers."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Dehydration synthesis links monomers with class-specific bonds: proteins use peptide bonds, nucleic acids use phosphodiester bonds, polysaccharides use glycosidic bonds, and triglycerides use ester bonds. B is the only correct pairing. A swaps in the nucleic-acid bond for proteins; C swaps in the protein bond for nucleic acids; D uses glycosidic instead of the correct ester linkage for fats; E is wrong for both classes named."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cell structure & organelles","stem":"A pancreatic cell synthesizes a digestive enzyme that will be secreted from the cell. Starting at the ribosome, which sequence of structures does this protein pass through before leaving the cell?","choices":["Free ribosome in cytosol, then smooth ER, then lysosome, then plasma membrane","Rough ER, then cis Golgi, then trans Golgi, then secretory vesicle, then plasma membrane","Rough ER, then trans Golgi, then cis Golgi, then secretory vesicle, then plasma membrane","Nucleus, then smooth ER, then rough ER, then Golgi, then plasma membrane","Rough ER, then Golgi, then lysosome, then plasma membrane"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Secreted proteins are made on ribosomes bound to the rough ER, enter the Golgi at the cis face, are processed toward the trans face, packaged into secretory vesicles, and released by exocytosis. C reverses Golgi polarity (proteins enter cis, exit trans). A and D route the protein through the smooth ER, which handles lipid synthesis and detox, not export of proteins. E sends it to a lysosome, which is for intracellular digestion, not secretion."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Membrane transport","stem":"Intestinal epithelial cells absorb glucose from the gut lumen even when the luminal glucose concentration is lower than the concentration inside the cell. This uptake stops when the Na+/K+ ATPase is inhibited. Which mechanism best explains the glucose uptake?","choices":["Simple diffusion of glucose down its concentration gradient","Facilitated diffusion through a GLUT carrier down glucose's gradient","Primary active transport in which ATP directly phosphorylates the glucose carrier","Secondary active transport powered by the inward Na+ gradient that the pump maintains","Osmosis, which carries glucose into the cell along with water"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Glucose is moving against its own gradient, so energy is required, but the glucose carrier itself does not hydrolyze ATP. Instead it cotransports glucose with Na+ moving down the steep inward Na+ gradient established by the Na+/K+ ATPase; this is secondary active transport. Inhibiting the pump collapses that Na+ gradient, so uptake stops. A and B describe movement down the gradient, which contradicts uptake against the gradient. C describes primary active transport, but the glucose carrier is not directly ATP-driven. E confuses solute transport with the movement of water."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cellular respiration","stem":"During intense exercise, a human muscle cell's oxygen supply becomes limiting, yet glycolysis continues and even speeds up. Which statement best explains how lactate fermentation allows glycolysis to keep producing ATP?","choices":["Fermentation produces large amounts of ATP directly from lactate.","Fermentation regenerates NAD+ so that glycolysis can keep oxidizing glucose.","Lactate enters the electron transport chain and serves as the final electron acceptor.","Fermentation supplies oxygen to the mitochondria so respiration can proceed.","Lactate is converted to pyruvate so the citric acid cycle can run without oxygen."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Without O2 the electron transport chain stalls and NADH cannot be re-oxidized there. Lactate fermentation transfers electrons from NADH to pyruvate, regenerating NAD+, which glycolysis needs to keep running and making its net 2 ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation. A is wrong: fermentation makes no ATP itself; the ATP comes from glycolysis. C is wrong: O2, not lactate, is the final electron acceptor of the ETC. D is wrong: fermentation does not generate O2. E is wrong: the citric acid cycle ultimately depends on O2 to reoxidize its carriers and does not run anaerobically."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Photosynthesis","stem":"In a chloroplast, which statement correctly connects the light-dependent reactions with the Calvin cycle and identifies the source of the O2 that is released?","choices":["The light reactions fix CO2 into sugar, and the released O2 comes from that CO2.","The Calvin cycle requires light directly and produces the ATP used by the light reactions.","The light reactions supply ATP and NADPH to the Calvin cycle, and the released O2 comes from splitting water.","The Calvin cycle supplies ATP and NADPH to the light reactions, and the released O2 comes from CO2.","Both the light reactions and the Calvin cycle split water to release O2."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The light-dependent reactions split water (the source of O2) and produce ATP and NADPH, which then power the Calvin cycle's fixation of CO2 into sugar. A wrongly places carbon fixation in the light reactions and sources O2 from CO2. B and D reverse the direction of energy flow (ATP and NADPH flow from the light reactions to the Calvin cycle) and wrongly source O2 from CO2. E is wrong because only the light reactions, at photosystem II, split water."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Central dogma (DNA->RNA->protein)","stem":"Within the coding region of a gene, a single nucleotide is inserted shortly after the start codon. Compared with a single-nucleotide substitution at the same position, what is the most likely consequence of this insertion?","choices":["It changes only one codon, altering at most a single amino acid.","It shifts the reading frame, altering most of the codons downstream of the insertion.","It has no effect because the ribosome simply skips the inserted base.","It converts one codon into a stop codon but leaves the reading frame intact.","It alters transcription but leaves the encoded amino acid sequence unchanged."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Inserting a single nucleotide (not a multiple of three) shifts the reading frame, so every codon downstream of the insertion is misread. This frameshift is usually far more disruptive than a substitution. A describes a substitution (a missense change), not an insertion. C is wrong because ribosomes do not skip bases to preserve the frame. D describes a nonsense point mutation, which changes a codon without shifting the frame. E is wrong because the change is within the coding sequence and does alter the protein."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mitosis & meiosis","stem":"A diploid cell with 2n = 8 enters meiosis. Immediately after meiosis I is complete, how many chromosomes are present in each daughter cell, and are the sister chromatids still joined?","choices":["8 chromosomes; sister chromatids have separated","4 chromosomes; sister chromatids are still joined","8 chromosomes; sister chromatids are still joined","4 chromosomes; sister chromatids have separated","2 chromosomes; sister chromatids are still joined"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes, reducing the count from 8 to the haploid number of 4, but each chromosome still consists of two sister chromatids joined at the centromere; sisters do not separate until meiosis II. A keeps the diploid number and separates sisters, which describes a later or different event. C fails to reduce the chromosome number at all. D correctly halves the count but wrongly separates the sisters (that happens in meiosis II). E over-reduces the chromosome number."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mendelian genetics","stem":"In pea plants, tall (T) is dominant to short (t) and purple (P) is dominant to white (p), and the two genes assort independently. In the cross TtPp x Ttpp, what fraction of the offspring are expected to be tall AND white?","choices":["9/16","3/8","1/4","3/16","1/2"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Treat each gene separately and multiply. Tt x Tt gives 3/4 tall. Pp x pp gives 1/2 purple and 1/2 white, so the probability of white is 1/2. Combined: 3/4 x 1/2 = 3/8. A (9/16) assumes a full dihybrid cross (TtPp x TtPp) for two dominant traits. C (1/4) accounts for only one gene. D (3/16) uses 3/4 x 1/4, treating white as if it came from a Pp x Pp cross. E ignores the probability of being tall."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Evolution & population genetics","stem":"In a large population at Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, a recessive condition (genotype aa) is seen in 16% of individuals. What fraction of the population is expected to be heterozygous carriers (Aa)?","choices":["0.16","0.40","0.48","0.36","0.24"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The recessive phenotype frequency is q^2 = 0.16, so q = 0.4 and p = 1 - 0.4 = 0.6. Heterozygotes are 2pq = 2(0.6)(0.4) = 0.48. A confuses the carrier frequency with the recessive-phenotype frequency (q^2). B reports q, the recessive allele frequency, not a genotype frequency. D reports p^2, the homozygous dominant frequency. E reports pq (half of 2pq), a common error of forgetting the factor of 2."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Physiology (circulatory system)","stem":"In the human circulatory system, which vessel carries oxygen-poor (deoxygenated) blood, and where does that blood travel next?","choices":["Pulmonary vein; it carries deoxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium","Pulmonary artery; it carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs","Aorta; it carries deoxygenated blood from the left ventricle to the body","Pulmonary artery; it carries oxygenated blood from the left ventricle to the body","Pulmonary vein; it carries oxygenated blood from the right atrium to the lungs"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The pulmonary artery is the exception to the rule that arteries carry oxygenated blood: it carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs, where gas exchange oxygenates it. A is wrong because the pulmonary vein carries oxygenated blood (lungs to left atrium). C is wrong because the aorta carries oxygenated blood to the body. D mislabels both the oxygen state and the origin and destination of the pulmonary artery. E mislabels the pulmonary vein's oxygen state and route."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Moles & stoichiometry","stem":"Aluminum burns in oxygen: 4 Al + 3 O2 -> 2 Al2O3. A reaction vessel is charged with 5.4 g of Al (27 g/mol) and 6.4 g of O2 (32 g/mol). What maximum mass of Al2O3 (102 g/mol) can form?","choices":["10.2 g","13.6 g","20.4 g","11.8 g","15.3 g"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Moles: Al = 5.4/27 = 0.20 mol; O2 = 6.4/32 = 0.20 mol. The 4:3 ratio means 0.20 mol Al needs only 0.20 x 3/4 = 0.15 mol O2, so O2 is in excess and Al is limiting. Al2O3 = 0.20 x (2/4) = 0.10 mol x 102 = 10.2 g. 13.6 g wrongly treats O2 as limiting (0.20 x 2/3 x 102). 20.4 g uses a 1:1 Al-to-Al2O3 ratio, ignoring the 4:2 coefficients. 11.8 g just sums the reactant masses (mass is conserved for the whole system, not for a single product). 15.3 g uses the 0.15 mol of O2 consumed as if it were moles of product."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Atomic structure & quantum numbers","stem":"A ground-state arsenic atom has 33 electrons. How many of its electrons occupy orbitals with the azimuthal (angular momentum) quantum number l = 1?","choices":["15","3","12","6","25"],"correct":0,"explanation":"l = 1 designates p orbitals. The configuration is 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10 4p3, so the p electrons are 2p6 + 3p6 + 4p3 = 15. Choice 3 counts only the valence 4p3. Choice 12 counts 2p6 + 3p6 but forgets the partially filled 4p3. Choice 6 counts just one filled p subshell. Choice 25 mistakenly adds the 3d10 electrons, but d orbitals have l = 2, not l = 1."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Periodic trends","stem":"The ions O2-, F-, Na+, Mg2+, and Al3+ are isoelectronic (each has 10 electrons). Which one has the smallest ionic radius?","choices":["Al3+","O2-","Mg2+","F-","Na+"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In an isoelectronic series every ion has the same electron count, so radius is set by nuclear charge: more protons pull the 10 electrons in tighter. Al3+ has 13 protons, the most of the group, giving the smallest radius; O2- has only 8 protons and is the largest. Picking O2- reflects the common error of assuming the most negative charge means smallest. Mg2+ (12 protons) and Na+ (11) are smaller than F- but larger than Al3+. F- (9 protons) is second largest."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Bonding, Lewis & VSEPR","stem":"Xenon tetrafluoride, XeF4, is formed from Xe (8 valence electrons) bonded to four F atoms. Which description of its molecular geometry and polarity is correct?","choices":["Square planar and nonpolar","Tetrahedral and nonpolar","See-saw and polar","Square pyramidal and polar","Octahedral and nonpolar"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Xe has 4 bonding pairs plus 2 lone pairs (AX4E2), giving octahedral electron geometry. The two lone pairs sit trans (axial), leaving the four F atoms in a square plane. The equal, opposed Xe-F dipoles cancel, so the molecule is nonpolar. Tetrahedral/nonpolar ignores both lone pairs (treats it as AX4). See-saw/polar is the SF4 pattern (AX4E1, one lone pair). Square pyramidal/polar is the BrF5 pattern (AX5E1). Octahedral/nonpolar counts the lone pairs as if they were bonds (AX6)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Gas laws","stem":"A fixed amount of ideal gas is at 2.00 atm and 300 K. The gas is then heated to 600 K while its volume is simultaneously reduced to half its original value. What is the new pressure?","choices":["8.00 atm","4.00 atm","2.00 atm","16.0 atm","0.500 atm"],"correct":0,"explanation":"With n constant, P1V1/T1 = P2V2/T2, so P2 = P1 x (V1/V2) x (T2/T1) = 2.00 x 2 x 2 = 8.00 atm. 4.00 atm applies only one factor of 2 (either the volume change or the temperature change alone). 2.00 atm inverts the temperature ratio (uses 300/600), letting the two effects cancel. 16.0 atm assumes halving the volume quadruples the pressure (x4 instead of x2). 0.500 atm inverts both ratios."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Thermochemistry","stem":"Given standard enthalpies of formation: CH4(g) = -75 kJ/mol, CO2(g) = -394 kJ/mol, H2O(l) = -286 kJ/mol, and O2(g) = 0, calculate delta-H for: CH4(g) + 2 O2(g) -> CO2(g) + 2 H2O(l).","choices":["-891 kJ","+891 kJ","-605 kJ","-966 kJ","-1041 kJ"],"correct":0,"explanation":"delta-H = sum(products) - sum(reactants) = [(-394) + 2(-286)] - [(-75) + 0] = (-966) - (-75) = -891 kJ. +891 kJ reverses the formula (reactants minus products). -605 kJ forgets the coefficient 2 on water (-394 - 286 + 75). -966 kJ leaves out the CH4 reactant term entirely (never subtracts -75). -1041 kJ adds the CH4 term instead of subtracting it (-966 - 75)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Kinetics","stem":"For the reaction A + B -> products, the experimentally determined rate law is rate = k[A]^2[B]. If [A] is doubled and [B] is halved at the same time, the rate changes by what factor?","choices":["Increases by a factor of 2","Increases by a factor of 4","No change (factor of 1)","Increases by a factor of 8","Decreases to one half"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Rate depends on [A] squared and [B] to the first power. Doubling [A] multiplies the rate by 2^2 = 4; halving [B] multiplies it by 1/2. Net = 4 x 1/2 = 2, so the rate doubles. Factor of 4 ignores the change in [B]. No change treats [A] as first order (x2) so it cancels the x1/2 from B. Factor of 8 applies the overall order of 3 as if every concentration were doubled (2^3). One half uses [A] as first order (x2) but squares B's halving (x1/4)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Equilibrium","stem":"For N2O4(g) <=> 2 NO2(g), Kc = 4.0 at a certain temperature. A mixture is prepared with [N2O4] = 0.10 M and [NO2] = 0.20 M. In which direction will the reaction proceed to reach equilibrium?","choices":["Q = 0.40, so the reaction proceeds forward toward products","Q = 0.40, so the reaction proceeds in reverse toward reactants","Q = 2.5, so the reaction proceeds toward reactants","Q = 2.0, so the reaction proceeds forward","Q = 4.0, so the system is already at equilibrium"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Q = [NO2]^2 / [N2O4] = (0.20)^2 / 0.10 = 0.040 / 0.10 = 0.40. Since Q (0.40) < K (4.0), the numerator (products) must grow, so the reaction shifts forward toward NO2. Choice 2 computes Q correctly but reverses the comparison rule. Choice 3 inverts the equilibrium expression (reactant over product squared = 0.10/0.04 = 2.5). Choice 4 forgets to square [NO2] (0.20/0.10 = 2.0). Choice 5 assumes Q must equal K without calculating."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Acids, bases & pH","stem":"A 0.10 M aqueous solution of a weak monoprotic acid HA has Ka = 1.0 x 10^-5. Assuming x is small relative to 0.10 M, what is the pH of the solution?","choices":["3.0","5.0","1.0","6.0","2.5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"For a weak acid, [H+] = x = sqrt(Ka x C) = sqrt((1.0e-5)(0.10)) = sqrt(1.0e-6) = 1.0e-3 M, so pH = -log(1.0e-3) = 3.0. pH 5.0 sets [H+] = Ka (i.e., pH = -log Ka), skipping the equilibrium. pH 1.0 treats HA as a strong acid with [H+] = 0.10 M. pH 6.0 forgets the square root and uses [H+] = Ka x C = 1.0e-6. pH 2.5 takes the square root of Ka alone (sqrt(1.0e-5) = 3.2e-3), omitting the concentration."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Electrochemistry","stem":"Standard reduction potentials: Cu2+ + 2e- -> Cu, E = +0.34 V; Zn2+ + 2e- -> Zn, E = -0.76 V. For the galvanic cell Zn | Zn2+ || Cu2+ | Cu, what is the standard cell potential?","choices":["+1.10 V","-1.10 V","-0.42 V","+2.20 V","+0.42 V"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Copper (higher reduction potential) is the cathode and zinc is the anode. E(cell) = E(cathode) - E(anode) = 0.34 - (-0.76) = +1.10 V, positive as required for a spontaneous galvanic cell. -1.10 V swaps the electrodes (treats Zn as cathode). -0.42 V adds the two reduction potentials (0.34 + (-0.76)). +2.20 V multiplies E by the number of electrons (n = 2); standard potential is intensive and is never multiplied by n. +0.42 V subtracts the magnitudes only (0.76 - 0.34), losing the sign logic."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IUPAC nomenclature","stem":"What is the correct IUPAC name for CH2=CH-CH2-CH(OH)-CH3?","choices":["pent-1-en-4-ol","2-hydroxypent-4-ene","pent-4-en-2-ol","pent-3-en-2-ol","pent-4-en-1-ol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The parent is a 5-carbon chain (pent-) containing both the C=C and the OH. The -ol is the principal characteristic group, so it must receive the lowest locant, not the double bond. Numbering from the CH3 end puts OH at C2 and the double bond at C4-C5: pent-4-en-2-ol. Choice 'pent-1-en-4-ol' is the classic error of giving the double bond the lowest locant; the suffix -ol outranks unsaturation for numbering. '2-hydroxypent-4-ene' wrongly demotes OH to a prefix substituent when it should be the suffix. 'pent-3-en-2-ol' miscounts the double-bond position, and 'pent-4-en-1-ol' misplaces OH on the terminal carbon."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Stereochemistry (R/S, chirality)","stem":"Which of the following compounds is a meso compound (achiral despite containing stereocenters)?","choices":["(2R,3R)-2,3-dibromobutane","(2R,3S)-2,3-dibromobutane","(2S,3S)-2,3-dibromobutane","(2R,3S)-2,3-dibromopentane","(2R,3R)-2,3-dibromopentane"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A meso compound needs stereocenters plus an internal mirror plane. In 2,3-dibromobutane the two ends are identical (CH3 on each side), so the (2R,3S) diastereomer has a mirror plane between C2 and C3 and is achiral (meso). The tempting trap is (2R,3S)-2,3-dibromopentane: it also has one R and one S center, but the ends differ (a methyl on one side, an ethyl on the other), so there is no internal mirror plane and it is a chiral diastereomer, not meso. (2R,3R)- and (2S,3S)-2,3-dibromobutane are the chiral enantiomeric pair, and (2R,3R)-dibromopentane is likewise chiral."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"SN1/SN2/E1/E2","stem":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane, (CH3)2CBr-CH2CH3, is heated with sodium ethoxide in ethanol. What is the major organic product?","choices":["2-ethoxy-2-methylbutane","2-methylbut-1-ene","2-methylbutan-2-ol","2-methylbut-2-ene","(E)-pent-2-ene"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A tertiary alkyl halide with a strong, small, negatively charged base (ethoxide) undergoes E2 elimination, and because the base is not bulky the Zaitsev (more-substituted) alkene dominates. Removing a beta-H from the ethyl group (the CH2) gives the trisubstituted 2-methylbut-2-ene, (CH3)2C=CHCH3. '2-methylbut-1-ene' is the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene, which would prevail only with a bulky base such as tert-butoxide. '2-ethoxy-2-methylbutane' is the substitution product, disfavored because a tertiary center blocks SN2 and a strong base pushes toward elimination. '2-methylbutan-2-ol' assumes water as the nucleophile, and '(E)-pent-2-ene' has the wrong carbon skeleton (it lacks the methyl branch present in the substrate)."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alkene addition reactions","stem":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with BH3.THF, followed by H2O2 and NaOH. What is the major product?","choices":["1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol","cis-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol","trans-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol","2-methylcyclohexan-1-one","1-methylcyclohexane-1,2-diol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov and syn. Boron (and ultimately OH) adds to the less substituted alkene carbon (C2), while H adds to the methyl-bearing carbon (C1) on the same face. Because H and OH add syn, the H on C1 ends up cis to the OH on C2, which forces the methyl on C1 to be trans to the OH, giving trans-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol. 'cis-2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol' has the correct anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry but ignores the required syn stereochemistry. '1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol' is the Markovnikov product you would get from acid hydration or oxymercuration. The ketone and the diol come from misreading the workup as an oxidation or the reagent as OsO4."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Aromaticity & EAS","stem":"p-Nitrotoluene (1-methyl-4-nitrobenzene) is treated with Br2 and FeBr3. Where does bromination occur predominantly?","choices":["At the carbon ortho to the nitro group and meta to the methyl group","At the carbon para to the methyl group (the position bearing the nitro group)","No substitution occurs because the nitro group deactivates the ring completely","At the carbon ortho to the methyl group and meta to the nitro group","At no preferred position; a statistical mixture of all open sites forms"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The methyl group is an activating ortho/para director and controls the orientation because an activator is the stronger director; the nitro group is a deactivating meta director. Methyl directs to its ortho carbons (C2/C6); the nitro group at C4 directs meta to itself, which is also C2/C6. The two effects reinforce at the position that is ortho to methyl and meta to nitro. Choosing 'ortho to nitro/meta to methyl' (C3/C5) wrongly follows the nitro group or treats it as an ortho director. 'Para to methyl' is already occupied by the nitro group. The ring is deactivated overall but still reacts, so 'no substitution' is wrong."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alcohols & carbonyls","stem":"1-Hexanol is treated with PCC to give X, and separately with hot concentrated KMnO4 to give Y. What are X and Y?","choices":["X = hexanoic acid; Y = hexanal","X = hexanal; Y = hexanoic acid","X = hexanal; Y = hexanal","X = hexan-2-one; Y = hexanoic acid","X = hex-1-ene; Y = hexanoic acid"],"correct":1,"explanation":"PCC is a mild, anhydrous oxidant that stops a primary alcohol at the aldehyde (X = hexanal). Hot, concentrated KMnO4 is a strong oxidant that carries a primary alcohol all the way to the carboxylic acid (Y = hexanoic acid). The reversed pairing swaps the reagent strengths. 'Hexanal/hexanal' forgets that hot KMnO4 over-oxidizes. A ketone is impossible because a primary alcohol oxidizes at the terminal carbon, and hex-1-ene would require dehydration, not oxidation."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Acidity & pKa","stem":"Which of the following carboxylic acids is the strongest acid?","choices":["CH3COOH (acetic acid)","ClCH2COOH (chloroacetic acid)","FCH2COOH (fluoroacetic acid)","Cl2CHCOOH (dichloroacetic acid)","Cl3CCOOH (trichloroacetic acid)"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Electron-withdrawing halogens stabilize the carboxylate by induction, and the effect is cumulative. Three chlorines in trichloroacetic acid (pKa ~0.7) withdraw the most electron density and give the most stabilized conjugate base, so it is the strongest acid (lowest pKa). The tempting distractor is fluoroacetic acid (pKa ~2.6), chosen because fluorine is the most electronegative single atom; however, one fluorine cannot outweigh three chlorines acting together. Acetic acid (pKa ~4.76), with no halogen, is the weakest of the set."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Resonance & stability","stem":"Which of the following carbocations is the most stable?","choices":["CH3CH2+ (ethyl cation)","CH2=CH-CH2+ (allyl cation)","CH3-O-CH2+ (methoxymethyl cation)","(CH3)2CH+ (isopropyl cation)","(CH3)3C+ (tert-butyl cation)"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The methoxymethyl cation is stabilized by donation of an oxygen lone pair, giving a resonance structure (CH3-O+=CH2) in which every atom has a complete octet. Lone-pair (resonance) donation from an adjacent heteroatom is stronger than the hyperconjugation that stabilizes an alkyl cation, so this primary-looking cation is actually the most stable (its hydride-ion affinity is lower than that of tert-butyl). The trap is the tert-butyl cation, picked by ranking only on degree of substitution; hyperconjugation from three methyls cannot match full octet stabilization. The allyl cation is resonance-stabilized but only spreads charge between two carbons without completing an octet at the cationic center."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IR & H-NMR spectroscopy","stem":"A compound with molecular formula C3H6O shows a strong IR absorption near 1715 cm-1 and a single peak (singlet) in its 1H NMR spectrum. Which compound is it?","choices":["Propanone (acetone)","Propanal","Prop-2-en-1-ol (allyl alcohol)","Methyl vinyl ether","Oxetane"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The strong band near 1715 cm-1 is a C=O stretch, which requires a carbonyl compound and rules out allyl alcohol (broad O-H, no carbonyl), methyl vinyl ether, and oxetane (both ethers, no carbonyl). A single 1H NMR singlet means all six hydrogens are equivalent, which fits the symmetric ketone acetone (two identical CH3 groups). Propanal also has a carbonyl near this frequency but would show three inequivalent signals, including the diagnostic aldehyde proton near 9.7 ppm, so it cannot be a singlet."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Synthesis / roadmap","stem":"Which reagent sequence best converts propene (CH2=CH-CH3) into propan-1-ol?","choices":["catalytic H2SO4, H2O","Hg(OAc)2, H2O; then NaBH4","HBr with peroxides (ROOR)","HBr (no peroxide); then aqueous NaOH","BH3.THF; then H2O2, NaOH"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Propan-1-ol is the anti-Markovnikov hydration product, which requires hydroboration-oxidation: BH3.THF then H2O2/NaOH places OH on the terminal (less substituted) carbon. Acid-catalyzed hydration and oxymercuration-demercuration both follow Markovnikov's rule and give propan-2-ol. The subtle trap is 'HBr with peroxides': it does give anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry, but the product is 1-bromopropane, the wrong functional group, not the alcohol. 'HBr (no peroxide) then NaOH' adds Markovnikov to give 2-bromopropane, leading to propan-2-ol."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Fractions & decimals","stem":"Evaluate (3/4 − 0.2) × 2.5 and express the result as a fraction.","choices":["11/20","11/8","5/8","61/20","55/4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Convert to a common form: 3/4 = 0.75, so 0.75 − 0.2 = 0.55 = 11/20. Then 11/20 × 2.5 = 11/20 × 5/2 = 55/40 = 11/8 (1.375). The trap 11/20 stops after the subtraction and forgets the ×2.5. 5/8 comes from misreading 0.2 as 1/2, giving 0.75 − 0.5 = 0.25, then ×2.5 = 0.625. 61/20 (3.05) adds 2.5 instead of multiplying. 55/4 (13.75) multiplies by 25 instead of 2.5, misplacing the decimal."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Algebra & equations","stem":"If x + y = 10 and x² − y² = 40, what is the value of x?","choices":["4","6","7","3","5"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Factor the difference of squares: x² − y² = (x + y)(x − y) = 40. Since x + y = 10, we get x − y = 40/10 = 4. Solving x + y = 10 and x − y = 4 gives x = (10 + 4)/2 = 7 (and y = 3). Choice 4 stops at the intermediate value x − y. Choice 3 is y, not x. Choice 5 halves the sum while ignoring the second equation. Choice 6 comes from x = sum − diff = 10 − 4 instead of averaging (10 + 4)/2."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Ratios & proportions","stem":"In a blend, A : B = 2 : 3 and B : C = 4 : 5. If the total amount of A, B, and C is 70 grams, how many grams of C are there?","choices":["35","24","30","16","15"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Make B common: A : B = 2 : 3 = 8 : 12 and B : C = 4 : 5 = 12 : 15, so A : B : C = 8 : 12 : 15 (sum 35 parts). With total 70, each part = 2, so C = 15 × 2 = 30. Choice 35 comes from failing to align B and using 2 : 3 : 5 (10 parts, C = 5 × 7). Choice 24 is B and choice 16 is A. Choice 15 is C's ratio number with the scale factor of 2 never applied."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Percentages","stem":"An item priced at $80 is increased by 20%, and then that new price is decreased by 25%. What is the final price?","choices":["76","96","72","60","120"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Apply the changes multiplicatively: 80 × 1.20 = 96, then 96 × 0.75 = 72. Choice 76 treats the changes as additive (+20% − 25% = −5%, giving 80 × 0.95). Choice 96 applies only the increase and forgets the decrease. Choice 60 applies only the 25% decrease to the original. Choice 120 mistakes the second change for another increase (96 × 1.25)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Exponents & roots","stem":"Evaluate (27^(2/3) × 4^(1/2)) ÷ 16^(1/4).","choices":["18","6","9","4.5","36"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Compute each: 27^(2/3) = (∛27)² = 3² = 9; 4^(1/2) = 2; 16^(1/4) = 2. So (9 × 2) ÷ 2 = 9. Choice 18 forgets to divide by the denominator (or reads 27^(2/3) as 27 × 2/3 = 18). Choice 6 takes 27^(2/3) as (∛27) × 2 = 6, multiplying the root by the power instead of raising it. Choice 4.5 reads 16^(1/4) as 16 ÷ 4 = 4, giving 18/4. Choice 36 multiplies by the denominator instead of dividing (18 × 2)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Geometry (area/volume)","stem":"A solid consists of a right circular cylinder of radius 3 and height 10 with a solid hemispherical cap of radius 3 on top. What is its total volume?","choices":["126π","108π","90π","96π","99π"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Cylinder volume = πr²h = π(9)(10) = 90π. Hemisphere volume = (1/2)(4/3)πr³ = (2/3)π(27) = 18π. Total = 90π + 18π = 108π. Choice 126π uses a full sphere (36π) instead of a hemisphere. Choice 90π omits the cap entirely. Choice 96π uses r² instead of r³ in the hemisphere ((2/3)π·9 = 6π). Choice 99π uses (1/3)πr³ = 9π for the cap.","figureSvg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 190\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M20 70 A40 40 0 0 1 100 70\"/><ellipse cx=\"60\" cy=\"70\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"12\"/><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"155\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"155\"/><path d=\"M20 155 A40 12 0 0 0 100 155\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"70\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/><text x=\"72\" y=\"66\" fill=\"#888\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\">r = 3</text><text x=\"108\" y=\"116\" fill=\"#888\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"11\">h = 10</text></svg>"},{"section":"qr","topic":"Trigonometry","stem":"For an acute angle θ in a right triangle, sin θ = 3/5. What is the value of 2 sin θ cos θ?","choices":["7/5","12/25","24/25","1","3/2"],"correct":2,"explanation":"With the 3-4-5 triangle, sin θ = 3/5 and cos θ = 4/5. Then 2 sin θ cos θ = 2 × (3/5)(4/5) = 24/25. Choice 7/5 computes sin θ + cos θ instead. Choice 12/25 drops the factor of 2 (just sin θ cos θ). Choice 1 confuses the expression with the identity sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. Choice 3/2 uses tan θ = 3/4 and doubles it (2 × 3/4)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Probability","stem":"A bag contains 4 red and 6 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both are the same color?","choices":["8/15","7/15","13/25","1/3","2/15"],"correct":1,"explanation":"P(both red) = (4/10)(3/9) = 12/90 and P(both blue) = (6/10)(5/9) = 30/90. Same color = 42/90 = 7/15. Choice 8/15 = 48/90 is P(different colors), the complement. Choice 13/25 treats the draws as with replacement: (2/5)² + (3/5)² = 4/25 + 9/25. Choice 1/3 counts only both blue (30/90). Choice 2/15 counts only both red (12/90)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Statistics (mean/median/SD)","stem":"The mean of five numbers is 20. Four of the numbers are 14, 16, 22, and 30. What is the median of all five numbers?","choices":["20","19","18","22","16"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Total = 20 × 5 = 100, and the four known values sum to 82, so the fifth number is 18. Ordered: 14, 16, 18, 22, 30, and the middle value is 18. Choice 20 mistakes the mean for the median. Choice 19 takes the median of only the four given numbers, (16 + 22)/2, forgetting to include the fifth. Choice 22 fails to reorder after inserting 18 (14, 16, 22, 30, 18) and picks the literal middle. Choice 16 uses a count of 4 instead of 5 when finding the missing value (total 80 → fifth = −2 → median 16)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Word problems (rate/work/mixture)","stem":"Working together, painters A and B can paint a room in 3 hours. Painter A working alone would take 5 hours. How long would painter B take to paint the room alone?","choices":["8 hours","7.5 hours","2 hours","4 hours","1.875 hours"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Rates add: combined rate = 1/3 room per hour, A's rate = 1/5. So B's rate = 1/3 − 1/5 = 5/15 − 3/15 = 2/15, meaning B alone takes 15/2 = 7.5 hours. Choice 8 adds the two given times (5 + 3). Choice 2 subtracts the times (5 − 3). Choice 4 averages the times ((5 + 3)/2). Choice 1.875 = 15/8 adds the rates (1/3 + 1/5 = 8/15) instead of subtracting them."}]},{"n":8,"tier":8,"questions":[{"section":"bio","topic":"Biomolecules","stem":"One molecule of glycerol reacts with three fatty acid molecules to form a triglyceride. How many water molecules are released, and what type of covalent bond joins the glycerol to each fatty acid?","choices":["3 water molecules; ester bonds","3 water molecules; peptide bonds","2 water molecules; ester bonds","4 water molecules; ester bonds","3 water molecules; glycosidic bonds"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each of glycerol's three hydroxyl groups condenses with the carboxyl group of one fatty acid by dehydration synthesis, forming three ester bonds and releasing three water molecules. Peptide bonds (B) join amino acids and glycosidic bonds (E) join sugars, so those name the wrong linkage. Two waters (C) forgets that all three hydroxyls react; four waters (D) over-counts, since only three condensation reactions occur."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cell structure & organelles","stem":"A hepatocyte is specialized for synthesizing lipids and for detoxifying lipophilic drugs. Given that it must do BOTH, which organelle would be most abundant in this cell?","choices":["Rough endoplasmic reticulum","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Peroxisomes","Golgi apparatus","Lysosomes"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The smooth ER is the site of lipid (phospholipid and steroid) synthesis and also carries the cytochrome P450 enzymes that detoxify drugs, so it uniquely satisfies both listed functions. Peroxisomes (C) are tempting because they do neutralize some toxins and break down fatty acids, but they are not the primary site of lipid synthesis. Rough ER (A) makes proteins, the Golgi (D) modifies and packages, and lysosomes (E) digest material, so none covers both roles."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Membrane transport","stem":"An intestinal epithelial cell absorbs glucose from the gut lumen even when the luminal glucose concentration is lower than inside the cell. This uphill uptake is powered by the simultaneous inward movement of Na+ down its electrochemical gradient. This glucose transport is best classified as:","choices":["Simple diffusion","Facilitated diffusion","Primary active transport","Secondary active transport","Exocytosis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Glucose is moving against its own gradient, so energy is required, which rules out simple and facilitated diffusion (both only go down-gradient). The energy comes from the Na+ gradient rather than direct ATP hydrolysis at the transporter, making this secondary active transport (a Na+/glucose symport). Primary active transport (C) is the trap: it also uses energy, but 'primary' specifically means the transporter itself hydrolyzes ATP, whereas here ATP is spent elsewhere to maintain the Na+ gradient. Exocytosis (E) moves bulk material in vesicles, not single solutes."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cellular respiration","stem":"A researcher adds a compound that makes the inner mitochondrial membrane freely permeable to protons (an uncoupler). With oxygen and substrates still plentiful, what is the immediate effect on the electron transport chain and on ATP synthesis?","choices":["Electron transport stops; ATP synthesis stops","Electron transport continues or speeds up; ATP synthesis stops","Electron transport stops; ATP synthesis continues","Both electron transport and ATP synthesis continue normally","Electron transport slows; ATP synthesis increases"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An uncoupler lets protons leak back across the membrane without passing through ATP synthase, so the proton-motive force collapses and chemiosmotic ATP synthesis halts. Because the gradient never builds up to oppose the pumps, the electron transport chain keeps running (often faster), dissipating its energy as heat. A is wrong because the ETC does not itself require an intact gradient to pass electrons to oxygen; only ATP synthase depends on the gradient. C and D incorrectly keep ATP synthesis going with no gradient, and E inverts the true relationship."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Photosynthesis","stem":"A plant photosynthesizing in bright light is suddenly moved into complete darkness while its CO2 supply is held constant. In the moments right after the lights go out, what happens to the levels of 3-phosphoglycerate (3-PGA) and ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate (RuBP) in the Calvin cycle?","choices":["3-PGA increases; RuBP decreases","3-PGA decreases; RuBP increases","Both 3-PGA and RuBP increase","Both 3-PGA and RuBP decrease","Neither changes, because the Calvin cycle does not need light"],"correct":0,"explanation":"In darkness the light reactions stop supplying ATP and NADPH. Reducing 3-PGA to G3P needs ATP and NADPH, so that step stalls and 3-PGA accumulates. Regenerating RuBP also needs ATP, so RuBP is no longer replenished, yet the still-present CO2 keeps being fixed onto whatever RuBP remains, so RuBP is consumed and falls. B describes the opposite manipulation (removing CO2 while light continues). E is wrong because although the Calvin enzymes do not directly absorb light, they depend on the ATP and NADPH that only the light reactions provide."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Central dogma (DNA->RNA->protein)","stem":"Within the coding region of a gene, which single mutation is generally expected to be the MOST damaging to the resulting protein?","choices":["A single-nucleotide substitution in the third (wobble) position of a codon","A single-nucleotide insertion near the 5' end of the coding sequence","A substitution that changes a codon to a different codon for the same amino acid","A substitution near the 3' end that changes one amino acid","A substitution that changes a codon to one for a chemically similar amino acid"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A single insertion causes a frameshift: every codon downstream of the insertion is read out of register, usually scrambling the rest of the protein and frequently introducing a premature stop codon. Placing it near the 5' end maximizes damage because nearly the whole protein is affected. Choices A and C are typically silent because of codon degeneracy at the third base. D and E are missense mutations that alter a single residue, and one near the 3' end changes very little of the chain, so all are far more localized than a frameshift."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mitosis & meiosis","stem":"A diploid organism has 2n = 4. Consider one of its cells at metaphase of meiosis II. How many chromosomes (counted by centromeres) and how many chromatids are present?","choices":["4 chromosomes; 8 chromatids","2 chromosomes; 4 chromatids","4 chromosomes; 4 chromatids","2 chromosomes; 2 chromatids","8 chromosomes; 8 chromatids"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Meiosis I already separated the homologous pairs, so each meiosis II cell is haploid with n = 2 chromosomes. At metaphase II those chromosomes still each consist of two sister chromatids joined at the centromere (they separate only in anaphase II), giving 2 x 2 = 4 chromatids. Choice A keeps 4 chromosomes, forgetting the number was halved in meiosis I. D forgets that sister chromatids are still attached at metaphase II. C and E miscount both figures."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mendelian genetics","stem":"An individual with genotype AaBb (the two genes are on separate chromosomes) is crossed with an aabb individual. What fraction of the offspring are expected to be homozygous recessive at BOTH loci (aabb)?","choices":["1/16","1/4","1/2","1/8","1/9"],"correct":1,"explanation":"This is a test cross, not a self-cross. Treat each gene separately: Aa x aa yields 1/2 aa offspring, and independently Bb x bb yields 1/2 bb. Multiplying, 1/2 x 1/2 = 1/4 aabb. The classic trap is 1/16 (A), which is the aabb fraction from an AaBb x AaBb self-cross; here one parent is already fully recessive, so the odds are higher. 1/2 (C) ignores the second gene, while 1/8 (D) and 1/9 (E) use incorrect denominators."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Evolution & population genetics","stem":"A recessive genetic disorder affects 1 in 100 people in a large population that is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. Approximately what fraction of the population are unaffected carriers (heterozygotes)?","choices":["1/100 (1%)","1/10 (10%)","About 18%","About 20%","About 81%"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Affected individuals represent q^2 = 1/100 = 0.01, so q = 0.1 and p = 0.9. Carrier frequency is 2pq = 2(0.9)(0.1) = 0.18, about 18%. The 10% trap (B) mistakes the allele frequency q for the carrier frequency. 20% (D) approximates 2q, forgetting the p factor in 2pq. 1% (A) just restates the affected frequency q^2, and 81% (E) is p^2, the homozygous dominant frequency."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Physiology (a body system)","stem":"During intense exercise, an actively contracting skeletal muscle produces large amounts of CO2 and lactic acid, lowering the local pH. What is the effect on hemoglobin in the capillaries serving that muscle?","choices":["Hemoglobin's affinity for O2 increases, releasing more O2 to the muscle","Hemoglobin's affinity for O2 decreases, releasing more O2 to the muscle","Hemoglobin's affinity for O2 increases, holding onto O2 more tightly","Hemoglobin's affinity for O2 decreases, holding onto O2 more tightly","pH has no effect on hemoglobin; only O2 partial pressure matters"],"correct":1,"explanation":"By the Bohr effect, rising CO2 and H+ (falling pH) lower hemoglobin's affinity for O2, shifting the oxygen dissociation curve to the right so more O2 is unloaded exactly where active tissue needs it. A and C wrongly raise affinity, which would trap O2 in the blood, the opposite of what an exercising muscle needs. D correctly lowers affinity but then contradicts itself by saying O2 is held more tightly, when lower affinity means easier release. E denies the Bohr effect, but pH is a real physiological modulator of O2 binding."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Moles & stoichiometry","stem":"Aluminum reacts with chlorine: 2 Al(s) + 3 Cl2(g) -> 2 AlCl3(s). A flask is charged with 5.40 g of Al and 24.85 g of Cl2, and the reaction is allowed to go to completion. What mass of the excess reactant remains? (Molar masses: Al = 27.0 g/mol, Cl2 = 71.0 g/mol.)","choices":["10.65 g Cl2","3.55 g Cl2","1.78 g Cl2","15.4 g Cl2","0 g (nothing remains)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Moles: Al = 5.40/27.0 = 0.200 mol; Cl2 = 24.85/71.0 = 0.350 mol. Al requires Cl2 in a 2:3 ratio, so 0.200 mol Al needs 0.300 mol Cl2. Only 0.300 of the 0.350 mol Cl2 is used, so Al is limiting and 0.050 mol Cl2 remains = 0.050 x 71.0 = 3.55 g (B). Distractor A (10.65 g) subtracts moles directly (0.350 - 0.200) ignoring the 3:2 ratio. C (1.78 g) multiplies the correct 0.050 mol by the atomic mass 35.5 instead of the molecular mass 71.0. D (15.4 g) inverts the mole ratio (uses 2/3 instead of 3/2, leaving 0.217 mol). E (0 g) wrongly assumes the mixture is stoichiometric."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Atomic structure & quantum numbers","stem":"What is the maximum number of electrons in an atom that can have both the principal quantum number n = 4 and the spin quantum number m_s = +1/2?","choices":["8","32","16","18","4"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The n = 4 shell contains n^2 = 16 orbitals (one 4s, three 4p, five 4d, seven 4f). Each orbital can hold at most one electron with m_s = +1/2 (the other electron in a filled orbital has m_s = -1/2), so the maximum is 16 (C). Distractor B (32) is the total capacity 2n^2 of the shell but forgets that the m_s = +1/2 restriction admits only one of the two electrons per orbital. A (8) uses 2n or counts only the s and p subshells. D (18) is the capacity of the n = 3 shell, a mix-up of which shell was specified. E (4) counts the number of subshells (s, p, d, f) rather than orbitals."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Periodic trends","stem":"Rank the following isoelectronic ions in order of increasing ionic radius (smallest to largest): O2-, F-, Na+, Mg2+, Al3+.","choices":["O2- < F- < Na+ < Mg2+ < Al3+","F- < O2- < Na+ < Mg2+ < Al3+","Na+ < Mg2+ < Al3+ < F- < O2-","Al3+ < Mg2+ < Na+ < F- < O2-","Al3+ < Mg2+ < Na+ < O2- < F-"],"correct":3,"explanation":"All five ions have 10 electrons (isoelectronic). For a fixed electron count, radius shrinks as the nuclear charge (proton count) rises, because more protons pull the same electron cloud inward. Proton counts: Al(13) > Mg(12) > Na(11) > F(9) > O(8), so radius increases in the reverse order: Al3+ < Mg2+ < Na+ < F- < O2- (D). Distractor A reverses the logic, assuming more protons means larger. B and C order the ions by charge magnitude or by grouping cations and anions rather than by Z. E gets the cations right but reverses the two anions: O2- (Z = 8) is actually larger than F- (Z = 9), not smaller."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Bonding, Lewis & VSEPR","stem":"Which of the following molecules has a seesaw molecular geometry?","choices":["XeF4","PF5","SF4","ClF3","BrF5"],"correct":2,"explanation":"SF4 has sulfur with 4 bonding pairs and 1 lone pair (AX4E), giving 5 electron domains in a trigonal-bipyramidal arrangement; the lone pair occupies an equatorial position, producing a seesaw shape (C). XeF4 is AX4E2 (two lone pairs) = square planar. PF5 is AX5 (no lone pairs) = trigonal bipyramidal. ClF3 is AX3E2 = T-shaped. BrF5 is AX5E = square pyramidal. Each distractor is a real geometry that a student picks by miscounting lone pairs; only the 4-bond, 1-lone-pair count of SF4 yields seesaw."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Gas laws","stem":"A rigid, sealed vessel holds equal masses of He and O2 at 300 K. What fraction of the total pressure is contributed by He? (Molar masses: He = 4.0 g/mol, O2 = 32.0 g/mol.)","choices":["1/2","1/9","1/8","4/5","8/9"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Partial pressure fraction equals mole fraction (same T and V). For equal mass m: mol He = m/4, mol O2 = m/32. Mole fraction of He = (m/4)/(m/4 + m/32) = (8/32)/(9/32) = 8/9 (E). The lighter gas supplies far more moles per gram, so it dominates the pressure. Distractor A (1/2) assumes equal mass means equal moles and equal pressures. B (1/9) inverts the fraction, giving the heavier gas the larger share. C (1/8) uses the molar-mass ratio 4:32 directly as the pressure ratio. D (4/5) uses O = 16 (atomic) instead of O2 = 32 for oxygen's molar mass, giving mol O2 = m/16 and 4/5."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Thermochemistry","stem":"Estimate the enthalpy change for H2(g) + Cl2(g) -> 2 HCl(g) using bond enthalpies: H-H = 436 kJ/mol, Cl-Cl = 242 kJ/mol, H-Cl = 431 kJ/mol.","choices":["+184 kJ","-184 kJ","+247 kJ","-862 kJ","+678 kJ"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Delta H = (bonds broken) - (bonds formed) = [D(H-H) + D(Cl-Cl)] - [2 x D(H-Cl)] = (436 + 242) - 2(431) = 678 - 862 = -184 kJ (B). Distractor A (+184) reverses the formula to formed minus broken. C (+247) forgets the coefficient 2 on HCl, counting only one H-Cl bond formed: 678 - 431 = +247. D (-862) counts only the bonds formed and ignores the energy needed to break the reactant bonds. E (+678) counts only the bonds broken and ignores the bonds formed."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Kinetics","stem":"A reaction proceeds by the mechanism: Step 1 (fast equilibrium): NO + Br2 <=> NOBr2; Step 2 (slow): NOBr2 + NO -> 2 NOBr. What is the predicted rate law for the overall reaction 2 NO + Br2 -> 2 NOBr?","choices":["rate = k[NOBr2][NO]","rate = k[NO][Br2]","rate = k[NO]^2","rate = k[NO]^2[Br2]","rate = k[NO][Br2]^2"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The rate is set by the slow step: rate = k2[NOBr2][NO]. NOBr2 is an intermediate and cannot appear in the final law. From the fast pre-equilibrium, [NOBr2] = K1[NO][Br2]. Substituting gives rate = k2K1[NO][Br2][NO] = k[NO]^2[Br2] (D), second order in NO and first in Br2. Distractor A leaves the intermediate NOBr2 in the law (not permitted). B substitutes for the intermediate but drops the second [NO] that comes from the slow step. C keeps [NO]^2 but drops [Br2] entirely. E swaps the orders, making Br2 second order and NO first order."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Equilibrium","stem":"For H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2 HI(g), Kc = 49 at a given temperature. A 1.00 L flask initially contains 1.00 M H2 and 1.00 M I2 and no HI. What is the equilibrium concentration of HI?","choices":["1.56 M","0.78 M","1.92 M","2.00 M","1.00 M"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Let x be the amount of H2 (and I2) that reacts. At equilibrium [H2] = [I2] = 1.00 - x and [HI] = 2x. Kc = (2x)^2/[(1-x)(1-x)] = 49. Taking the square root of both sides: 2x/(1-x) = 7, so 2x = 7 - 7x, 9x = 7, x = 0.778. Thus [HI] = 2x = 1.56 M (A). Distractor B (0.78) solves for x correctly but forgets that [HI] = 2x. C (1.92) skips the square root, solving 2x/(1-x) = 49. D (2.00) assumes the reaction goes to completion. E (1.00) guesses 50% conversion (x = 0.5) by symmetry, ignoring the value of Kc."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Acids, bases & pH","stem":"What is the pH of a buffer that is 0.20 M NH3 and 0.20 M NH4Cl? Kb(NH3) = 1.8 x 10^-5, Kw = 1.0 x 10^-14.","choices":["4.74","11.28","9.26","5.00","2.72"],"correct":2,"explanation":"This is a buffer of a weak base and its conjugate acid. pOH = pKb + log([NH4+]/[NH3]) = pKb + log(0.20/0.20) = pKb = -log(1.8 x 10^-5) = 4.74. Then pH = 14.00 - 4.74 = 9.26 (C), which is basic, as expected for an ammonia buffer. Distractor A (4.74) reports pOH as the pH, forgetting the 14 - pOH conversion. B (11.28) ignores the NH4Cl and treats the solution as pure 0.20 M NH3 ([OH-] = sqrt(Kb x 0.20)). D (5.00) ignores the NH3 and treats it as pure 0.20 M NH4+ acting as a weak acid (Ka = Kw/Kb). E (2.72) combines the pure-weak-base error with reporting pOH as pH."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Electrochemistry","stem":"Given the standard reduction potentials Fe3+ + e- -> Fe2+ (E° = +0.77 V) and MnO4- + 8 H+ + 5 e- -> Mn2+ + 4 H2O (E° = +1.51 V), what is the standard cell potential E°cell for the reaction in which MnO4- oxidizes Fe2+?","choices":["+2.28 V","+0.74 V","-0.74 V","-2.34 V","+3.70 V"],"correct":1,"explanation":"MnO4- is reduced (cathode) and Fe2+ is oxidized (anode). E°cell = E°cathode - E°anode = 1.51 - 0.77 = +0.74 V (B). Standard potentials are intensive: even though the balanced equation multiplies the Fe half-reaction by 5 to match electrons, the E° values are never multiplied by coefficients or electron counts. Distractor A (+2.28) adds the two potentials instead of subtracting. C (-0.74) reverses the cell, treating Fe as the cathode. D (-2.34) multiplies Fe's E° by 5 electrons (1.51 - 5 x 0.77). E (+3.70) multiplies the correct cell potential by 5 to 'balance' electrons, wrongly treating E° as an extensive quantity."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IUPAC nomenclature","stem":"Give the correct IUPAC name for the compound CH2=CH-CH2-CH(OH)-CH3.","choices":["pent-1-en-4-ol","2-hydroxypent-4-ene","pent-4-en-2-ol","pent-3-en-2-ol","4-pentenol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The principal characteristic group is -OH, cited as the suffix -ol, and it must receive the lowest locant, taking priority over the C=C double bond. Numbering CH2=CH-CH2-CH(OH)-CH3 from the CH3 end puts OH at C2 and the double bond at C4-C5, giving pent-4-en-2-ol. 'pent-1-en-4-ol' numbers from the alkene end to give the double bond the lowest locant, which violates the rule that the -ol suffix outranks unsaturation. 'pent-3-en-2-ol' miscounts the double-bond position: a CH2 sits between the carbinol carbon and the alkene, so the C=C is at 4,5 not 3,4. '2-hydroxypent-4-ene' wrongly treats OH as a hydroxy prefix instead of the senior suffix. '4-pentenol' omits the required OH locant and modern -en- format, so it is not an acceptable name."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Stereochemistry (R/S, chirality)","stem":"2,3-Dichlorobutane can exist as several stereoisomers. Which statement best describes the (2R,3S) stereoisomer?","choices":["It is chiral and optically active, with a nonsuperimposable mirror image.","It is the enantiomer of (2S,3R)-2,3-dichlorobutane.","It has no stereocenters because C2 and C3 bear identical substituent patterns.","It is a meso compound and is optically inactive despite containing two stereocenters.","It is a diastereomer of (2R,3R)-2,3-dichlorobutane and is itself chiral."],"correct":3,"explanation":"In (2R,3S)-2,3-dichlorobutane each stereocenter carries the same set of groups (Cl, H, CH3, and the opposite CHClCH3 half). The opposite R and S descriptors create an internal mirror plane, so the molecule is superimposable on its mirror image: it is meso, hence achiral and optically inactive. Because its mirror image is identical, '(2S,3R)' names the very same molecule, so it is NOT an enantiomer of it. It is not optically active (the internal symmetry cancels rotation). It does have two genuine stereocenters. It is indeed a diastereomer of the (2R,3R) isomer, but the extra claim that it is chiral is false, so that choice fails."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"SN1/SN2/E1/E2","stem":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane, CH3-CBr(CH3)-CH2-CH3, is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (a strong, bulky base) and warmed. What is the major organic product?","choices":["2-methyl-2-butene","2-methyl-1-butene","2-methylbutan-2-ol","2-tert-butoxy-2-methylbutane","3-methyl-1-butene"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The substrate is a tertiary halide, and a strong, sterically bulky base like potassium tert-butoxide drives E2 elimination. Because the base is bulky, it abstracts the more accessible proton from a methyl group, giving the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene, 2-methyl-1-butene. 2-methyl-2-butene is the Zaitsev (more-substituted) product expected with a small base such as ethoxide, but bulky bases reverse that selectivity. Substitution is wrong here: SN1 to 2-methylbutan-2-ol is disfavored because tert-butoxide is a strong base, not a weak nucleophilic solvent, and SN2 to the tert-butyl ether is impossible at a hindered tertiary carbon with a bulky nucleophile. 3-methyl-1-butene has the wrong carbon skeleton."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alkene addition reactions","stem":"(E)-2-Butene is treated with Br2 in CCl4. What is the major product and its stereochemistry?","choices":["(±)-2,3-dibromobutane, a racemic pair of enantiomers","a single enantiomer, (2R,3R)-2,3-dibromobutane","the (2R,3R)/(2S,3S) pair, formed by syn addition","2-bromobutane, by Markovnikov addition","meso-2,3-dibromobutane"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Br2 adds across an alkene with anti (trans) stereochemistry via a bromonium ion. Anti addition to the trans (E) double bond places the two C-Br bonds on opposite faces and yields the internally mirror-symmetric (2R,3S) product, which is meso-2,3-dibromobutane. The racemic (±) pair is the result for the cis (Z) alkene, so that choice comes from swapping the geometry. A single enantiomer ignores that a chiral product from an achiral alkene forms as a racemate. The syn-addition choice is wrong because Br2 adds anti. '2-bromobutane by Markovnikov addition' treats Br2 like HBr, but both bromines add."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Aromaticity & EAS","stem":"Which of the following species is aromatic?","choices":["cyclopentadienyl anion","cyclopentadienyl cation","cyclobutadiene","cyclooctatetraene (neutral)","1,3-cyclohexadiene"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Aromaticity requires a cyclic, planar, fully conjugated ring with (4n+2) pi electrons (Huckel's rule). The cyclopentadienyl anion has 6 pi electrons in a planar, fully conjugated five-membered ring, so it is aromatic. The cyclopentadienyl cation (4 pi) and cyclobutadiene (4 pi) meet the 4n count and are antiaromatic. Cyclooctatetraene has 8 pi electrons and puckers into a nonplanar tub shape, so it is nonaromatic rather than antiaromatic. 1,3-Cyclohexadiene contains sp3 CH2 carbons that interrupt the continuous ring of p orbitals, so it cannot be aromatic despite having a six-membered ring with double bonds."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alcohols & carbonyls","stem":"Methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CO-OCH3, is treated with excess NaBH4 in methanol, followed by aqueous workup. What is the major product?","choices":["pentane-1,4-diol","5-hydroxypentan-2-one","methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate","methyl 4-oxopentanoate (unchanged)","4-hydroxypentanoic acid"],"correct":2,"explanation":"NaBH4 is a mild hydride donor that reduces aldehydes and ketones but not esters or carboxylic acids. In methyl 4-oxopentanoate only the C4 ketone is reduced, giving methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate with the ester intact. Reducing both carbonyls to pentane-1,4-diol would require the stronger LiAlH4, not NaBH4. Reducing the ester while sparing the ketone (5-hydroxypentan-2-one) reverses the true chemoselectivity. NaBH4 readily reduces a ketone, so 'unchanged' is wrong, and NaBH4 does not hydrolyze an ester to a carboxylic acid, ruling out 4-hydroxypentanoic acid."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Acidity & pKa","stem":"Which of the following has the most acidic proton?","choices":["the O-H of ethanol","the terminal C-H of propyne (HC≡C-CH3)","an alpha C-H of acetone","the central CH2 of pentane-2,4-dione (CH3-CO-CH2-CO-CH3)","the O-H of water"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The central methylene of pentane-2,4-dione is the most acidic (pKa about 9). Removing one of those protons gives an enolate whose negative charge is delocalized onto TWO flanking carbonyl oxygens, an unusually stable conjugate base. This beats the O-H acids listed: ethanol (pKa ~16) and water (pKa ~15.7) yield an alkoxide/hydroxide with far less delocalization. The tempting trap is the rule of thumb 'O-H is always more acidic than C-H,' which fails when the C-H is doubly activated by two carbonyls. A terminal alkyne C-H (pKa ~25) is acidic for a hydrocarbon but much weaker, and an alpha C-H of acetone (pKa ~20) is stabilized by only one carbonyl."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Resonance & stability","stem":"Which of the following carbocations is the most stable?","choices":["the tert-butyl cation, (CH3)3C+","the 1-phenylethyl cation, C6H5-CH(+)-CH3","the isopropyl cation, (CH3)2CH+","the allyl cation, CH2=CH-CH2(+)","the 2-oxopropyl cation, CH3-CO-CH2(+)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The 1-phenylethyl cation is a secondary benzylic cation: the positive charge is delocalized by resonance into the aromatic ring, spreading it over several carbons, on top of stabilization from the adjacent methyl. This extended resonance stabilization outweighs stabilization by hyperconjugation alone, so it is more stable than the tert-butyl cation. The tert-butyl choice is the classic trap ('a 3 degree cation is always most stable'), which fails against a resonance-delocalized benzylic cation. The isopropyl cation is only secondary. The allyl cation is resonance-stabilized but only primary, with two equivalent contributors, giving less overall stabilization than the benzylic case. The 2-oxopropyl cation sits directly beside an electron-withdrawing carbonyl, which destabilizes the adjacent positive charge."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IR & H-NMR spectroscopy","stem":"A compound with molecular formula C4H8O shows a strong IR absorption at 1715 cm-1. Its 1H NMR spectrum consists of a 3H triplet, a 2H quartet, and a 3H singlet. Which compound is it?","choices":["butanal","2-methylpropanal","oxolane (tetrahydrofuran)","but-3-en-1-ol","butan-2-one"],"correct":4,"explanation":"All five choices are C4H8O isomers, so the spectra must distinguish them. The strong 1715 cm-1 IR band is a carbonyl (C=O), which eliminates oxolane (an ether, no C=O) and but-3-en-1-ol (which would show a broad O-H near 3300 and =C-H stretches). The NMR pattern of a 3H triplet plus a 2H quartet is an ethyl group (CH3CH2 coupling only to each other), and the isolated 3H singlet is a CH3 with no neighboring protons. Together these fit CH3-CO-CH2-CH3, butan-2-one. Butanal and 2-methylpropanal are aldehydes: each would show a distinctive aldehyde proton near 9.7 ppm and different coupling (a chain multiplet or a 6H doublet with a septet), not the clean ethyl-plus-singlet pattern."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Synthesis / roadmap","stem":"Which reaction sequence best converts 1-bromopropane (CH3CH2CH2Br) into propan-2-ol (CH3CH(OH)CH3)?","choices":["aqueous NaOH (single step)","(1) NaOEt, heat; then (2) BH3·THF, then H2O2, NaOH","(1) NaOEt, heat; then (2) dilute H2SO4, H2O","(1) NaOEt, heat; then (2) HBr, peroxides","PCC, CH2Cl2"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The leaving group is on C1 but the target OH is on C2, so simple substitution cannot deliver the right regiochemistry. Direct SN2 with aqueous NaOH just replaces Br at C1, giving propan-1-ol. The correct route first eliminates with sodium ethoxide and heat (E2) to form propene, then adds water by acid-catalyzed Markovnikov hydration, placing OH on the more substituted C2 to give propan-2-ol. Following elimination with hydroboration-oxidation (BH3 then H2O2/NaOH) adds water anti-Markovnikov, giving propan-1-ol instead. Elimination then HBr with peroxides adds Br anti-Markovnikov, regenerating 1-bromopropane. PCC is an oxidant, but there is no alcohol present for it to act on."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Fractions & decimals","stem":"Evaluate the expression:  5/6 ÷ 2/3 + 1/4 × 2/5 − 1/10","choices":["1/2","5/9","4/5","5/4","29/20"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Apply order of operations: division and multiplication first. 5/6 ÷ 2/3 = 5/6 × 3/2 = 5/4, and 1/4 × 2/5 = 1/10. Then 5/4 + 1/10 − 1/10 = 5/4, because the two 1/10 terms cancel (the edge case). 1/2 results from working strictly left-to-right and ignoring precedence. 5/9 results from multiplying instead of dividing (failing to invert), giving 5/6 × 2/3 = 10/18. 4/5 comes from inverting the wrong fraction (6/5 × 2/3). 29/20 comes from adding the final 1/10 instead of subtracting it (25/20 + 2/20 + 2/20)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Algebra & equations","stem":"Solve for all real x:  √(2x + 7) = x + 2","choices":["x = 1 only","x = -3 only","x = 1 and x = -3","x = -1 and x = 3","No real solution"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Squaring gives 2x + 7 = x² + 4x + 4, so x² + 2x − 3 = 0 → (x + 3)(x − 1) = 0, yielding candidates x = 1 and x = -3. You must check both in the ORIGINAL equation. x = 1: √9 = 3 = 1 + 2 (valid). x = -3: √1 = 1, but -3 + 2 = -1, so 1 ≠ -1 (extraneous, rejected). Only x = 1 survives. Choice C keeps both roots without checking; B keeps the rejected root and drops the valid one; D comes from a sign slip writing x² − 2x − 3 = 0 → (x − 3)(x + 1); E discards the valid root."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Ratios & proportions","stem":"A recipe uses flour and sugar in the ratio 7:4. A baker adds 3 more cups of flour than the recipe calls for and uses 2 fewer cups of sugar, changing the ratio of flour to sugar to 2:1. How many cups of sugar did the original recipe call for?","choices":["7","20","26","28","49"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Let flour = 7k and sugar = 4k. After the changes: (7k + 3)/(4k − 2) = 2/1, so 7k + 3 = 8k − 4, giving k = 7. Original sugar = 4(7) = 28 cups (check: (49+3)/(28−2) = 52/26 = 2). Choice 20 comes from writing 8k − 2 (failing to distribute the ×2 to the −2), which gives k = 5. Choice 26 is the sugar amount AFTER removing 2 cups (4k − 2), not the original. Choice 49 is the original flour, not sugar. Choice 7 is merely the multiplier k."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Percentages","stem":"A store marks a product up 40% above its cost, then during a sale takes 25% off the marked price. If the final sale price is $84, what was the store's cost?","choices":["$60.00","$73.04","$80.00","$98.82","$112.00"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Let cost = C. Marked price = 1.40C; after 25% off, sale price = 0.75 × 1.40C = 1.05C = 84, so C = $80. Choice $112 undoes only the discount (84/0.75). Choice $60 undoes only the markup (84/1.40). Choice $73.04 treats the net change as +15% (84/1.15). Choice $98.82 treats the net as 15% off cost (84/0.85). Percent changes do not simply add or subtract; you must chain the factors ×1.40 then ×0.75, which gives $80."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Exponents & roots","stem":"If 2^x = 5, what is the value of 2^(3x − 1)?","choices":["14","62.5","124","125","250"],"correct":1,"explanation":"2^(3x − 1) = 2^(3x) · 2^(−1) = (2^x)³ / 2 = 5³ / 2 = 125/2 = 62.5. Choice 125 forgets the −1 (computes only 2^(3x)). Choice 250 multiplies by 2 instead of dividing (misreading 2^(−1) as ×2). Choice 124 subtracts 1 from 125, treating the exponent's −1 as 'minus one.' Choice 14 uses the false rule 2^(3x) = 3·2^x, giving 3(5) − 1."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Geometry (area/volume)","stem":"A right circular cylinder has its radius increased by 20% and its height decreased by 20%. By what percentage does its volume change?","choices":["decreases 4%","no change (0%)","increases 15.2%","increases 24%","increases 44%"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Volume V = πr²h. New volume = π(1.2r)²(0.8h) = π(1.44)(0.8)r²h = 1.152 V, a 15.2% increase. Choice 0% assumes the +20% and −20% cancel (they do not, because radius is squared). Choice −4% forgets to square the radius, using 1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96. Choice +44% applies the squared radius factor (1.2² = 1.44) but forgets the height decrease. Choice +24% just computes 44% − 20% as if the effects added linearly."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Trigonometry","stem":"In a right triangle, angle A is acute and sin A = 3/5. What is the value of tan A + cos A?","choices":["0.78","1.35","1.40","1.55","2.13"],"correct":3,"explanation":"sin A = 3/5 gives a 3-4-5 right triangle (opposite 3, hypotenuse 5, adjacent 4). So cos A = 4/5 and tan A = 3/4. Then tan A + cos A = 3/4 + 4/5 = 15/20 + 16/20 = 31/20 = 1.55. Choice 1.35 mistakenly uses cos A = 3/5 (3/4 + 3/5). Choice 1.40 uses tan A = 3/5 (opposite/hypotenuse error) or simply adds sin + cos. Choice 2.13 inverts tan to 4/3 (4/3 + 4/5 = 32/15). Choice 0.78 adds the fractions incorrectly as (3+4)/(4+5) = 7/9."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Probability","stem":"A bag contains 4 red marbles and 6 blue marbles. Two marbles are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that the two marbles are different colors?","choices":["4/15","7/15","8/15","12/25","1/2"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Without replacement, P(different) = P(red then blue) + P(blue then red) = (4/10)(6/9) + (6/10)(4/9) = 24/90 + 24/90 = 48/90 = 8/15. Choice 4/15 counts only one ordering (forgets to double). Choice 7/15 is the complement, P(same color) = 1 − 8/15, a tempting off-by-complement error. Choice 12/25 (= 0.48) mistakenly uses replacement: 2(4/10)(6/10). Choice 1/2 is a symmetry guess with no computation."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Statistics (mean/median/SD)","stem":"A class of 20 students has a mean score of 72. A subgroup of 8 of these students has a mean score of 78. What is the mean score of the remaining 12 students?","choices":["66","68","69.6","75","102"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Total of all 20 = 20 × 72 = 1440. The subgroup of 8 sums to 8 × 78 = 624, so the remaining 12 sum to 1440 − 624 = 816, giving a mean of 816/12 = 68. Choice 75 is just the plain average of the two means, (72 + 78)/2, which ignores the different group sizes. Choice 66 simply subtracts the 6-point gap (72 − 6). Choice 69.6 spreads the subgroup's 48-point total excess over all 20 students instead of the 12 (72 − 48/20). Choice 102 divides 816 by 8 (the wrong count)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Word problems (rate/work/mixture)","stem":"How many liters of a 60% acid solution must be added to 10 liters of a 25% acid solution to produce a 40% acid solution?","choices":["2.5 L","3 L","7.5 L","10 L","13.33 L"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Balance the acid: 0.60x + 0.25(10) = 0.40(x + 10). So 0.6x + 2.5 = 0.4x + 4 → 0.2x = 1.5 → x = 7.5 L. Choice 2.5 sets the right side to 0.40(10), ignoring that the total volume grows to x + 10. Choice 10 wrongly assumes equal volumes because 40% looks 'in the middle.' Choice 13.33 inverts the alligation ratio (the correct ratio of 60% to 25% is 15:20 = 3:4, giving (3/4)(10) = 7.5, not (4/3)(10)). Choice 3 comes from mis-dividing 1.5 by 0.5 instead of 0.2."}]},{"n":9,"tier":9,"questions":[{"section":"bio","topic":"Biomolecules","stem":"A linear tripeptide has the sequence Glu-Lys-His (written N-terminus to C-terminus). Use these pKa values: alpha-amino (N-terminus) = 9.0; alpha-carboxyl (C-terminus) = 2.0; Glu side chain = 4.0; Lys side chain = 10.5; His side chain (imidazole) = 6.0. What is the net charge of this peptide at pH 7.0?","choices":["-2","-1","0","+1","+2"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Assign each ionizable group by comparing pH to its pKa. Amino groups are +1 when protonated (pH below pKa); carboxyl groups are -1 when deprotonated (pH above pKa). N-terminus (pKa 9, pH<pKa) = +1; C-terminus (pKa 2, pH>pKa) = -1; Glu side chain (pKa 4, pH>pKa) = -1; Lys side chain (pKa 10.5, pH<pKa) = +1; His side chain (pKa 6): because pH 7 is ABOVE 6, the imidazole is mostly deprotonated and essentially neutral = 0. Sum = +1 -1 -1 +1 + 0 = 0 (choice C). The classic trap is counting His as +1 simply because it is a 'basic' residue, which gives +1 (choice D); but at pH 7 the peptide is just above the imidazole pKa, so it carries no net charge there. Dropping the C-terminus or the Glu side chain instead yields +1 or +2."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cell structure & organelles","stem":"A newly synthesized enzyme carries an N-terminal signal peptide and, during its transit through the secretory system, acquires a mannose-6-phosphate tag. Which itinerary correctly describes this protein's path from synthesis to its final destination?","choices":["Cytosolic ribosome to rough ER lumen to transport vesicle to cis-Golgi to trans-Golgi network to lysosome","Cytosolic ribosome to rough ER to cis-Golgi to trans-Golgi to secretory vesicle to plasma membrane (secretion)","Cytosolic ribosome to smooth ER to Golgi to lysosome","Cytosolic ribosome to rough ER lumen directly to the lysosome, bypassing the Golgi","Free cytosolic ribosome to the cytosol to the lysosome by direct post-translational import"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A signal peptide directs co-translational insertion into the rough ER lumen; the protein then buds off in a transport vesicle to the cis-Golgi and moves through to the trans-Golgi network. The mannose-6-phosphate tag (added in the Golgi) is the address label that binds M6P receptors in the trans-Golgi network and routes the enzyme to the lysosome (choice A). Choice B is the DEFAULT secretory route to the cell surface, but the M6P tag specifically diverts the protein away from secretion, so it is the key trap. The smooth ER (choice C) lacks ribosomes and is not where signal-peptide proteins enter. The M6P tag is added IN the Golgi, so the protein cannot skip the Golgi (choice D). A signal peptide means the ribosome docks on the ER, not a free cytosolic ribosome with post-translational import (choice E)."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Membrane transport","stem":"A cell with an internal osmolarity of 300 mOsm is placed in a large bath containing 150 mM NaCl (fully dissociating and NONpenetrating; contributes 300 mOsm) plus 100 mM urea (freely penetrating; contributes 100 mOsm). The bath total osmolarity is therefore 400 mOsm. What happens to the cell's volume as it reaches equilibrium?","choices":["The cell shrinks and stays shrunken, because the bath's total osmolarity (400 mOsm) exceeds the cell's 300 mOsm","The cell swells and lyses, because urea entry pulls water in","The cell transiently shrinks, then returns to its original volume, ending unchanged, because urea is a penetrating solute so the effective (tonic) osmolarity is only the 300 mOsm from NaCl (isotonic)","The cell swells to a new larger steady volume, because entering urea permanently raises intracellular osmolarity","The cell shrinks to a smaller steady volume set by the 400 mOsm total gradient"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Osmolarity and tonicity are not the same. Only NONpenetrating solutes set tonicity, because a solute that freely crosses the membrane cannot sustain an osmotic gradient. Urea equilibrates across the membrane, so at steady state it contributes nothing to net water movement; the effective (tonic) osmolarity is just the 300 mOsm of NaCl, which equals the cell interior, i.e. ISOTONIC. Momentarily the 400 mOsm bath draws water out, but as urea enters and equilibrates, water follows back, returning the cell to its original volume (choice C). Choices A and E fall for the trap of using total osmolarity (hyperosmotic) to predict tonicity. Choice B/D over-weight urea entry as if it accumulated; urea only equalizes, it does not concentrate inside."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cellular respiration","stem":"Isolated, actively respiring mitochondria are treated with 2,4-dinitrophenol (DNP), a small lipophilic weak acid that shuttles protons across the inner membrane, making it leaky to H+. Which outcome best describes the immediate effect on electron transport (oxygen consumption) and on ATP synthesis?","choices":["Both electron transport (O2 consumption) and ATP synthesis stop, because the proton gradient powers both processes","Electron transport and O2 consumption continue and even accelerate, while ATP synthesis by ATP synthase nearly ceases; the released free energy is dissipated as heat","O2 consumption stops, but ATP synthesis continues from the standing proton gradient","ATP synthesis increases, because protons now move across the membrane more rapidly","Nothing changes in the mitochondrion, because DNP acts only on cytosolic glycolysis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"DNP is an uncoupler: it collapses the proton-motive force by letting H+ leak back across the inner membrane, bypassing ATP synthase. ATP synthase needs the gradient, so ATP output falls sharply. But the electron transport chain does not require an existing gradient to run; if anything, removing the 'back-pressure' of a high gradient lets the chain pump faster, so O2 consumption RISES and the energy is released as heat rather than captured as ATP (choice B). Choice A is the central trap: people assume the ETC needs the gradient too, but the ETC generates the gradient, it is not driven by it. Choice C reverses which process depends on the gradient. Choice D forgets that leaked protons bypass the synthase. Choice E misplaces DNP's action; it works on the inner mitochondrial membrane."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Photosynthesis","stem":"Intact, isolated chloroplasts are brightly illuminated but the CO2 in the medium is completely removed. After a brief period, what is the most likely effect on the light reactions (electron transport, O2 evolution, and photophosphorylation)?","choices":["The light reactions continue unchanged, steadily producing O2, NADPH, and ATP","The light reactions accelerate, because there is no CO2 fixation competing for their products","O2 evolution and photophosphorylation slow and stop, because the halted Calvin cycle stops regenerating NADP+ and ADP, depriving the light reactions of their electron acceptor and of ADP","Only ATP synthesis stops; electron transport and O2 evolution proceed normally using water","The Calvin cycle keeps running on CO2 stored in the thylakoid lumen, so nothing changes"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The light and dark reactions are coupled through their shared pools of NADP+/NADPH and ADP/ATP. Without CO2, the Calvin cycle cannot fix carbon, so it stops consuming NADPH and ATP. NADPH then piles up and NADP+ (the terminal electron acceptor of the photosynthetic electron transport chain) is depleted; with no acceptor, electron flow from water backs up and O2 evolution halts. Likewise ADP runs out, so ATP synthase cannot make ATP (choice C). Choice D is the tempting partial truth: it correctly stops ATP synthesis but wrongly lets electron transport and O2 evolution continue, forgetting that NADP+ depletion also chokes electron flow. Choices A and B ignore the acceptor limitation entirely; choice E invents a CO2 reservoir that does not exist."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Central dogma (DNA->RNA->protein)","stem":"A protein-coding gene is normally read in a single reading frame. A mutant allele has a single-nucleotide INSERTION within codon 5 and, farther downstream, a single-nucleotide DELETION within codon 15. No new stop codon is generated in the affected stretch. Which best describes the mutant protein?","choices":["Every amino acid downstream of the insertion is altered, producing a completely nonfunctional protein","Only the amino acids encoded between the two mutation sites are altered; downstream of the deletion the original reading frame is restored and the sequence returns to normal","There is no change, because a single insertion and a single deletion cancel each other exactly","Exactly one amino acid changes at the insertion site and one at the deletion site, and the rest is normal","The protein is exactly one amino acid longer than normal but otherwise unchanged"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Reading frame is set by triplets. A +1 insertion shifts the frame for everything after codon 5, garbling those amino acids. A -1 deletion downstream changes the net shift to 0 (+1 then -1), so from the deletion onward the ribosome is back in the ORIGINAL frame and reads the wild-type sequence again. Thus only the stretch BETWEEN the two mutations is scrambled; the C-terminal region is restored (choice B). This is the logic behind the classic demonstration of a triplet code. Choice A is the trap for those who think any frameshift ruins everything downstream, ignoring that a compensating indel restores the frame. Choice C ignores the garbled middle segment. Choices D and E treat frameshifts like simple point mutations or in-frame length changes, which they are not."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mitosis & meiosis","stem":"In a normal diploid male, a single primary spermatocyte undergoes meiosis. During anaphase I, ONE homologous pair fails to separate (nondisjunction); every other step of both divisions proceeds normally. What are the chromosome constitutions of the four resulting sperm?","choices":["Two normal (n), one (n+1), and one (n-1)","Two (n+1) and two (n-1); none are normal","All four are normal (n), because meiosis II corrects the error","All four are (n+1)","Three normal (n) and one (n+1)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"In meiosis I nondisjunction, both homologs of the affected pair travel to the SAME pole. Both resulting secondary spermatocytes are therefore abnormal: one has the extra homolog (leading to n+1 products) and the other is missing it (leading to n-1 products). Each then divides normally in meiosis II, so all four gametes are aneuploid: two n+1 and two n-1 (choice B). Choice A is the key trap: that pattern (two normal, one n+1, one n-1) is what you get from meiosis II nondisjunction, where only one of the two secondary cells is affected and the other yields two normal gametes. The remaining choices ignore that nondisjunction moves whole chromosomes to one side while depleting the other."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mendelian genetics","stem":"In Labrador retrievers, coat color is set by two genes. Gene B: allele B (black) is dominant to b (brown). Gene E: at least one E allows dark pigment to be deposited in the coat, whereas the genotype ee blocks deposition and produces a yellow coat regardless of the B genotype. Two dogs, each of genotype BbEe, are crossed. What fraction of the offspring are expected to be brown?","choices":["1/16","3/16","4/16","9/16","6/16"],"correct":1,"explanation":"This is recessive epistasis: ee is epistatic and masks gene B as yellow. A dog is brown only if it is bb (brown pigment, not black) AND E_ (pigment is actually deposited). From BbEe x BbEe: P(bb) = 1/4 and P(E_) = 3/4, so P(brown) = 1/4 x 3/4 = 3/16 (choice B). The full ratio is 9 black (B_E_) : 3 brown (bbE_) : 4 yellow (__ee). Choice A (1/16) is the trap of requiring bb AND ee (that is a subset of yellow, not brown). Choice C (4/16) is the yellow fraction, confused for brown. Choice D (9/16) is black. Only 3/16 satisfies bb with at least one E."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Evolution & population genetics","stem":"A certain trait is caused by an X-linked recessive allele. In a large, randomly mating population at Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, the trait affects about 1 in 100 males. Approximately what fraction of FEMALES are carriers (heterozygous)?","choices":["About 1 in 10,000","About 1 in 200","About 1 in 100","About 1 in 50","About 1 in 25"],"correct":3,"explanation":"For an X-linked locus, males are hemizygous, so the frequency of affected males equals the recessive allele frequency directly: q = 1/100 = 0.01, giving p = 0.99. Carrier (heterozygous) females occur at frequency 2pq = 2(0.99)(0.01) = 0.0198, which is about 1 in 50 (choice D). Choice C (1/100) is the trap of reporting q itself and forgetting the factor of 2p. Choice A (1/10,000) is q^2, the frequency of AFFECTED females (and it wrongly treats the locus as autosomal by squaring the male incidence). Choice E (1/25) roughly doubles the correct value, and choice B (1/200) halves q. The distinguishing move is recognizing that for X-linked traits q comes straight from affected males."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Physiology (renal system)","stem":"A drug selectively blocks the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter on the luminal (apical) membrane of the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. Assuming circulating ADH levels remain normal, what is the most likely effect on the medullary interstitial osmotic gradient and on the urine?","choices":["The medullary gradient increases, producing more concentrated urine","The medullary gradient dissipates, so the collecting duct reabsorbs less water and the urine becomes more dilute and greater in volume","There is no change in urine, because water reabsorption is controlled solely by ADH acting on the collecting duct","Urine becomes more concentrated, because blocking solute reabsorption leaves more solute in the tubule to draw water out","The gradient is unaffected, but blood volume rises as water is retained"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The thick ascending limb is water-impermeable and uses the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter to pump solute into the medullary interstitium; this is the engine of the countercurrent multiplier that builds the hyperosmotic medullary gradient. That gradient is what lets the collecting duct osmotically reabsorb water when ADH opens its aquaporins. Blocking the cotransporter removes the solute deposition, so the medullary gradient collapses; even with normal ADH there is no osmotic driving force to pull water out, so a large volume of dilute urine is produced (choice B) - this is exactly how loop diuretics work. Choice C is the trap of thinking ADH alone determines water reabsorption, ignoring that ADH needs the medullary gradient to act on. Choices A, D, and E get the direction of the gradient or of fluid balance backwards."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Moles & stoichiometry","stem":"Butane burns by 2 C4H10 + 13 O2 -> 8 CO2 + 10 H2O. A burner is fed 29.0 g of C4H10 (molar mass 58.12 g/mol) and 100.0 g of O2 (molar mass 32.00 g/mol). If the reaction proceeds at 85.0% yield, what mass of CO2 (molar mass 44.01 g/mol) is produced?","choices":["74.7 g","87.9 g","71.9 g","84.6 g","61.2 g"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Moles: butane = 29.0/58.12 = 0.499 mol; O2 = 100.0/32.00 = 3.125 mol. The equation needs O2:butane = 13:2 = 6.5, so 0.499 mol butane would require 0.499 x 6.5 = 3.24 mol O2, but only 3.125 mol O2 is present -> O2 is limiting (the trap is assuming the hydrocarbon limits). CO2 = 3.125 x (8/13) = 1.923 mol; theoretical mass = 1.923 x 44.01 = 84.6 g; at 85.0% yield = 71.9 g. Distractors: 74.7 g wrongly treats butane as limiting (0.499 x 4 x 44.01 x 0.85); 87.9 g is butane-limiting with no yield step; 84.6 g is the correct limiting reagent but forgets the 85% yield; 61.2 g applies the 0.85 factor twice (84.6 x 0.85^2)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Atomic structure & quantum numbers","stem":"In a ground-state atom of zinc (Z = 30, electron configuration 1s2 2s2 2p6 3s2 3p6 4s2 3d10), how many electrons have the magnetic quantum number ml = 0?","choices":["14","7","6","8","30"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Every subshell in Zn is completely filled, so each orbital (each ml value) holds 2 electrons. The ml = 0 orbitals are: 1s, 2s, 3s, 4s (4 s-orbitals), one ml = 0 orbital in each of 2p and 3p (2 orbitals), and one ml = 0 orbital in 3d (1 orbital) = 7 orbitals total. Since all are doubly occupied, electrons with ml = 0 = 7 x 2 = 14. Distractors: 7 counts orbitals, not electrons; 6 counts only the p and d ml = 0 electrons (4 + 2) and forgets that every s orbital is ml = 0; 8 counts only the s electrons; 30 is the total electron count (all Z)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Periodic trends","stem":"Using standard periodic trends (including the well-known irregularities within a period), rank these third-period elements in order of INCREASING first ionization energy: Mg, Al, Si, P, S.","choices":["Mg < Al < Si < P < S","Al < Mg < Si < P < S","Mg < Al < Si < S < P","Al < Mg < Si < S < P","S < P < Si < Al < Mg"],"correct":3,"explanation":"First ionization energy rises left-to-right but has two dips in period 3. Al (578 kJ/mol) is LOWER than Mg (738) because Al's electron leaves a higher-energy, less-penetrating 3p orbital while Mg has a filled, stable 3s2. S (1000) is LOWER than P (1012) because P has a stable half-filled 3p3 and S must place a fourth p-electron into an already-occupied orbital (pairing repulsion). So increasing order is Al < Mg < Si < S < P. Distractors: Mg < Al < Si < P < S is the naive monotonic trend (both anomalies missed); Al < Mg < Si < P < S catches only the Al/Mg dip; Mg < Al < Si < S < P catches only the S/P dip; S < P < Si < Al < Mg is essentially reversed (atomic-size ordering)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Bonding, Lewis & VSEPR","stem":"Consider the tetrachloroiodate ion, ICl4^- (iodine is the central atom; valence electrons: I = 7, each Cl = 7, plus 1 for the negative charge). Which description gives the correct molecular geometry, central-atom hybridization, and overall polarity?","choices":["Tetrahedral, sp3, nonpolar","Square planar, sp3d2, nonpolar","See-saw, sp3d, polar","Square planar, sp3d2, polar","Octahedral, sp3d2, nonpolar"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Total valence electrons = 7 + 4(7) + 1 = 36. Four I-Cl bonds use 8 electrons; the four Cl atoms complete octets with 24 lone-pair electrons (32 used), leaving 36 - 32 = 4 electrons = 2 lone pairs on iodine. Central I has 4 bonding domains + 2 lone pairs = 6 electron domains -> octahedral electron geometry, sp3d2. The two lone pairs occupy trans (axial) positions, giving a square planar molecular shape, which is symmetric -> nonpolar. Distractors: tetrahedral/sp3/nonpolar ignores the two lone pairs (treats it like CH4); see-saw/sp3d/polar miscounts to only 1 lone pair (5 domains); square planar/sp3d2/polar has the right shape but wrongly calls the symmetric molecule polar; octahedral/sp3d2/nonpolar confuses the electron geometry with the molecular geometry."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Gas laws","stem":"A rigid 5.00 L vessel at 500 K is charged with 1.00 mol N2 and 2.40 mol H2. The reaction N2 + 3 H2 -> 2 NH3 goes until the limiting reagent is completely consumed. Using R = 0.0821 L*atm/(mol*K), what is the final total pressure in the vessel?","choices":["13.1 atm","27.9 atm","21.3 atm","16.4 atm","14.8 atm"],"correct":4,"explanation":"Check the limiting reagent: 1.00 mol N2 would need 3.00 mol H2, but only 2.40 mol H2 is present, so H2 limits. H2 consumed = 2.40 mol -> N2 consumed = 2.40/3 = 0.800 mol and NH3 formed = 2.40 x (2/3) = 1.60 mol. Leftover N2 = 1.00 - 0.800 = 0.200 mol; H2 = 0. Final moles = 0.200 + 1.60 = 1.80 mol. P = nRT/V = 1.80 x 0.0821 x 500 / 5.00 = 14.8 atm. Distractors: 13.1 atm counts only the 1.60 mol NH3 (forgets leftover N2); 27.9 atm assumes moles are unchanged (uses the initial 3.40 mol); 21.3 atm forgets to subtract the consumed N2 (uses 1.00 + 1.60 = 2.60 mol); 16.4 atm assumes both reactants are fully consumed to 2.00 mol NH3."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Thermochemistry","stem":"Estimate the enthalpy change (delta H) for C2H4(g) + H2(g) -> C2H6(g) from average bond enthalpies (kJ/mol): C=C 614, C-C 348, C-H 413, H-H 436.","choices":["-124 kJ","+124 kJ","-390 kJ","-560 kJ","-826 kJ"],"correct":0,"explanation":"delta H = (bonds broken) - (bonds formed). Reactants: C2H4 has 1 C=C + 4 C-H, and H2 has 1 H-H -> 614 + 4(413) + 436 = 2702 kJ broken. Products: C2H6 has 1 C-C + 6 C-H -> 348 + 6(413) = 2826 kJ formed. delta H = 2702 - 2826 = -124 kJ. Distractors: +124 kJ reverses the sign (formed minus broken); -390 kJ uses the C=C value (614) for the product's C-C single bond (formed = 3092); -560 kJ forgets to break the H-H bond (broken = 2266); -826 kJ makes both errors (broken 2266 with formed 3092)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Kinetics","stem":"For A + B -> products, initial-rate data (rate in M/s) are: Exp 1: [A]=0.10, [B]=0.10, rate=2.0x10^-3; Exp 2: [A]=0.20, [B]=0.10, rate=8.0x10^-3; Exp 3: [A]=0.20, [B]=0.20, rate=1.6x10^-2. What is the rate constant k, including units?","choices":["0.20 M^-1 s^-1","20 M^-3 s^-1","2.0 M^-2 s^-1","2.0 M^-1 s^-1","0.020 M^-2 s^-1"],"correct":2,"explanation":"From Exp 1 to 2, [A] doubles and rate quadruples -> second order in A. From Exp 2 to 3, [B] doubles and rate doubles -> first order in B. Rate = k[A]^2[B], overall order 3, so k has units M^(1-3) s^-1 = M^-2 s^-1. Using Exp 1: k = 2.0x10^-3 / ((0.10)^2 x 0.10) = 2.0x10^-3 / 1.0x10^-3 = 2.0 M^-2 s^-1. Distractors: 0.20 M^-1 s^-1 assumes first order in both (overall 2); 20 M^-3 s^-1 assumes second order in B (divides by 1x10^-4); 2.0 M^-1 s^-1 has the right number but wrong units (overall order miscounted); 0.020 M^-2 s^-1 is a power-of-ten arithmetic slip."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Equilibrium","stem":"For N2O4(g) <=> 2 NO2(g), Kc = 0.125 at a given temperature. A 2.00 L flask is charged with 1.00 mol N2O4 and no NO2. What is the equilibrium concentration of NO2?","choices":["0.250 M","0.221 M","0.110 M","0.354 M","0.390 M"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Initial [N2O4] = 1.00 mol / 2.00 L = 0.500 M. ICE: [N2O4] = 0.500 - x, [NO2] = 2x. Kc = (2x)^2/(0.500 - x) = 0.125 -> 4x^2 = 0.0625 - 0.125x -> 4x^2 + 0.125x - 0.0625 = 0. Quadratic: x = (-0.125 + sqrt(0.015625 + 1.0))/8 = (-0.125 + 1.0078)/8 = 0.1103. [NO2] = 2x = 0.221 M. The small-x approximation is invalid here (x would be 25% of 0.500). Distractors: 0.250 M uses the unvalidated small-x approximation (4x^2/0.500 = 0.125); 0.110 M reports x instead of 2x; 0.354 M forgets to divide moles by the 2.00 L volume (uses 1.00 M with the approximation); 0.390 M reports the leftover [N2O4] (0.500 - 0.110) instead of [NO2]."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Acids, bases & pH","stem":"Calculate the pH of the solution formed by mixing 50.0 mL of 0.200 M acetic acid (CH3COOH, Ka = 1.8x10^-5) with 30.0 mL of 0.100 M NaOH.","choices":["5.11","4.74","2.90","4.38","8.66"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Moles acid = 0.0500 x 0.200 = 0.0100 mol; moles NaOH = 0.0300 x 0.100 = 0.00300 mol. NaOH converts 0.00300 mol acid to acetate, leaving 0.00700 mol CH3COOH and 0.00300 mol CH3COO- -> a buffer. pKa = -log(1.8x10^-5) = 4.74. pH = pKa + log([A-]/[HA]) = 4.74 + log(0.00300/0.00700) = 4.74 + (-0.368) = 4.38 (mole ratio is fine since both share the same total volume). Distractors: 5.11 inverts the ratio to log(HA/A-); 4.74 assumes pH = pKa (equimolar buffer); 2.90 ignores the acetate and treats it as pure 0.0875 M weak acid; 8.66 wrongly assumes all the acid was neutralized and computes pH from acetate hydrolysis alone (as if at equivalence)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Electrochemistry","stem":"A constant current of 5.00 A is passed through molten AlCl3 for exactly 1.00 hour. What mass of aluminum is deposited at the cathode? (Al3+ + 3 e- -> Al; F = 96485 C/mol e-; molar mass Al = 26.98 g/mol.)","choices":["1.68 g","5.03 g","2.52 g","15.1 g","0.0280 g"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Charge Q = I x t = 5.00 A x 3600 s = 18000 C. Moles of electrons = 18000 / 96485 = 0.1866 mol e-. Since Al3+ + 3 e- -> Al, moles Al = 0.1866 / 3 = 0.0622 mol. Mass = 0.0622 x 26.98 = 1.68 g. Distractors: 5.03 g forgets the 3-electron requirement (uses n = 1); 2.52 g uses n = 2; 15.1 g multiplies by 3 instead of dividing by 3; 0.0280 g uses t = 60 s (treats minutes as seconds, forgetting to convert the hour to 3600 s)."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IUPAC nomenclature","stem":"Give the correct IUPAC name for the hydrocarbon shown (the up branch is a methyl group; the down branch is an ethyl group on the main chain).","choices":["4-ethyl-6-methyloctane","3-ethyl-5-methyloctane","5-ethyl-3-methyloctane","3-methyl-5-ethyloctane","5-ethyl-3-methylnonane"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The longest continuous chain is 8 carbons (octane); going into the ethyl branch only gives a 7-carbon chain. Numbering from the left gives the locant set {3-methyl, 5-ethyl} = {3,5}; from the right it is {4,6}. At the first point of difference 3 < 4, so number from the left, and cite substituents alphabetically (ethyl before methyl): 5-ethyl-3-methyloctane. The trap '4-ethyl-6-methyloctane' numbers from the far end to give the alphabetically first substituent (ethyl) the lower number, but that tiebreaker only applies when the locant SETS are tied; here {3,5} beats {4,6} outright. '3-ethyl-5-methyloctane' swaps the two branch identities, '3-methyl-5-ethyloctane' has correct locants but lists substituents out of alphabetical order, and 'nonane' miscounts the parent chain as 9 carbons.","figureSvg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 280 135\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><polyline points=\"25,75 55,55 85,75 115,55 145,75 175,55 205,75 235,55\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"45\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polyline points=\"145,75 145,105 172,122\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/></svg>"},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Stereochemistry (R/S, chirality)","stem":"Assign the absolute configuration at C2 and C3 of the aldotetrose shown in the Fischer projection below.","choices":["(2S, 3R)","(2R, 3S)","(2R, 3R)","(2S, 3S)","The molecule is meso, so it is achiral and carries no R/S descriptors"],"correct":1,"explanation":"In a Fischer projection horizontal bonds point TOWARD the viewer, so when the lowest-priority group (H) is horizontal you must reverse the apparent rotation. At C2: OH is #1; the CHO branch (duplicated set O,O,H) outranks the C3 branch (O,C,H), so CHO is #2 and C3 is #3; H (#4) is horizontal. The 1->2->3 path OH(right)->CHO(up)->C3(down) traces counterclockwise, and reversing (H toward viewer) gives R. At C3: OH is #1; the C2 branch (O,C,H) outranks CH2OH (O,H,H); H (#4) is horizontal. The path OH(left)->C2(up)->CH2OH(down) is clockwise, and reversing gives S. Thus (2R,3S). '(2S,3R)' is the enantiomer you get if you forget to reverse for the toward-viewer H at BOTH centers. The molecule is NOT meso: the two ends (CHO vs CH2OH) differ, so there is no internal mirror plane.","figureSvg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Georgia, serif\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"142\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"72\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"128\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"72\" y1=\"104\" x2=\"128\" y2=\"104\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\">CHO</text><text x=\"66\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"end\">H</text><text x=\"134\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"start\">OH</text><text x=\"66\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"end\">HO</text><text x=\"134\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"start\">H</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\">CH2OH</text></svg>"},{"section":"ochem","topic":"SN1/SN2/E1/E2","stem":"3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol (pinacolyl alcohol), (CH3)3C-CH(OH)-CH3, is heated with concentrated H2SO4. What is the major organic product?","choices":["3,3-dimethyl-1-butene","2,3-dimethyl-2-butene","2,3-dimethyl-1-butene","2-methyl-2-butene","No reaction; the alcohol is recovered unchanged"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Hot concentrated acid drives E1 dehydration. Protonation and loss of water give a secondary carbocation at C2. On the unrearranged skeleton the only beta-hydrogen is on C1 (C3 is quaternary), which would give 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (the tempting distractor). Instead, a methyl migrates from C3 to form the far more stable tertiary carbocation (the 2,3-dimethylbut-2-yl cation), and Zaitsev elimination gives the tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene. '2,3-dimethyl-1-butene' is the rearranged but less-substituted (anti-Zaitsev) alkene; '2-methyl-2-butene' has the wrong number of carbons (C5, not C6). Secondary cations readily form under acid and heat, so 'no reaction' is wrong."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alkene addition reactions","stem":"3-Methyl-1-butene, CH2=CH-CH(CH3)2, is treated with HBr in the absence of peroxides. What is the major product?","choices":["2-bromo-3-methylbutane","1-bromo-3-methylbutane","2-bromo-2-methylbutane","1-bromo-2-methylbutane","An equimolar mixture of 2-bromo-2-methylbutane and 2-bromo-3-methylbutane"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Without peroxides, HBr adds by the ionic Markovnikov pathway. Protonation of the terminal carbon gives a secondary carbocation at C2; a hydride shift from the adjacent C-H produces the more stable tertiary carbocation, which bromide traps to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane as the major product. '2-bromo-3-methylbutane' is the unrearranged (minor) product. '1-bromo-3-methylbutane' is the anti-Markovnikov product, which requires peroxides/radical conditions that are explicitly absent. Because the tertiary rearranged cation dominates, the 'equimolar mixture' option is wrong."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Aromaticity & EAS","stem":"Which of the following species is aromatic?","choices":["Cyclooctatetraene (neutral)","Cyclobutadiene","Cyclopentadienyl cation","Cyclooctatetraene dianion","Cycloheptatrienyl (tropylium) anion"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Aromaticity requires a planar, cyclic, fully conjugated ring with 4n+2 pi electrons. Adding two electrons to cyclooctatetraene gives the dianion with 10 pi electrons (4n+2, n=2); it flattens to a planar ring and is aromatic. Neutral cyclooctatetraene has 8 pi electrons and puckers into a non-planar tub, so it is nonaromatic. Cyclobutadiene (4 pi) and the cyclopentadienyl cation (4 pi) are antiaromatic. The cycloheptatrienyl ANION has 8 pi electrons and is antiaromatic; it is the cycloheptatrienyl CATION (tropylium, 6 pi) that is aromatic, making this a classic charge-sign trap."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alcohols & carbonyls","stem":"Methyl 4-oxopentanoate (methyl levulinate), CH3COCH2CH2CO2CH3, is treated with excess CH3MgBr, then worked up with aqueous acid. What is the product?","choices":["2,5-dimethylhexane-2,5-diol","methyl 4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanoate","5-hydroxy-5-methylhexan-2-one","pentane-1,4-diol","2,4-dimethylpentane-2,4-diol"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Excess Grignard reacts with BOTH carbonyls. The ester consumes two equivalents (the OCH3 leaves and a tertiary alcohol bearing two new methyls forms), while the ketone consumes one equivalent to give a tertiary alcohol. Both carbonyl carbons become C(CH3)2OH, separated by the two CH2 groups: 2,5-dimethylhexane-2,5-diol. 'Methyl 4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanoate' assumes only the ketone reacts. '5-hydroxy-5-methylhexan-2-one' assumes the ester stops at a ketone while the true ketone is left alone. 'Pentane-1,4-diol' treats CH3MgBr as a hydride reducer (adds H, not CH3). '2,4-dimethylpentane-2,4-diol' comes from miscounting the chain as having only one CH2 between the carbonyls."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Acidity & pKa","stem":"Which compound has the most acidic C-H bond?","choices":["Propyne (a terminal alkyne)","Toluene","Cyclopenta-1,3-diene (cyclopentadiene)","Cyclopentene","Cyclopentane"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Deprotonating cyclopentadiene gives the cyclopentadienyl anion, which is AROMATIC (6 pi electrons, 4n+2). That aromatic stabilization of the conjugate base makes the sp3 C-H of cyclopentadiene remarkably acidic (pKa ~16) despite it looking like an ordinary hydrocarbon. The tempting trap is propyne: an sp-hybridized terminal alkyne C-H is the most acidic among ordinary hydrocarbons (pKa ~25), but that is still far weaker than cyclopentadiene. Benzylic (toluene, ~43) and allylic (cyclopentene, ~44) C-H bonds are resonance-stabilized but not aromatic, and cyclopentane (~50) is the least acidic."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Resonance & stability","stem":"Which of the following carbocations is the most stable?","choices":["(CH3)3C+  (tert-butyl cation)","CH3-O-CH2+  (methoxymethyl cation)","(CH3)2CH+  (isopropyl cation)","CH3-CO-CH2+  (2-oxopropyl cation)","CH3CH2+  (ethyl cation)"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The methoxymethyl cation is stabilized by donation of an oxygen lone pair, giving an all-octet resonance contributor (CH3-O+=CH2). This lone-pair (pi) donation from the heteroatom outweighs oxygen's inductive electron withdrawal and makes the cation more stable than even a tertiary alkyl cation. The tert-butyl cation (3 degrees, hyperconjugation only) is the classic trap. The 2-oxopropyl cation is actually the LEAST stable: its empty orbital sits next to the electron-poor carbonyl carbon, so there is no favorable resonance and the carbonyl withdraws electron density inductively (a 'resonance illusion' distractor). Isopropyl (2 degrees) and ethyl (1 degree) are simple, poorly stabilized cations."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IR & H-NMR spectroscopy","stem":"A compound with molecular formula C9H10O2 shows a strong IR band at 1738 cm-1 and NO broad absorption in the 2500-3300 cm-1 region. Its 1H NMR shows: delta 7.35 (5H, multiplet), delta 5.10 (2H, singlet), delta 2.07 (3H, singlet). What is the structure?","choices":["ethyl benzoate","methyl phenylacetate","benzyl acetate","phenyl propanoate","3-phenylpropanoic acid"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Degrees of unsaturation = (2x9+2-10)/2 = 5, consistent with a benzene ring (4) plus one C=O (1). The 1738 cm-1 band with no broad O-H means an ester, not a carboxylic acid, which eliminates 3-phenylpropanoic acid. The two singlets are decisive: a 2H singlet at 5.10 is an OCH2 flanked by a phenyl ring and an ester oxygen, and the 3H singlet at 2.07 is an acetate CH3-C=O. Both match benzyl acetate (PhCH2-O-CO-CH3). Methyl phenylacetate also gives two singlets but near 3.6/3.7 ppm, not 5.10/2.07. Ethyl benzoate and phenyl propanoate each contain an ethyl group, which would appear as a quartet plus a triplet, contradicting the observed two singlets."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Synthesis / roadmap","stem":"Which reagent sequence best converts 1-methylcyclohexene into 2-methylcyclohexanone?","choices":["1) aqueous H2SO4;  2) PCC","1) BH3.THF;  2) H2O2, NaOH;  3) PCC","1) Hg(OAc)2, H2O;  2) NaBH4;  3) Na2Cr2O7, H2SO4","1) BH3.THF;  2) H2O2, NaOH","1) O3;  2) (CH3)2S"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To make the ketone at C2 you must place the OH on the LESS substituted carbon (C2), which requires anti-Markovnikov hydration. Hydroboration-oxidation (BH3 then H2O2/NaOH) delivers OH to C2 as a secondary alcohol, and PCC then oxidizes it to 2-methylcyclohexanone. Both Markovnikov routes are traps: aqueous acid and oxymercuration-reduction put the OH on the more substituted C1, giving a tertiary alcohol that CANNOT be oxidized to a ketone. Stopping after H2O2/NaOH yields only the alcohol, not the ketone. Ozonolysis cleaves the ring double bond entirely, giving a ring-opened keto-aldehyde (6-oxoheptanal), not the target."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Fractions & decimals","stem":"Evaluate the expression below and give the result in lowest terms, where a bar denotes a repeating decimal:\n\n(0.̶36 + 1/6) ÷ 0.̶45\n\n(Here 0.̶36 = 0.363636... and 0.̶45 = 0.454545...)","choices":["7/6","6/7","4/5","11/17","35/66"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Convert the repeating decimals to fractions: 0.̶36 = 36/99 = 4/11 and 0.̶45 = 45/99 = 5/11. Then 4/11 + 1/6 = 24/66 + 11/66 = 35/66. Dividing by 5/11 means multiplying by 11/5: (35/66)(11/5) = 385/330 = 7/6. Distractor 6/7 comes from inverting the final result (dividing in the wrong direction). 4/5 comes from skipping the +1/6 term and computing only (4/11)÷(5/11). 11/17 comes from adding fractions incorrectly (4/11 + 1/6 -> 5/17 by summing numerators and denominators), then dividing. 35/66 is the value after adding but before dividing by 0.̶45 (stopping one step early)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Algebra & equations","stem":"Real numbers x and y satisfy x + y = 5 and x^3 + y^3 = 35. What is the value of xy?","choices":["6","30","90","6.25","-6"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Use the identity x^3 + y^3 = (x + y)^3 - 3xy(x + y). Substituting: 35 = 5^3 - 3xy(5) = 125 - 15xy, so 15xy = 90 and xy = 6. (The pair is real: t^2 - 5t + 6 = 0 gives t = 2, 3, and 2^3 + 3^3 = 35.) Distractor 30 comes from dropping the (x + y) factor on the 3xy term (using 125 - 3xy = 35). Distractor 90 is 15xy, reported without the final division by 15. Distractor 6.25 assumes x = y = 2.5. Distractor -6 comes from a sign error (writing 125 + 15xy = 35)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Ratios & proportions","stem":"In a collection of items, the ratio A : B = 3 : 4 and the ratio B : C = 6 : 7. If A + B + C = 210, how many items are in group C?","choices":["84","105","72","54","60"],"correct":0,"explanation":"B appears in both ratios with different values (4 and 6), so scale to a common B using LCM 12: A : B = 9 : 12 and B : C = 12 : 14, giving A : B : C = 9 : 12 : 14 (sum 35 parts). Then 210 / 35 = 6 per part, so C = 14 x 6 = 84. Distractor 105 comes from naively chaining as A : B : C = 3 : 4 : 7 (sum 14), giving C = 7 x 15 = 105 without equalizing B. Distractor 72 is B (12 x 6), and 54 is A (9 x 6) — the correct part value but the wrong group. Distractor 60 is B under the naive chain (4 x 15)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Percentages","stem":"Over four consecutive trading days a stock's closing price changes by +20%, then -15%, then +25%, then -10%, with each change applied to the previous day's closing price. Compared with its starting price, the price after the four days is:","choices":["up 14.75%","up 20%","up 27.5%","up 2%","up 15%"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Multiply the growth factors: 1.20 x 0.85 x 1.25 x 0.90. Step by step: 1.20 x 0.85 = 1.02; 1.02 x 1.25 = 1.275; 1.275 x 0.90 = 1.1475, which is up 14.75%. Distractor up 20% comes from simply adding the four percentages (+20 - 15 + 25 - 10). Distractor up 27.5% omits the final -10% step (1.20 x 0.85 x 1.25 = 1.275). Distractor up 2% applies only the first two changes (1.20 x 0.85 = 1.02). Distractor up 15% is the 'eyeball' trap of rounding the compounded figure to a clean number instead of multiplying exactly."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Exponents & roots","stem":"Suppose 2^x = 3 and 2^y = 5. What is the value of 2^(3x - 2y)?","choices":["27/25","25/27","2","1/2","9/10"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Rewrite: 2^(3x - 2y) = (2^x)^3 / (2^y)^2 = 3^3 / 5^2 = 27/25. Distractor 25/27 inverts the exponent (computing 2^(2y - 3x)). Distractor 2 comes from subtracting the two values, 27 - 25, instead of dividing. Distractor 1/2 is the classic trap of misreading 2^x = 3 as x = 3 and 2^y = 5 as y = 5, giving 2^(9 - 10) = 2^(-1). Distractor 9/10 mixes bases and exponents as (3 x 3)/(2 x 5)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Geometry (area/volume)","stem":"A sphere is inscribed in a right circular cylinder so that it touches the cylinder's curved surface and both circular bases. The cylinder's volume is 54π. What is the volume of the sphere?","choices":["36π","72π","27π","18π","12π"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Because the sphere touches the sides and both bases, the cylinder's radius equals the sphere's radius r and its height equals the sphere's diameter 2r. Cylinder volume = πr^2(2r) = 2πr^3 = 54π, so r^3 = 27 and r = 3. Sphere volume = (4/3)πr^3 = (4/3)π(27) = 36π. Distractor 72π uses height = r instead of 2r, giving r^3 = 54. Distractor 27π uses a sphere-to-cylinder ratio of 1:2 instead of the correct 2:3. Distractor 18π treats it like an inscribed cone (1/3 of the cylinder). Distractor 12π uses r^2 instead of r^3 in the sphere formula, (4/3)π(9).","figureSvg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 160 170\" width=\"160\" height=\"170\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"><ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"35\" rx=\"50\" ry=\"13\"/><ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"135\" rx=\"50\" ry=\"13\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"135\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"135\"/><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"85\" r=\"50\"/></svg>"},{"section":"qr","topic":"Trigonometry","stem":"An acute angle θ satisfies sinθ + cosθ = 7/5. What is the value of tanθ + cotθ?","choices":["25/12","25/24","12/25","50/49","7/5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Square the given equation: (sinθ + cosθ)^2 = sin^2θ + cos^2θ + 2sinθcosθ = 1 + 2sinθcosθ = 49/25. So 2sinθcosθ = 24/25 and sinθcosθ = 12/25. Then tanθ + cotθ = (sin^2θ + cos^2θ)/(sinθcosθ) = 1/(sinθcosθ) = 25/12. (Consistent acute solution: sinθ, cosθ = 4/5, 3/5.) Distractor 25/24 forgets to halve (treats 2sinθcosθ = 24/25 as sinθcosθ before inverting). Distractor 12/25 is sinθcosθ itself, forgetting that tanθ + cotθ is its reciprocal. Distractor 50/49 omits the +1 in the identity (using 49/25 = 2sinθcosθ, so sinθcosθ = 49/50). Distractor 7/5 just restates sinθ + cosθ."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Probability","stem":"A bag contains 5 red marbles and 4 blue marbles. Three marbles are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that exactly two of the three drawn are red?","choices":["10/21","25/42","5/42","10/63","20/21"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Count with combinations: total ways = C(9,3) = 84. Favorable = choose 2 of 5 red and 1 of 4 blue = C(5,2) x C(4,1) = 10 x 4 = 40. Probability = 40/84 = 10/21. Distractor 25/42 answers 'at least two red' (40 + C(5,3) = 50, over 84). Distractor 5/42 forgets to choose the one blue marble, using C(5,2)/C(9,3) = 10/84. Distractor 10/63 computes a single ordering (5/9)(4/8)(4/7) but forgets there are 3 arrangements of RRB. Distractor 20/21 is the mistaken complement of 1/21."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Statistics (mean/median/SD)","stem":"A set of five distinct positive integers has a mean of 10 and a median of 12. What is the greatest possible value of the largest element in the set?","choices":["22","23","24","21","25"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The five integers sum to 5 x 10 = 50, and the sorted middle (third) value is 12. To maximize the largest element, minimize the other four subject to the constraints. The two below the median must be the smallest distinct positive integers, 1 and 2; the fourth value must be a distinct integer greater than 12, so its minimum is 13. Then the largest = 50 - (1 + 2 + 12 + 13) = 22, realized by {1, 2, 12, 13, 22}. Distractor 23 lets the fourth value equal the median (12), violating distinctness: {1, 2, 12, 12, 23}. Distractor 24 allows 0 as a value, which is not positive: {0, 1, 12, 13, 24}. Distractor 21 wastes a value by using 1 and 3 below the median: {1, 3, 12, 13, 21}. Distractor 25 combines both errors: {0, 1, 12, 12, 25}."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Word problems (rate/work/mixture)","stem":"A 20-liter tank is filled with pure acid. First, 4 liters are drained and replaced with water and the tank is stirred. Then 4 liters of the resulting mixture are drained and again replaced with water. How many liters of pure acid remain in the tank?","choices":["12.8","12","16","10.24","0.8"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each replacement removes 4/20 = 1/5 of whatever is currently in the tank and keeps the remaining fraction 16/20 = 0.8 of the acid. After two replacements the acid is 20 x (0.8)^2 = 20 x 0.64 = 12.8 liters. Distractor 12 treats it linearly, subtracting 4 liters of acid each time (20 - 4 - 4), ignoring that the second draw is now diluted. Distractor 16 performs only one replacement (20 x 0.8). Distractor 10.24 applies the 0.8 factor three times (20 x 0.8^3), one operation too many. Distractor 0.8 squares the removed fraction instead of the remaining fraction: 20 x (4/20)^2."}]},{"n":10,"tier":10,"questions":[{"section":"bio","topic":"Biomolecules","stem":"A soluble globular enzyme functions normally in the cytosol. A single missense mutation replaces a glutamate residue (acidic, charged R group) that sits on the protein's exterior surface with a valine residue (nonpolar R group). Assuming overall folding is otherwise preserved, which consequence is MOST likely?","choices":["The primary structure is unchanged, so folding and solubility are unaffected because R groups do not influence tertiary structure.","Loss of a buried hydrophobic core contact destabilizes the folded state, causing the protein to unfold.","A hydrophobic patch now sits on the protein surface, decreasing solubility and promoting aggregation.","A new disulfide bridge forms at the mutation site, rigidifying the polypeptide backbone.","Peptide-bond geometry changes at that position, converting a local alpha helix into a beta sheet."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct (C): Surface residues on a soluble protein are normally polar/charged so they hydrogen-bond with water. Swapping a charged glutamate for nonpolar valine creates a hydrophobic patch on the exterior, which water excludes; such patches lower solubility and drive aggregation (the same logic as the Glu->Val substitution in sickle-cell hemoglobin). A wrongly claims R groups don't affect structure—they determine tertiary folding and surface chemistry. B is the reverse scenario: valine is hydrophobic, so placing it on the surface adds hydrophobicity rather than removing a core contact, and nothing is lost from the core. D fails because valine has no sulfur/thiol group, so it cannot form a disulfide bond (only cysteine can). E is wrong because a single side-chain change does not rewrite backbone secondary structure wholesale."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cell structure & organelles","stem":"A researcher wants the single strongest piece of evidence that mitochondria arose from a free-living alpha-proteobacterium engulfed by an ancestral cell (endosymbiotic theory), rather than by inward folding of the host's own plasma membrane. Which observation best distinguishes these two origins?","choices":["Mitochondria are enclosed by a double membrane.","Mitochondria contain their own circular DNA that replicates independently and 70S-type ribosomes sensitive to antibiotics that target bacterial ribosomes.","Mitochondria import most of their proteins from the surrounding cytosol.","The inner mitochondrial membrane is folded into cristae that increase surface area.","Mitochondria are the primary site of oxidative phosphorylation in the cell."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct (B): Independent circular DNA plus bacterial-type (70S) ribosomes that respond to bacterial antibiotics point specifically to a bacterial ancestor, something plasma-membrane invagination of a eukaryotic host could not produce. A is tempting but weak: simple invagination of the host membrane could also yield a double membrane, so it does not discriminate between the two hypotheses. C actually argues against organelle autonomy (most genes moved to the nucleus) and does not point to a bacterial origin. D and E describe mitochondrial function/structure related to energy production but say nothing about evolutionary origin."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Membrane transport","stem":"In an intestinal epithelial cell, glucose is moved from the low-glucose lumen into the high-glucose cytosol against its gradient by the apical SGLT1 Na+/glucose symporter. A drug is applied that specifically and completely blocks the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase. What happens to apical glucose uptake over the next several minutes, and why?","choices":["No effect, because SGLT1 hydrolyzes ATP directly to pump glucose across the apical membrane.","Uptake continues unchanged, because glucose is simply moving down its concentration gradient by facilitated diffusion.","Uptake gradually declines as the transmembrane Na+ gradient collapses, because the symporter is powered by that gradient, not by ATP directly.","Uptake immediately reverses, exporting glucose back into the lumen using the K+ gradient.","Uptake increases, because inhibiting the pump raises intracellular Na+, which drives the symporter faster."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct (C): SGLT1 is a secondary active transporter. It does not use ATP itself; it rides the inward Na+ gradient that the Na+/K+ ATPase maintains. Block the pump and the Na+ gradient runs down over time, so the energy source for uphill glucose transport fades and uptake gradually falls. A misclassifies SGLT1 as a primary active (ATP-hydrolyzing) transporter. B is wrong because glucose is being moved against its gradient (lumen low to cytosol high), which cannot be facilitated diffusion. D invents a K+-driven reversal that does not occur. E is the key trap: rising intracellular Na+ shrinks the difference between lumen and cytosol Na+, weakening (not strengthening) the driving gradient, so uptake decreases rather than increases."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Cellular respiration","stem":"A chemical uncoupler (such as 2,4-dinitrophenol) is added to actively respiring mitochondria. It makes the inner mitochondrial membrane freely permeable to protons. Compared with the untreated state, how do (1) electron transport chain activity, (2) oxygen consumption, and (3) ATP synthesis change?","choices":["ETC activity decreases, oxygen consumption decreases, ATP synthesis decreases.","ETC activity increases, oxygen consumption increases, ATP synthesis decreases.","ETC activity increases, oxygen consumption increases, ATP synthesis increases.","ETC activity is unchanged, oxygen consumption is unchanged, ATP synthesis increases.","ETC activity decreases, oxygen consumption is unchanged, ATP synthesis increases."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct (B): An uncoupler lets protons leak back across the inner membrane without passing through ATP synthase. The proton-motive force can never build up, so the ETC runs faster (it is no longer held back by a high back-pressure), and because the ETC delivers electrons to O2, oxygen consumption rises. Energy is released as heat instead of being captured, so ATP synthesis falls. A is the classic mix-up with an ETC inhibitor (like cyanide), which shuts the chain down and lowers O2 use; an uncoupler does the opposite. C wrongly assumes protons still make ATP even though they bypass ATP synthase. D and E are internally inconsistent with a collapsed gradient that cannot power ATP synthesis."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Photosynthesis","stem":"A plant that has been photosynthesizing steadily in bright light is abruptly moved into complete darkness while CO2 remains freely available. In the first few seconds after the lights go out, what happens to the concentrations of 3-phosphoglycerate (3-PGA) and ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate (RuBP) in the Calvin cycle?","choices":["Both 3-PGA and RuBP increase.","Both 3-PGA and RuBP decrease.","3-PGA increases while RuBP decreases.","3-PGA decreases while RuBP increases.","Both remain constant, because the Calvin cycle is light-independent and does not need the light reactions."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct (C): In the dark the light reactions stop supplying ATP and NADPH. RuBP carboxylation by rubisco continues briefly (CO2 is still present), producing 3-PGA, but 3-PGA can no longer be reduced to G3P without NADPH/ATP, so it piles up. Meanwhile RuBP keeps being consumed by carboxylation yet cannot be regenerated (regeneration requires ATP), so it falls. D is exactly the opposite experiment's result—removing CO2 in the light makes RuBP accumulate and 3-PGA drop; students who confuse the two manipulations pick it. E exploits the label light-independent reactions: the Calvin cycle does not directly use photons, but it depends entirely on the ATP and NADPH the light reactions provide, so it does not run normally in the dark."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Central dogma (DNA->RNA->protein)","stem":"A messenger RNA has the sequence 5'-AUG CCG UAA-3'. Which of the following correctly represents the DNA template (antisense) strand from which this mRNA was transcribed?","choices":["3'-TAC GGC ATT-5'","3'-UAC GGC AUU-5'","3'-ATT GGC TAC-5'","5'-TAC GGC ATT-3'","3'-ATG CCG TAA-5'"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct (A): The template strand is complementary and antiparallel to the mRNA. Pairing each mRNA base (A-T, U-A, G-C, C-G) and running the strand 3' to 5' opposite the 5'-to-3' mRNA gives 3'-TAC GGC ATT-5'. B uses uracil in DNA, which is wrong—DNA templates contain thymine, not U. C has the right letters but the sequence is reversed (read in the wrong direction). D has the correct base letters but is labeled 5'-to-3', ignoring the required antiparallel orientation. E is the coding (sense) strand written with the wrong polarity, mistaking the coding strand for the template."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mitosis & meiosis","stem":"A diploid organism has a chromosome number of 2n = 8. Consider one of its cells that is arrested in metaphase of meiosis II. How many chromosomes (counted as centromeres) and how many total sister chromatids are present in this cell?","choices":["4 chromosomes and 8 chromatids.","8 chromosomes and 16 chromatids.","4 chromosomes and 4 chromatids.","8 chromosomes and 8 chromatids.","2 chromosomes and 4 chromatids."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct (A): Chromosome number is counted by centromeres. Meiosis I already separated homologs, so a meiosis-II cell is haploid: n = 4 chromosomes. Sister chromatids have NOT yet separated at metaphase II (they split in anaphase II), so each of the 4 chromosomes still has 2 chromatids, giving 8 chromatids. B keeps the full diploid count, treating meiosis II like mitosis and ignoring that homologs already separated. C assumes sisters have already split, but that happens only in anaphase II. D and E miscount either the chromosome number or the chromatid total by mismatching the stage."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Mendelian genetics","stem":"Three unlinked genes assort independently. In the cross AaBbCc x AaBbCc, what fraction of the offspring are expected to be EITHER homozygous recessive at all three loci OR display the dominant phenotype at all three loci?","choices":["27/64","7/16","1/8","1/64","3/4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct (B): P(dominant phenotype at all three loci) = (3/4)^3 = 27/64. P(homozygous recessive at all three, aabbcc) = (1/4)^3 = 1/64. These outcomes are mutually exclusive, so add them: 27/64 + 1/64 = 28/64 = 7/16. A counts only the all-dominant term and forgets the OR. D counts only the all-recessive term. C misreads the question as homozygous (either AA or aa) at every locus, which would be (1/2)^3 = 1/8. E is the single-locus dominant probability (3/4) not raised to the third power."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Evolution & population genetics","stem":"In a large, randomly mating population at Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, an X-linked recessive trait is expressed in 1 out of every 25 males. What fraction of females in this population is expected to be AFFECTED (show the trait)?","choices":["1/5","1/25","about 8/100","1/625","1/50"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct (D): Males are hemizygous for X-linked genes, so a male's phenotype frequency equals the recessive allele frequency: q = 1/25 = 0.04. Females need two copies to be affected, so affected females = q^2 = (0.04)^2 = 0.0016 = 1/625. B assumes females are affected at the same rate as males, ignoring that females require two recessive alleles. C (2pq = 2(0.96)(0.04) ~ 0.077, about 8/100) is the frequency of unaffected carrier (heterozygous) females, not affected ones. A takes the square root of the male frequency, reversing the q versus q^2 relationship. E arbitrarily halves the male frequency."},{"section":"bio","topic":"Physiology (a body system)","stem":"During intense exercise, a working skeletal muscle produces large amounts of CO2, lactic acid, and heat, which accumulate in its capillary beds. How does this local environment change hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, and what is the functional benefit?","choices":["Affinity increases (curve shifts left) because high CO2 makes hemoglobin bind O2 more tightly, loading more O2 into the muscle.","Affinity decreases (curve shifts right) because of the lower pH, higher CO2, and higher temperature, so hemoglobin unloads more O2 to the active tissue.","Affinity decreases (curve shifts right), but this actually reduces oxygen delivery to the muscle.","Affinity is unchanged, because hemoglobin saturation depends only on the partial pressure of oxygen.","Affinity increases (curve shifts left), which promotes greater O2 unloading in the tissue."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct (B): This is the Bohr effect. Rising CO2 and H+ (lower pH) plus elevated temperature all lower hemoglobin's O2 affinity, shifting the dissociation curve to the right so that at a given partial pressure hemoglobin releases more O2 precisely where metabolically active tissue needs it. A gets the direction backwards: high CO2 and acid decrease affinity, not increase it. C states the right shift correctly but draws the wrong conclusion, since decreased affinity means MORE unloading, improving delivery. D ignores the Bohr effect entirely; affinity is not fixed. E is internally contradictory, since a left shift means higher affinity (tighter binding), which would reduce, not promote, unloading."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Moles & stoichiometry","stem":"28 g of N2 is combined with 9.0 g of H2 and the reaction N2 + 3H2 -> 2NH3 proceeds. If the reaction gives a 75% yield, what mass of NH3 is actually obtained? (Molar masses: N2 = 28, H2 = 2.0, NH3 = 17 g/mol)","choices":["34 g","45.3 g","25.5 g","38.3 g","51 g"],"correct":2,"explanation":"N2 = 28/28 = 1.0 mol; H2 = 9.0/2.0 = 4.5 mol. The 1:3 ratio requires 3 mol H2 per mol N2, so 1.0 mol N2 needs only 3.0 mol H2; H2 is in excess and N2 is limiting. Theoretical NH3 = 1.0 mol N2 x 2 = 2.0 mol = 34 g; at 75% yield that is 25.5 g (key). 34 g forgets to apply the yield. 45.3 g divides by 0.75 instead of multiplying (applies the percent backwards). 51 g wrongly treats H2 as limiting (4.5 x 2/3 x 17) and ignores yield; 38.3 g makes the same limiting-reagent error but then does apply the 75%."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Atomic structure & quantum numbers","stem":"In a many-electron atom, what is the maximum number of electrons that can have the quantum numbers n = 3 and ml = +1?","choices":["2","4","6","10","18"],"correct":1,"explanation":"For n = 3, the allowed subshells are l = 0 (3s), l = 1 (3p), and l = 2 (3d). The value ml = +1 exists only when l is at least 1, so it appears once in 3p and once in 3d, giving two distinct orbitals. Each orbital holds 2 electrons (ms = +1/2 and -1/2), so 4 total (key). 2 counts only the 3p orbital and forgets that 3d also contains an ml = +1 orbital. 6 wrongly counts three subshells (incorrectly including 3s, which has only ml = 0). 10 is the entire 3d subshell (ignoring the ml restriction). 18 is the entire n = 3 shell (ignoring both l and ml restrictions)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Periodic trends","stem":"Which of these second-period elements has the greatest first ionization energy?","choices":["B","Be","C","N","O"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The naive left-to-right trend predicts O (farthest right) has the highest first ionization energy, but nitrogen's half-filled 2p3 subshell is unusually stable. Removing an electron from N is therefore harder than from O, whose fourth 2p electron must pair up and suffers extra electron-electron repulsion, so N > O. For the analogous reason B < Be (removing a 2p electron of B is easier than a filled-2s electron of Be), making Be a second trap. The order is N > O > C > Be > B, so the greatest is N (key). O is the classic wrong pick from applying the trend without the half-filled-stability exception."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Bonding, Lewis & VSEPR","stem":"Which of the following molecules is nonpolar?","choices":["SF4","ClF3","XeF4","NF3","BrF5"],"correct":2,"explanation":"XeF4 has 6 electron domains on Xe (4 bonding pairs + 2 lone pairs). In the octahedral electron geometry the two lone pairs occupy trans positions, forcing the four Xe-F bonds into a square-planar shape whose bond dipoles cancel exactly, so the molecule is nonpolar (key). SF4 is see-saw (one lone pair) and does not cancel, so it is polar despite looking superficially like symmetric CF4. ClF3 is T-shaped (polar), NF3 is trigonal pyramidal (polar), and BrF5 is square pyramidal (polar). Only the two-lone-pair, square-planar XeF4 is symmetric enough to be nonpolar."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Gas laws","stem":"A rigid 2.0 L flask holds N2 at 3.0 atm and a rigid 1.0 L flask holds O2 at 6.0 atm, both at the same temperature. The flasks are connected by a valve that is opened, giving a total volume of 3.0 L at constant temperature. What is the total pressure of the mixture?","choices":["4.0 atm","4.5 atm","6.0 atm","8.0 atm","9.0 atm"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Each gas expands into the full 3.0 L (Boyle's law), then Dalton's law sums the partial pressures. N2: 3.0 atm x (2.0/3.0) = 2.0 atm; O2: 6.0 atm x (1.0/3.0) = 2.0 atm; total = 4.0 atm (key). 9.0 atm simply adds the original pressures and ignores the volume change. 4.5 atm naively averages 3 and 6. 8.0 atm expands only N2 (to 2 atm) while leaving O2 at its original 6 atm. 6.0 atm just takes the larger of the two initial pressures."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Thermochemistry","stem":"Given: (1) C(s) + O2(g) -> CO2(g), dH = -394 kJ; (2) H2(g) + 1/2 O2(g) -> H2O(l), dH = -286 kJ; (3) C2H2(g) + 5/2 O2(g) -> 2 CO2(g) + H2O(l), dH = -1300 kJ. What is dH for 2 C(s) + H2(g) -> C2H2(g)?","choices":["-2374 kJ","-1980 kJ","-226 kJ","+226 kJ","+620 kJ"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Combine as 2 x (1) + (2) - (3): 2(-394) + (-286) - (-1300) = -788 - 286 + 1300 = +226 kJ (key). Reaction (3) must be reversed (sign flipped) because C2H2 is a product in the target, and reaction (1) must be doubled to supply 2 C. +620 forgets to double reaction (1) (-394 - 286 + 1300). -1980 uses (3) without reversing its sign and also fails to double (1) (-394 - 286 - 1300). -2374 doubles (1) correctly but forgets to reverse (3) (-788 - 286 - 1300). -226 has the correct magnitude but the wrong overall sign (target written backward)."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Kinetics","stem":"For A + B -> products at constant temperature: Exp 1: [A]=0.10 M, [B]=0.10 M, rate = 2.0 x 10^-3 M/s. Exp 2: [A]=0.20 M, [B]=0.10 M, rate = 8.0 x 10^-3 M/s. Exp 3: [A]=0.10 M, [B]=0.20 M, rate = 2.0 x 10^-3 M/s. What is the initial rate when [A]=0.30 M and [B]=0.30 M?","choices":["2.0 x 10^-3 M/s","6.0 x 10^-3 M/s","1.8 x 10^-2 M/s","5.4 x 10^-2 M/s","1.8 x 10^-1 M/s"],"correct":2,"explanation":"From Exp 1 to Exp 2, doubling [A] quadruples the rate, so the reaction is second order in A. From Exp 1 to Exp 3, doubling [B] leaves the rate unchanged, so it is zero order in B. Thus rate = k[A]^2 with k = 2.0 x 10^-3 / (0.10)^2 = 0.20 M^-1 s^-1. At [A] = 0.30 M: rate = 0.20 x (0.30)^2 = 1.8 x 10^-2 M/s (key); [B] is irrelevant. 5.4 x 10^-2 wrongly treats B as first order. 6.0 x 10^-3 treats A as first order (just tripling the base rate). 2.0 x 10^-3 assumes the rate is unchanged (zero order overall). 1.8 x 10^-1 is an order-of-magnitude error in the rate constant."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Equilibrium","stem":"The Ksp of PbCl2 is 1.6 x 10^-5. What is the molar solubility of PbCl2 in a 0.10 M NaCl solution?","choices":["1.6 x 10^-4 M","4.0 x 10^-4 M","8.0 x 10^-4 M","1.6 x 10^-3 M","1.6 x 10^-2 M"],"correct":3,"explanation":"PbCl2 <-> Pb2+ + 2 Cl-, so Ksp = [Pb2+][Cl-]^2. With the common ion, [Cl-] is fixed at about 0.10 M (2s is negligible next to 0.10), giving Ksp = s(0.10)^2 = 0.010 s = 1.6 x 10^-5, so s = 1.6 x 10^-3 M (key). 1.6 x 10^-2 ignores the common ion entirely (that is the pure-water solubility from 4s^3 = Ksp). 1.6 x 10^-4 forgets to square [Cl-]. 8.0 x 10^-4 wrongly divides by the stoichiometric coefficient 2. 4.0 x 10^-4 doubles the chloride to 0.20 M before using it."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Acids, bases & pH","stem":"A 1.0 L buffer contains 0.20 mol acetic acid and 0.20 mol acetate (pKa = 4.74). If 0.050 mol of solid NaOH is dissolved in it with no change in volume, what is the resulting pH?","choices":["4.52","4.74","4.87","4.96","12.70"],"correct":3,"explanation":"NaOH is a strong base that converts acetic acid into acetate: acid = 0.20 - 0.05 = 0.15 mol, acetate = 0.20 + 0.05 = 0.25 mol. Henderson-Hasselbalch: pH = 4.74 + log(0.25/0.15) = 4.74 + 0.22 = 4.96 (key). 4.87 subtracts 0.05 from the acid but forgets to add the same 0.05 to the base. 4.74 ignores the NaOH and uses the initial 1:1 ratio. 4.52 reverses the shift, treating the addition as if it were acid (log(0.15/0.25)). 12.70 ignores the buffer entirely and treats the 0.05 mol OH- as free strong base."},{"section":"gchem","topic":"Electrochemistry","stem":"Consider the cell Zn(s) | Zn2+ (0.10 M) || Cu2+ (1.0 M) | Cu(s), for which E(cell, standard) = +1.10 V. Using 0.0592 V for the Nernst constant at 25 C, what is the actual cell potential?","choices":["1.04 V","1.07 V","1.10 V","1.13 V","1.16 V"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The reaction Zn + Cu2+ -> Zn2+ + Cu transfers n = 2 electrons, and Q = [Zn2+]/[Cu2+] = 0.10/1.0 = 0.10. Nernst: E = E(standard) - (0.0592/n)logQ = 1.10 - (0.0296)(-1) = 1.10 + 0.030 = 1.13 V (key). A low product-ion concentration relative to the reactant ion pushes the reaction forward, raising the potential. 1.07 V inverts Q (uses [Cu2+]/[Zn2+], log = +1). 1.16 V forgets to divide by n. 1.04 V both forgets n and inverts Q. 1.10 V ignores the Nernst correction and just reports the standard value."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IUPAC nomenclature","stem":"Consider the alkane CH3-CH(CH3)-CH2-CH2-CH(CH2CH3)-CH2-CH3. Applying IUPAC rules (longest chain, lowest set of locants, alphabetical citation), what is its correct systematic name?","choices":["5-ethyl-2-methylheptane","3-ethyl-6-methylheptane","2-methyl-5-ethylheptane","5-ethyl-2-methyloctane","4-ethyl-2-methylheptane"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The longest chain is 7 carbons (heptane); an equal-length 7-carbon chain also runs through the ethyl group, but every 7-carbon chain carries exactly one methyl and one ethyl, so substituent count cannot break the tie. Number for the lowest locant set: methyl on C2 and ethyl on C5 gives {2,5}, which beats {3,6} obtained from the other end. Substituents are then cited alphabetically (ethyl before methyl), giving 5-ethyl-2-methylheptane. 3-ethyl-6-methylheptane numbers from the wrong end ({3,6}); 2-methyl-5-ethylheptane has correct locants but cites the substituents in numerical instead of alphabetical order; the octane answer miscounts the parent chain as 8 carbons; 4-ethyl-2-methylheptane misplaces the ethyl locant."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Stereochemistry (R/S, chirality)","stem":"The Fischer projection below shows L-cysteine (in a Fischer projection, horizontal bonds point toward the viewer and vertical bonds point away). What is the absolute configuration at the stereocenter? (Atomic numbers: H=1, C=6, N=7, O=8, S=16.)","choices":["S - priorities are assigned correctly, but the counterclockwise arrangement is read directly without reversing for the horizontal (toward-viewer) hydrogen.","S - the carboxyl group is ranked above CH2SH because it holds more oxygens, then the sense is reversed for the toward-viewer hydrogen.","R - CH2SH outranks COOH because sulfur outranks oxygen, and because the lowest-priority H points toward the viewer, the apparent counterclockwise sense is reversed.","S - the structure is drawn as an L-amino acid, and L-amino acids are S by convention.","S - ranking by attached atom with O taken above S places CH2SH lowest among the carbons, giving a clockwise sense reported directly as S."],"correct":2,"explanation":"The nitrogen of NH2 (Z=7) outranks both attached carbons, so NH2 is priority 1. The two carbons tie at the first atom (C), so compare their substituent sets: the carboxyl carbon is (O,O,O) while the CH2SH carbon is (S,H,H). Sulfur (16) outranks oxygen (8) at the first point of difference, so CH2SH is priority 2 and COOH is priority 3; H is priority 4. Tracing 1->2->3 (NH2 on the left, CH2SH at the bottom, COOH at the top) is counterclockwise, which naively reads S - but the lowest-priority H sits on a horizontal, toward-viewer bond, so the true sense is reversed to R. L-cysteine is the classic L-amino acid that is R rather than S. Reading the counterclockwise sense directly, or ranking COOH above CH2SH (treating O as beating S), both mis-land on S.","figureSvg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 220 220\" width=\"220\" height=\"220\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Fischer projection of L-cysteine\"><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"158\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"62\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"158\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-family=\"Georgia, serif\">COOH</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-family=\"Georgia, serif\">CH2SH</text><text x=\"54\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-family=\"Georgia, serif\">H2N</text><text x=\"166\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"currentColor\" font-family=\"Georgia, serif\">H</text></svg>"},{"section":"ochem","topic":"SN1/SN2/E1/E2","stem":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol, (CH3)3C-CH(OH)-CH3, is treated with concentrated HBr. What is the major organic product?","choices":["2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane","2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane","2,3-dimethyl-2-butene","3,3-dimethyl-1-butene","2,3-dimethyl-1-butene"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Concentrated HBr protonates the OH; loss of water gives a secondary carbocation, (CH3)3C-CH(+)-CH3. A methyl group migrates from the adjacent quaternary carbon to form the more stable tertiary carbocation, (CH3)2C(+)-CH(CH3)2 (a pinacolyl-type 1,2-methyl shift). Bromide, a strong nucleophile, then traps the tertiary cation to give 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane. 2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane is the tempting un-rearranged product (bromide capturing the secondary cation before the shift). The alkenes are elimination products: with a good nucleophile like bromide present, substitution dominates over E1, so they are not major."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alkene addition reactions","stem":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with BH3 in THF, followed by H2O2 and aqueous NaOH. What is the major product?","choices":["trans-2-methylcyclohexanol","cis-2-methylcyclohexanol","1-methylcyclohexanol","2-methylcyclohexanone","3-methylcyclohexanol"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Hydroboration-oxidation adds H and OH with anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry and syn stereochemistry. Boron (then OH) goes to the less-substituted alkene carbon (C2), and H goes to the methyl-bearing carbon (C1), with H and OH delivered to the same face. Because the added H and the OH are on the same face, the pre-existing C1 methyl ends up on the face opposite the OH, giving trans-2-methylcyclohexanol. The cis answer is the trap from mistaking which groups end up on the same face. 1-methylcyclohexanol is the Markovnikov product of oxymercuration or acid-catalyzed hydration; the ketone wrongly treats H2O2/NaOH as a ketone-forming oxidation; 3-methylcyclohexanol places the OH at the wrong carbon."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Aromaticity & EAS","stem":"Which of the following species is aromatic? (Assume each ring can adopt a planar, fully conjugated geometry unless its electron count or geometry forbids it.)","choices":["Cyclopentadienyl cation","1,3-Cyclobutadiene","The cycloheptatrienyl anion","Cyclopentadienyl anion","Neutral cyclooctatetraene"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Aromaticity requires a planar, cyclic, fully conjugated ring containing 4n+2 pi electrons. The cyclopentadienyl anion has 6 pi electrons (n=1), so it is aromatic. The cyclopentadienyl cation (4 pi) and cyclobutadiene (4 pi) are antiaromatic. The cycloheptatrienyl anion has 8 pi electrons and is antiaromatic - note that the aromatic C7 species is the tropylium CATION (6 pi), not the anion. Neutral cyclooctatetraene has 8 pi electrons and puckers into a nonplanar tub, making it nonaromatic (and even a planar form would be antiaromatic)."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Alcohols & carbonyls","stem":"Methyl acetate (CH3CO2CH3) is treated with 2 equivalents of ethylmagnesium bromide, then worked up with aqueous acid. What is the major product?","choices":["Butan-2-one","Pentan-3-ol","3-Methylpentan-3-ol","2-Methylbutan-2-ol","Butan-2-ol"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Grignard reagents add twice to esters. The first equivalent of ethyl adds to the carbonyl; the tetrahedral intermediate collapses and expels methoxide to give a ketone, butan-2-one (methyl ethyl ketone). The second equivalent of ethyl then adds to that ketone, producing a tertiary alkoxide that becomes 3-methylpentan-3-ol after aqueous workup (the central carbon bears OH, one methyl from the acetate, and two added ethyls). Stopping at butan-2-one is the classic trap (esters do not stop at the ketone with excess Grignard). Pentan-3-ol wrongly drops the acetate methyl, and the remaining choices miscount the number of ethyls added."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Acidity & pKa","stem":"Reasoning from the stability of the conjugate base, which of the following is the strongest acid?","choices":["Acetic acid","2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (picric acid)","Phenol","2,2,2-Trifluoroethanol","Ethanol"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Acid strength tracks conjugate-base stability. In picric acid, the three nitro groups (all ortho and para to the O) stabilize the resulting phenoxide by BOTH resonance (delocalizing the negative charge onto the nitro oxygens) and induction, driving its pKa (about 0.4) well below that of acetic acid (4.76) - a phenol that out-acidifies a carboxylic acid. Acetic acid is the intended trap (the reflex that carboxylic acids are always the strongest). Trifluoroethanol's three fluorines withdraw only inductively through sp3 carbon, with no resonance into the alkoxide, so it is far weaker; phenol and ethanol are weaker still."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Resonance & stability","stem":"Which of the following carbocations is the most stable?","choices":["The cyclopropenyl cation","The tert-butyl cation","The benzyl cation","The cyclopentadienyl cation","The allyl cation"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The cyclopropenyl cation has just 2 pi electrons in a planar, cyclic, conjugated ring (4n+2 with n=0), making it aromatic and exceptionally stabilized - so much so that its salts are actually isolable, unlike ordinary tertiary or benzylic cations. The tert-butyl cation is stabilized by hyperconjugation, and the benzyl and allyl cations by resonance, but none of these is aromatic. The cyclopentadienyl cation is the key trap: it looks resonance-rich (a five-membered ring with delocalization), but it has 4 pi electrons and is antiaromatic, so it is strongly destabilized rather than stabilized."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"IR & H-NMR spectroscopy","stem":"A compound with molecular formula C4H8O2 shows a strong IR absorption at 1740 cm-1 and no broad band in the 2500-3300 cm-1 region. Its 1H NMR spectrum shows a 3H singlet at 2.0 ppm, a 2H quartet at 4.1 ppm, and a 3H triplet at 1.3 ppm. What is the compound?","choices":["Methyl propanoate","Butanoic acid","1,4-Dioxane","Ethyl acetate","2-Methylpropanoic acid (isobutyric acid)"],"correct":3,"explanation":"One degree of unsaturation plus a strong 1740 cm-1 carbonyl and the ABSENCE of any broad 2500-3300 cm-1 band rules out a carboxylic acid and indicates an ester. The ethyl pattern (a 2H quartet with a 3H triplet) together with an isolated 3H acetyl singlet at 2.0 ppm, and with the OCH2 quartet deshielded all the way to 4.1 ppm, identifies ethyl acetate. Methyl propanoate is the sharpest trap (same formula), but its OCH3 would appear as a 3H singlet near 3.7 ppm and its CH2 quartet near 2.3 ppm, not at 4.1. Butanoic and isobutyric acid are excluded by the missing broad O-H stretch, and 1,4-dioxane has no carbonyl at all."},{"section":"ochem","topic":"Synthesis / roadmap","stem":"Which reagent sequence converts benzene into 3-bromoaniline (m-bromoaniline)?","choices":["(1) Br2/FeBr3; (2) HNO3/H2SO4; (3) Sn/HCl, then NaOH","(1) HNO3/H2SO4; (2) Br2/FeBr3; (3) Sn/HCl, then NaOH","(1) HNO3/H2SO4; (2) Sn/HCl, then NaOH; (3) Br2/FeBr3","(1) Br2/FeBr3; (2) Sn/HCl; (3) HNO3/H2SO4","(1) HNO3/H2SO4; (2) Br2/FeBr3; (3) a second HNO3/H2SO4"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A meta relationship requires a meta-directing group to be on the ring when the second substituent is installed. Nitrate benzene first (the nitro group is a meta director), then brominate to place Br meta to the nitro group, and only then reduce the nitro group to the amine with Sn/HCl (aryl C-Br survives). Brominating first is wrong because bromine is an ortho/para director, so nitration would give the para product; reducing to the amine before bromination is also wrong because the amino group is a strong ortho/para activator that directs bromine ortho/para and promotes polybromination. Repeating the nitration never generates the amine at all."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Fractions & decimals","stem":"Let x = 0.272727... (the two-digit block '27' repeating forever) and y = 0.16666... (only the digit '6' repeating). What is the exact value of x - y?","choices":["1/9","7/66","2/5","5/66","31/275"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Convert each repeating decimal: x = 27/99 = 3/11, and y = 0.1(6) = 1/6. With LCD 66, 3/11 = 18/66 and 1/6 = 11/66, so x - y = 18/66 - 11/66 = 7/66 (about 0.106). 2/5 comes from illegally subtracting straight across, (3-1)/(11-6) = 2/5. 31/275 comes from misreading y as the terminating 0.16 = 4/25, giving 3/11 - 4/25 = 75/275 - 44/275 = 31/275. 5/66 is a numerator slip (writing 1/6 as 13/66 instead of 11/66, so 18-13 = 5). 1/9 (about 0.111) is the nearest 'clean' fraction to the decimal answer and traps anyone who rounds instead of computing exactly."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Algebra & equations","stem":"A real number x satisfies x + 1/x = 3. What is the value of x^4 + 1/x^4?","choices":["49","81","47","51","79"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Square the given: (x + 1/x)^2 = x^2 + 2 + 1/x^2 = 9, so x^2 + 1/x^2 = 7. Square again: (x^2 + 1/x^2)^2 = x^4 + 2 + 1/x^4 = 49, so x^4 + 1/x^4 = 49 - 2 = 47 (independent of which real root you take). Choice 49 forgets the final '-2' (stops at squaring 7). Choice 51 subtracts the 2 with the wrong sign (49 + 2). Choice 81 just raises the sum to the fourth power, (x + 1/x)^4 = 3^4, ignoring the cross terms entirely. Choice 79 is 3^4 - 2, a hybrid of both mistakes."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Ratios & proportions","stem":"The quantity y varies directly with x and inversely with the square of z. When x = 6 and z = 2, y = 3. What is y when x = 15 and z = 5?","choices":["1.2","3","6","0.6","7.5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The model is y = kx/z^2. From y = 3 at x = 6, z = 2: 3 = k(6)/4, so k = 2. Then y = 2(15)/25 = 30/25 = 1.2. Choice 3 never squares z (uses y = kx/z, giving k = 1): x and z both scale by 2.5x, so they cancel and y stays 3. Choice 6 squares z only when finding k but not when applying it. Choice 0.6 does the reverse: forgets the square when finding k (k = 1) but squares when applying, giving 15/25. Choice 7.5 ignores z entirely (y proportional to x only), giving 3(15/6)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Percentages","stem":"A stock's price first rises 25%, then falls 20%, then falls 10%, in that order. What is the overall change in the price?","choices":["5% decrease","10% decrease","5% increase","10% increase","No net change"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Percent changes multiply, not add: final/initial = 1.25 x 0.80 x 0.90 = 0.90, a 10% decrease. Choice '5% decrease' comes from illegally adding the percents (+25 - 20 - 10 = -5). 'No net change' traps anyone who stops after the first two steps, since 1.25 x 0.80 = 1.00, and forgets the final 10% drop. '10% increase' and '5% increase' arise from sign confusion about which operations are gains versus losses."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Exponents & roots","stem":"Evaluate 8^(2/3) + 16^(-3/4) + (1/32)^(-2/5).","choices":["16","65/8","35/8","129/16","8"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Write each base as a power of 2. 8^(2/3) = 2^2 = 4. 16^(-3/4) = 2^(4 x -3/4) = 2^-3 = 1/8. (1/32)^(-2/5) = (2^-5)^(-2/5) = 2^2 = 4. Sum = 4 + 1/8 + 4 = 65/8. Choice 16 flips the sign on the middle term (2^+3 = 8), giving 4 + 8 + 4. Choice 35/8 mishandles the last term as 2^-2 = 1/4 instead of 4, giving 4 + 1/8 + 1/4. Choice 129/16 uses 2^-4 = 1/16 for the middle term (forgetting the exponent product 4 x 3/4 = 3), giving 4 + 1/16 + 4. Choice 8 simply drops the 1/8 term as if it were negligible."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Geometry (area/volume)","stem":"A sphere is inscribed in a cube whose edge length is 6. A second, smaller cube is then inscribed inside that sphere. What is the volume of the smaller cube?","choices":["8√3","24√3","27","54√2","216"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The inscribed sphere touches the outer cube's faces, so its diameter equals the cube's edge, 6. The smaller cube is inscribed in the sphere, so the sphere's diameter equals that cube's space diagonal: s√3 = 6, giving s = 6/√3 = 2√3. Volume = (2√3)^3 = 8 x 3√3 = 24√3. Choice 8√3 wrongly computes (2√3)^3 as 2^3 x √3, forgetting that (√3)^3 = 3√3. Choice 54√2 uses the face diagonal (s√2 = 6, s = 3√2) instead of the space diagonal, giving (3√2)^3 = 54√2. Choice 27 sets the small edge equal to the sphere's radius, 3. Choice 216 sets the small edge equal to 6, the sphere's diameter (same as the big cube)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Trigonometry","stem":"For an angle θ with 0° ≤ θ ≤ 180°, sin θ + cos θ = 1/2. What is the value of sin θ · cos θ?","choices":["-3/4","-3/8","3/8","1/4","1/8"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Square both sides: (sin θ + cos θ)^2 = sin^2θ + 2 sinθ cosθ + cos^2θ = 1/4. Since sin^2θ + cos^2θ = 1, this gives 1 + 2 sinθ cosθ = 1/4, so 2 sinθ cosθ = -3/4 and sinθ cosθ = -3/8. The negative product is consistent: a valid θ (about 114°) lies in [0°,180°] where sine is positive but cosine is negative. Choice -3/4 forgets to divide by 2. Choice 3/8 drops the negative sign. Choice 1/8 forgets the Pythagorean '1', treating (1/2)^2 as just 2 sinθ cosθ. Choice 1/4 stops at the squared value (1/2)^2 without isolating the product."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Probability","stem":"A committee of 3 people is chosen at random from a group of 5 men and 4 women. What is the probability that the committee includes at least one man and at least one woman?","choices":["1/6","37/42","5/6","20/21","5/7"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Total ways = C(9,3) = 84. Use the complement of the 'mixed' event: all-men = C(5,3) = 10, all-women = C(4,3) = 4. Favorable = 84 - 10 - 4 = 70, so probability = 70/84 = 5/6. Choice 1/6 is the complement itself, (10 + 4)/84 = 14/84, i.e., the probability of an all-same committee. Choice 37/42 = 74/84 subtracts only the all-men case. Choice 20/21 = 80/84 subtracts only the all-women case. Choice 5/7 = 60/84 reflects an over-subtraction (arithmetic slip such as 84 - 24)."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Statistics (mean/median/SD)","stem":"Five positive integers have a mean of 12, a median of 12, and a unique mode of 8. What is the greatest possible value of the largest of the five integers?","choices":["16","19","20","24","44"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Sort them a ≤ b ≤ c ≤ d ≤ e. Mean 12 means the sum is 60; median 12 means c = 12. For 8 to be the unique mode it must appear at least twice, and both copies must sit below the median, so a = b = 8. That leaves d + e = 60 - 8 - 8 - 12 = 32 with d ≥ 12. To maximize e, minimize d, but d cannot be 12 (that would make 12 appear twice, tying 8 and destroying the 'unique' mode), so the smallest allowed d is 13, giving e = 19 (set: 8, 8, 12, 13, 19). Choice 20 lets d = 12, illegally creating a second mode. Choice 24 puts a third 8 in the c-slot (8, 8, 8, 12, 24), which drops the median to 8. Choice 44 is just 60 - 8 - 8, ignoring the median and the fourth value. Choice 16 comes from finding any valid-looking set without maximizing."},{"section":"qr","topic":"Word problems (rate/work/mixture)","stem":"An empty tank is filled by pipe A (which alone fills it in 6 hours) and pipe B (which alone fills it in 4 hours), both open from the start. A drain C (which alone empties a full tank in 3 hours) is opened the instant the tank becomes half full and then stays open. What is the total time to fill the tank completely?","choices":["2.4 hours","6 hours","7.2 hours","8.4 hours","12 hours"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Stage 1 (A and B only): rate = 1/6 + 1/4 = 5/12 tank/hour. Time to fill the first half = (1/2) ÷ (5/12) = 6/5 = 1.2 hours. Stage 2 (A, B, and drain C): net rate = 5/12 - 1/3 = 5/12 - 4/12 = 1/12 tank/hour. Time for the remaining half = (1/2) ÷ (1/12) = 6 hours. Total = 1.2 + 6 = 7.2 hours. Choice 12 assumes C is open the entire time (net 1/12 for a full tank). Choice 2.4 ignores the drain completely (A and B fill the whole tank). Choice 6 counts only the second stage, forgetting the initial 1.2 hours. Choice 8.4 double-counts by adding the full-fill time 2.4 to the second-stage 6."}]}]};
